<node id="PID Controllers">
  <data key="d0">PID Controllers</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">PID Controllers are a type of control system discussed in the book, including basic and modified versions.&lt;SEP&gt;PID Controllers are widely used in industrial control systems and are known for their applicability to various types of control problems.&lt;SEP&gt;PID controllers are a type of control system with three parameters: proportional gain, integral gain, and derivative gain, widely used in industrial applications.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a6772221556c17af33ecd85904f8b357&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-69b1697f44d2725bfd2f580c242ea9b0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d25f3afc6ec15e59ee641a894432f365</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Control Systems">
  <data key="d0">Control Systems</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Control Systems are frameworks designed to manage, command, direct, or regulate the behavior of other devices or systems through the use of control loops and feedback mechanisms. They are characterized by their ability to regulate dynamic processes, often involving time-varying reference inputs, distinguishing them from regulator systems. Control Systems are a central topic in the field, with their analysis and design covered extensively in literature. They are commonly analyzed using various mathematical methods, including frequency-response, root-locus, and the Nyquist path and mapping theorem. These systems are often represented through block diagrams and involve complex numbers for design and analysis, typically depicted on the complex plane. The mathematical modeling of Control Systems is crucial for understanding and managing system dynamics, making it a key focus of study in the discipline.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9f818528633d493e4d171630e04e1d9b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-053e8edb648cfc91fffeac7f5faec45b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-98b7da91ec0504b2cb24aec37c61430a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d25f3afc6ec15e59ee641a894432f365&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9b8dd02e28c6fb3ac089290386b4c847&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-958b33ac0865635b15e9b137a06a79e4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-83bded94a3ff142ba41772e1b37f4f63&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a218a11cf9e9a1be2297492e35c21b7b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a6b4cd2d53cd90421b101e0434c0e9d1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a6772221556c17af33ecd85904f8b357&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d34b551b0c812125805bc0bfa51cbbdb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ac56253b9f22aee18032b4cf99f1d27d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8a2cf2ba37e76669ac29da1b6aa8f4af</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="State Space">
  <data key="d0">State Space</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">State Space is a mathematical framework and model used extensively in the analysis and modeling of dynamic systems, particularly in the field of control systems. This concept is pivotal for describing systems with multiple inputs and outputs by utilizing state variables. In essence, State Space represents the mathematical model of a physical system through a set of input, output, and state variables, which are interrelated by first-order differential equations. It provides a comprehensive way to describe the behavior of dynamic systems using vectors and matrices, allowing for a detailed representation of the system's dynamics. This framework is applicable to linear, continuous-time control systems and is characterized as an n-dimensional space where each axis corresponds to a state variable, with any state being representable as a point within this space. Chapters 9 and 10 in the relevant literature discuss the application of State Space in control system analysis, highlighting its role in capturing all possible states a system can achieve.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-70007355d6d14c2b8730b0480830d394&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1acc9332423db1bbcc93fc2b0ec7e34d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-232100dc2d77888cd2276dfa3e1c28a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9e97cb703344a8d83dd7f1085591043d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ed88cc0500a21b898e5a196a843b2781&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6d8c0bd0542b3fd879eac68953207dee&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a6772221556c17af33ecd85904f8b357&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-faf6d229bd33d9a677ddb2c4f3fe8552&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1431c462e05df5434bfc4728f13fa990&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c5e9c5e918d686131ecabed119aa3d7f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ba4c929673f3e997b800f45ab5b8f8b5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f1db318b25ae401b0bef5ad1a5d9a160&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-16d9cf4a88154292d8f7994b72a870f0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-008977814adc708a3eb952aa138c8696&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7fae5f4af39f45021c25745a260390d9</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Mechanical Systems">
  <data key="d0">Mechanical Systems</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Mechanical Systems are one of the types of systems whose mathematical models are derived in the book.&lt;SEP&gt;Mechanical systems are physical systems involving components like masses and springs, governed by principles such as Newton's laws.&lt;SEP&gt;Mechanical systems are types of dynamic systems that can be modeled using differential equations for control system analysis.&lt;SEP&gt;Mechanical systems refer to the various components and principles involved in the analysis and design of machines and structures.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-201ad2bff594c69ddcdebbbde0310588&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a6772221556c17af33ecd85904f8b357&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a99de50670eed69da300e8009547bd36&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d25f3afc6ec15e59ee641a894432f365</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Electrical Systems">
  <data key="d0">Electrical Systems</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Electrical Systems are another type of system whose mathematical models are derived in the book.&lt;SEP&gt;Electrical systems are dynamic systems often represented by differential equations in control systems.&lt;SEP&gt;Electrical systems consist of circuits and components like resistors and capacitors, analyzed using Kirchhoff's laws.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a6772221556c17af33ecd85904f8b357&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a99de50670eed69da300e8009547bd36&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d25f3afc6ec15e59ee641a894432f365</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Fluid Systems">
  <data key="d0">Fluid Systems</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Fluid Systems include liquid-level, pneumatic, and hydraulic systems, discussed in the book.&lt;SEP&gt;Fluid systems include liquid-level, pneumatic, and hydraulic systems, important in control system analysis.&lt;SEP&gt;Fluid systems involve the mathematical modeling and analysis of systems using fluids, often focusing on dynamics and control aspects.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a6772221556c17af33ecd85904f8b357&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d427a9d59bf3e814fd05720d1b33d0f8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d25f3afc6ec15e59ee641a894432f365</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Thermal Systems">
  <data key="d0">Thermal Systems</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Thermal Systems refer to systems that involve heat transfer and thermal dynamics. These systems are analyzed through the concepts of thermal resistance and capacitance, which are crucial for mathematical modeling. They can be modeled using these parameters to understand the heat transfer between substances. Additionally, Thermal Systems are discussed in the context of mathematical modeling in books, emphasizing their importance in understanding complex thermal dynamics. These systems also involve temperature control and are analyzed within control systems frameworks. Furthermore, Thermal Systems are often subject to transport lag, which affects their phase response, highlighting the need for precise modeling and analysis to manage these effects effectively.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-131d68e1d8fb1dac5267fbb38c0b52ef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-30f7b7fbdbec5ce9d814319c46f46972&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d25f3afc6ec15e59ee641a894432f365&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eae6e7cf7c375d236da8e4fd6f746d02&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-76155cfacd298ba99866d06587bc0e7d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a6772221556c17af33ecd85904f8b357&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-da4cffe0215472e30c589d1be7e6d819</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Routh's Stability Criterion">
  <data key="d0">Routh's Stability Criterion</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Routh's Stability Criterion is a mathematical approach used to determine the stability of control systems and linear systems by analyzing the coefficients of their characteristic equations. This method involves examining the sign changes and positivity of the coefficients in a polynomial equation to assess the system's stability. It is a widely recognized procedure for determining the absolute stability of a system, and it is often presented in literature as a fundamental technique for stability analysis.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-390f7affb8c4405e8dbb8cd9df26af73&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d25f3afc6ec15e59ee641a894432f365&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eee931e9f23a29ab6271ed415dea0168&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7d4f87e9dd3cfeb7f1869f4f209585ee&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a6772221556c17af33ecd85904f8b357&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8ad1f447db822714c4aaac1a32550e21&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c2f0b2bf0954cb3d23352a1767704ce&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0eb8913e939d945dc7d0ee9f203204fb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bfaabf07740c618e0c89f07d1cf48dea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-923b909910f72564cfae409c3c852e81</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Hurwitz Stability Criterion">
  <data key="d0">Hurwitz Stability Criterion</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Hurwitz Stability Criterion involves the use of determinants to assess the asymptotic stability of a system by ensuring positive conditions on the diagonal elements.&lt;SEP&gt;Hurwitz Stability Criterion is a mathematical approach used to determine the stability of a system by analyzing the signs of determinants derived from polynomial coefficients.&lt;SEP&gt;Hurwitz Stability Criterion is another method presented in the book for stability analysis of control systems.&lt;SEP&gt;Hurwitz stability criterion is another method for evaluating control system stability.&lt;SEP&gt;The Hurwitz stability criterion is a mathematical condition used to determine the stability of a system by ensuring that all the roots of a characteristic polynomial have negative real parts.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d25f3afc6ec15e59ee641a894432f365&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7d4f87e9dd3cfeb7f1869f4f209585ee&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a6772221556c17af33ecd85904f8b357&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-85b819ddf3cb3d7c56ca7d6f41195981&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-59252f7260535877b27b152342d1fa5d</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Root-Locus Analysis">
  <data key="d0">Root-Locus Analysis</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Root-Locus Analysis is a method used in control systems to determine the stability of a system by analyzing the locations of poles and zeros in the complex plane.&lt;SEP&gt;Root-Locus Analysis is a method used to reshape the transient-response characteristics of closed-loop control systems, providing direct information on transient response.&lt;SEP&gt;Root-Locus Analysis is a technique discussed in the book for designing control systems.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a6772221556c17af33ecd85904f8b357&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-053e8edb648cfc91fffeac7f5faec45b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-50c4cfa192898028842df51ccba67794</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Frequency-Response Analysis">
  <data key="d0">Frequency-Response Analysis</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Frequency-Response Analysis is a technique discussed in the book for designing control systems.&lt;SEP&gt;Frequency-response analysis studies how a system responds to inputs at different frequencies.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a6772221556c17af33ecd85904f8b357&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d34b551b0c812125805bc0bfa51cbbdb</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Nyquist Stability Criterion">
  <data key="d0">Nyquist Stability Criterion</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The Nyquist Stability Criterion is a graphical method used in control system engineering to determine the stability of a closed-loop system. It is based on analyzing the open-loop frequency-response curves and the Nyquist plot. This method assesses the stability by examining the encirclements of the critical point, specifically the -1+j0 point, in the complex plane. By evaluating the locus of the inverse transfer function or the frequency response, such as G(jω)H(jω), the criterion provides insights into the absolute stability of the system. The Nyquist Stability Criterion is a fundamental tool in the frequency-response design of control systems, enabling engineers to determine the stability of linear closed-loop systems through the analysis of open-loop frequency-response characteristics.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-094d0f53918a4b57661980c588c5dd88&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5518828c63927a29cbd23a5354674537&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-053e8edb648cfc91fffeac7f5faec45b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a3d1b21ce83b859c71c888d5c7aae6ba&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9f0dfc9266a7cbf0daf9292867c654cf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9afe985fc7dab84bc86b6f660723b27e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a5b3fa9ac1d3ce61d8dbdd21acb60d10&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a55275992e9d9009ddd4f2b26d4260c1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-021d406fb1875a04e6ca85d6a4589897&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-431795c802c4337e55d260c23ac796ea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a6772221556c17af33ecd85904f8b357&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-136efeec2362cbbe6ae14a66f80a408f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f59f8623e4ff8e64d49175daeeadad05&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1fc8172454011b7e111636e450b0431d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a480968a716a749c7646332b8d7dec12&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2d08ffa0936970988485d30a9ea44afb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-97ff6cf0253e8f192c45acefe4824e0b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1022a5cf276c07ee56ffc50c13799b6d</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Bode Diagram">
  <data key="d0">Bode Diagram</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The Bode Diagram is a graphical representation used to analyze and illustrate the frequency response of a system, particularly in control systems and signal processing. It consists of two plots: one showing the magnitude (or gain) and the other depicting the phase angle, both as functions of frequency. This representation is crucial for assessing the stability and performance of a system. The Bode Diagram is widely used in engineering to design compensators and analyze system dynamics, including the gain and phase shift characteristics of low-pass filters. It provides a clear visualization of how a system responds to different frequencies, making it an essential tool in frequency-response analysis and control system engineering.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-6a256259f60bf17c11f960427ffa11ff&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4bf77eaf82ba369d832435b0e6f076eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a3d1b21ce83b859c71c888d5c7aae6ba&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9f0dfc9266a7cbf0daf9292867c654cf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9b87b5f3d20e81642454297b93cc16f3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a8c47160042a9398aa709e25eded3b0c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-95a8d8a1b3cfb0710c46f13ae2e3b48e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-181fee5061e5d9adf0f04d196cc15e7f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fd21bbb7bb0f22b9146dce0bacde7f33&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-696e4f2dc4a3654f0413002d870333af&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1ef9a9b21f3821e990c650615c128744&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2afbc3d7bb9c389990910a284d3b9dfd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6d46c03f5b1a1f1ab07036c79e47616c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-50c4cfa192898028842df51ccba67794&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1022a5cf276c07ee56ffc50c13799b6d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5518828c63927a29cbd23a5354674537&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4e42383167231f90ca804ba822f6b73d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-29f2803ebeab2bb881d9725116174a56&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2f074d90ecc0d7974d2a0b0c59285149&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-84a06449dbf5e39bcd3e56867b9d2ab2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c0b45e8c05ce6d6a2e33cb4a65bc30d4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2fac437c10d4b085f8ef3459b8dfdb4f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4a53699f6a5efb94ddd426141d45cba8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a2ddda49062cacf815e40ae9bc8167c7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-926952abfdc3443e6470e2a990053a6c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b467c546fd593046d07eac1e9826b33c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-562db1bae3e6a30605a16f1c339c6e3d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cba4dc46aaf3979ba1619c43d08d1720&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7bfba099219ef0865c8cfbb638e366f1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4e3fcd1b5ae55946bb03cb8fa5a6859d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e0c2c5d2084cf4dc42eac20165445910&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ccb516dd7fa038fc65484f2cc9669722&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fbfe186ba42a9341b463befd113d8ee4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8c39c97551687b0bf865de0bebf31277&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-131d68e1d8fb1dac5267fbb38c0b52ef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f22851addd0d0d43a22e89bf8e277faf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-016ebe44f8f410b4e420081ccf7a65f9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-289220cc5e6fcc41f8da8923ec0e0105&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8baf5807e5435582388b6ba8ea165e4d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1c56a057e86a61de468030b87f550fa4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-189305ffdfb5a3f7a9695e56559bcef3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8221452e166faf8569286a459dc76b3e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-60969709c0f545814b039c0d77d040ef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-855fbfd6cc8ae599aa41c969d6418c33&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0e2d5c8521bc1f35d5a5daaf0b7fe7eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-05bc9bb179b1e6a64609fa853f1c7706&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d8efef9175eeea628e4106a451e3ce5d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-36751d4e3f942193eb2e541b3e4dd23b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-97ff6cf0253e8f192c45acefe4824e0b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7617e3bbfe2c63a69317713315650eb3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b2a20c61ffd29ee123aa4008d1f4ba5f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3a5c8adb826b6a09285fe1ad5b9d9b8c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f4c46665e037287407ddbff2463962aa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ae064a86d4a638278e16558779a2cbf9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ab1222a4f68b9900993c6bdde861432b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-92e23c313eaf5a6d348bce8a1588be0f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cfe03dc7efd5be8b8b6f705865273b86&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-60ef1c3f8eed32705fec8c85d559930a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-618424e4625f7d33fa3ab142d292d0b7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-471b11b0e8da61744401ac854b4948f8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5ce576a87f38c3c3621dc3b3d8f16913&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6b139b34ebbeaf26c5893fedc131b125&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a6772221556c17af33ecd85904f8b357&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bca6ad29eb261282c2df664425ef7d0b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4f6dad58f78a125a7c454b0f2177f13a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-731aadaa135cc2ef1c9482aab829f4e1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6d35fea10973c2b24ef34a97da4f0e8b</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Pole Placement">
  <data key="d0">Pole Placement</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Pole Placement is a control system design technique used to position the closed-loop poles of a system in desired locations to achieve specific dynamic characteristics, stability, and performance. It is a concept within control theory that involves determining the placement of poles in a system's transfer function. This technique is often discussed in the context of state space and is crucial for designing controllers that ensure the system meets the desired performance criteria.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-292c92c504fb43e76a57c3f50454fcbe&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0ae81e09d61ed423567970de31eac0a6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cd5a5410abc287fd3f4c9718236dc051&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a6772221556c17af33ecd85904f8b357&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-31df32fb8d3f0302ba80571341b33840&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-39704bca2c3f08c736c74b4257605c0a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1779b26d10c1b1abb7d91ca4e5524e77&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0c5b8198217ef0317fadb7c0c3009acb</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="State Observers">
  <data key="d0">State Observers</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">State Observers are a concept in control system design discussed in state space.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a6772221556c17af33ecd85904f8b357</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Quadratic Optimal Control">
  <data key="d0">Quadratic Optimal Control</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Quadratic Optimal Control is a technique for designing regulator systems discussed in the book.&lt;SEP&gt;Quadratic optimal control is a method that balances system response with control energy requirements, often used in control system design.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a6772221556c17af33ecd85904f8b357&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9838df333da6616701c1cce66c00e732</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Robust Control Systems">
  <data key="d0">Robust Control Systems</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Robust Control Systems are designed to maintain performance despite uncertainties and variations in system parameters.&lt;SEP&gt;Robust Control Systems are designed to maintain stability and performance despite uncertainties or errors between the model and the actual plant.&lt;SEP&gt;Robust Control Systems are discussed in the book as part of control system design in state space.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a6772221556c17af33ecd85904f8b357&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-16d9cf4a88154292d8f7994b72a870f0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1acc9332423db1bbcc93fc2b0ec7e34d</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Ziegler–Nichols Rules">
  <data key="d0">Ziegler–Nichols Rules</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Ziegler–Nichols Rules are guidelines for tuning PID controllers discussed in the book.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a6772221556c17af33ecd85904f8b357</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Computational Optimization Approach">
  <data key="d0">Computational Optimization Approach</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Computational Optimization Approach is a method for designing PID controllers using computational techniques.&lt;SEP&gt;The Computational Optimization Approach is a method used to design control systems by finding optimal parameter values to meet specific transient response specifications.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4bc56ac7e2091f578e7c8a1de9726168&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a6772221556c17af33ecd85904f8b357</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Two-Degrees-of-Freedom Control">
  <data key="d0">Two-Degrees-of-Freedom Control</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Two-Degrees-of-Freedom Control is a modification approach for PID controllers discussed in the book.&lt;SEP&gt;Two-degrees-of-freedom control is a general control scheme that involves separate controllers in both feedforward and feedback paths to enhance system flexibility.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a6772221556c17af33ecd85904f8b357&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a4349e4239d3b82325e7a8cb18d1189c</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Zero-Placement Approach">
  <data key="d0">Zero-Placement Approach</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">A method used in control systems to design a transfer function by strategically placing zeros to meet system requirements.&lt;SEP&gt;A method used in control systems to eliminate steady-state errors in response to ramp and acceleration inputs.&lt;SEP&gt;Zero-Placement Approach is a technique to improve response characteristics in control systems.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a6772221556c17af33ecd85904f8b357&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-692c80987f518b576a9461026d5a6aad&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-273f66b6e44351603fc5160de59ad839</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Servo Systems">
  <data key="d0">Servo Systems</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Servo Systems are a type of control system designed for precise position control, discussed in the book.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a6772221556c17af33ecd85904f8b357</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Regulator Systems">
  <data key="d0">Regulator Systems</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Regulator Systems are a type of control system where the reference input is constant, including zero.&lt;SEP&gt;Regulator Systems are designed to maintain a desired output despite disturbances.&lt;SEP&gt;Regulator systems are control systems designed to maintain a desired output level despite disturbances.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a6772221556c17af33ecd85904f8b357&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9f818528633d493e4d171630e04e1d9b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dfd846455e2fefcbadf8b8c8b3d71195</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
