<node id="Introduction to Control Systems">
  <data key="d0">Introduction to Control Systems</data>
  <data key="d1">event</data>
  <data key="d2">Introduction to Control Systems is the title of the book authored by Katsuhiko Ogata, focusing on control theories.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-daaf945da92ace5d9157652556798d00</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="James Watt">
  <data key="d0">James Watt</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">James Watt developed the centrifugal governor for speed control of steam engines in the eighteenth century.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-daaf945da92ace5d9157652556798d00</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Minorsky">
  <data key="d0">Minorsky</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Minorsky worked on automatic controllers for steering ships and contributed to early control theory.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-daaf945da92ace5d9157652556798d00</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Hazen">
  <data key="d0">Hazen</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Hazen introduced the term servomechanisms and discussed relay servomechanisms for position control systems.&lt;SEP&gt;Hazen is referenced in relation to engineering and control systems.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d427a9d59bf3e814fd05720d1b33d0f8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-daaf945da92ace5d9157652556798d00</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Nyquist">
  <data key="d0">Nyquist</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Nyquist developed a procedure for determining the stability of closed-loop systems based on open-loop response.&lt;SEP&gt;Nyquist was a key figure in the development of frequency-response methods in the 1930s and 1940s, contributing significantly to control theory.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-053e8edb648cfc91fffeac7f5faec45b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-daaf945da92ace5d9157652556798d00</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Bode">
  <data key="d0">Bode</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Bode contributed to frequency-response methods, enabling the design of linear closed-loop control systems.&lt;SEP&gt;Bode was a prominent contributor to the development of frequency-response methods in control theory during the 1930s and 1940s.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-053e8edb648cfc91fffeac7f5faec45b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-daaf945da92ace5d9157652556798d00</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Ziegler and Nichols">
  <data key="d0">Ziegler and Nichols</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Ziegler and Nichols are individuals who developed widely-used methods for tuning PID controllers, providing systematic approaches for setting control parameters.&lt;SEP&gt;Ziegler and Nichols proposed tuning rules for PID controllers in 1942, which are still influential in control systems tuning.&lt;SEP&gt;Ziegler and Nichols suggested tuning rules for PID controllers, known as Ziegler-Nichols tuning rules.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-6f67e9b469ce0543584be32ab4c6f89c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-daaf945da92ace5d9157652556798d00&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d25f3afc6ec15e59ee641a894432f365</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Evans">
  <data key="d0">Evans</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Evans developed the root-locus method, a core component of classical control theory.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-daaf945da92ace5d9157652556798d00</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Classical Control Theory">
  <data key="d0">Classical Control Theory</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Classical Control Theory involves traditional methods of analyzing and designing control systems.&lt;SEP&gt;Classical control theory, also known as conventional control theory, is a method used in the analysis and design of control systems.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-faf6d229bd33d9a677ddb2c4f3fe8552&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-daaf945da92ace5d9157652556798d00</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Modern Control Theory">
  <data key="d0">Modern Control Theory</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Modern Control Theory is a field of study that deals with the behavior of dynamical systems with inputs and how to influence the system's behavior.&lt;SEP&gt;Modern Control Theory is an approach to the analysis and design of complex control systems, applicable to multiple-input, multiple-output systems, and both linear and nonlinear, time invariant or time varying systems.&lt;SEP&gt;Modern Control Theory refers to advanced methodologies in control systems, contrasted with conventional control theory.&lt;SEP&gt;Modern control theory is based on time-domain analysis and synthesis using state variables for complex systems.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-baf80d031591815111410b79dbb30df9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ed88cc0500a21b898e5a196a843b2781&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-daaf945da92ace5d9157652556798d00&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e8ea5a2f30b2dcfbbb586c6854279bee</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Robust Control Theory">
  <data key="d0">Robust Control Theory</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Robust Control Theory is a design method for control systems that addresses uncertainties in system modeling. It ensures stability and performance in control systems design by accounting for possible errors between the model and the actual system. This theory deals with the stabilization of control systems under uncertainty, employing tools such as the small gain theorem and the \(H_{\infty}\) norm. It is particularly applied when a mathematical model involves considerable errors, thereby guaranteeing system performance despite these uncertainties. Robust Control Theory is featured in literature that focuses on maintaining systems' stability under model errors.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-daaf945da92ace5d9157652556798d00&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-338b0c8db14460db8abd15b27fa186d5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d34b551b0c812125805bc0bfa51cbbdb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e7d38d679f58a32db379c91feb378361&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3480cf8d746209a8fad2e41342750188&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-16d9cf4a88154292d8f7994b72a870f0</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Automatic Control">
  <data key="d0">Automatic Control</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Automatic control is essential in engineering and science, involved in systems like space vehicles, robotics, and industrial operations.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-daaf945da92ace5d9157652556798d00</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Controlled Variable">
  <data key="d0">Controlled Variable</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Controlled Variable is the specific output of a system that is regulated or controlled.&lt;SEP&gt;The Controlled Variable is the quantity or condition that is measured and controlled within a system.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-3480cf8d746209a8fad2e41342750188&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-faf6d229bd33d9a677ddb2c4f3fe8552</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Control Signal or Manipulated Variable">
  <data key="d0">Control Signal or Manipulated Variable</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The Control Signal or Manipulated Variable is varied by the controller to affect the value of the controlled variable.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-3480cf8d746209a8fad2e41342750188</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Plant">
  <data key="d0">Plant</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">In control systems, the term "Plant" refers to the physical system or process being controlled. It is a crucial component within the control loop, responsible for receiving input signals from the controller and producing output signals that are sent back to the controller or other system components. The Plant is typically represented by a transfer function or mathematical equations that describe its dynamics and response to input signals. Examples of such systems include mechanical devices, heating furnaces, chemical reactors, or spacecraft. 

The Plant is often modeled as a linear time-invariant system, characterized by a transfer function, such as G(s) or Gp(s), which dictates its behavior. In some cases, it may be represented as a first-order system with a time constant Ts, denoted by the block '1/(Ts+1)'. The Plant can be part of various control concepts, such as observer-based control or PID control, where it is described by specific equations or transfer functions that define its interaction with other system components.

In industrial settings, the Plant receives inputs from actuators to achieve desired outputs, and it may require compensators for performance adjustments due to its fixed and non-modifiable nature. It plays a vital role in the control system, processing input signals according to its dynamics and contributing to the overall system's functionality and efficiency.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4bc56ac7e2091f578e7c8a1de9726168&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b1abf1bd63b798f8e59025a047459778&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-219f7fb5973694e4ed3fc49b9c5d4c60&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2602a3aebfee4edc08475d0264ce7134&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a8712fa7ddd9dc034d222eaf660d62f1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-87f3dae9453dc00aeaa24b0a25b4f9e5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7291b615cce3723bc1941e91175ccf27&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2bcf605f83825a20ced09f3cfbb124a8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3480cf8d746209a8fad2e41342750188&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5743b50920c06e8fe8331392a198cbcc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-32e837730a9d05b1df592f0ae66e9fc2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-620d81e2a5a2e80e3b426e6ed3a7d42b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a7067d3378c6320f2be99583ea919091&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6a6c1f27816ba00765df34580fd4352b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-31df32fb8d3f0302ba80571341b33840&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a12a1fcc8b96ef105600adda81cf5b8f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ed23283c207553099a8ea220b94e1005&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c2db63f41e22678b776f42091570392a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b31537ba5725a3819547235d3a68c08e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-88ad04f5c92febfb736b3e73f1b155d2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0279e47752e3eff4e78f68ce4e1c721f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f599210548e4a595268cbd4a1d4b5e3c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-28ae81c40745842e3e00aecec87aa63f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dfd846455e2fefcbadf8b8c8b3d71195&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-caeb6d047b3cd8348a555d68841dee56&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-12d9f86a0ab5347bed74adfcf7735e09&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6f67e9b469ce0543584be32ab4c6f89c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2350dc025ef445f345d067152812c1c9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-115c47567f4cb2d5b0df6b61937e6281&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9cff8b4398a7527756d649d91c3b5802&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9a954f327a9c49380980f7f1e64531f7</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Process">
  <data key="d0">Process</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">A Process is an operation to be controlled, which can be natural or artificial and consists of a series of controlled actions or movements.&lt;SEP&gt;The process is a part of the control system that transforms the controller's output into a new signal.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-3480cf8d746209a8fad2e41342750188&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3ab453e611d8eb756961ea34b36c8dd8</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="System">
  <data key="d0">System</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The System is a combination of components that act together to perform a certain objective, which can be either physical or abstract. It is a complex arrangement of elements, such as circuits or components, that interact to perform a specific function. The System is often analyzed through transfer functions and is described by a mathematical model involving differential equations, specifically focusing on state variables x₁ and x₂. This dynamic setup includes state variables and observability, characterized by matrices and transfer functions. It is defined by a set of equations involving matrices A, B, and C, and is completely observable. The System is analyzed within the context of control theory for state controllability, eigenvalue placement, and output controllability. The mathematical model is used to analyze the response of the System to inputs, providing insights into its performance and functionality.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-8288442d2392d765b02cbc791d1b5378&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f55b04c74c20ff1394ec57c53c60a408&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-be8a262c59d580a999bcba6b1211d485&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cf7ceea51cabf1fcb837b2df7b1f884d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1344a7537de0ac1c48323314da3e0fa4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a5b38ba15c11c9500daafd6a0fa68942&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e87750a7956fffd621ae63f70162b51a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3480cf8d746209a8fad2e41342750188&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5ae5ca3b7a89038d8b6dbc78ab1fcc29&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-30afd1ea0d3bf0f6ff1f6ab5550759e8</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Disturbance">
  <data key="d0">Disturbance</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">A Disturbance is a signal that adversely affects the output of a system, either internal or external.&lt;SEP&gt;Disturbance refers to any external factor that disrupts the normal operation of a system.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-3480cf8d746209a8fad2e41342750188&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-faf6d229bd33d9a677ddb2c4f3fe8552</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Feedback Control">
  <data key="d0">Feedback Control</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Feedback Control is an operation that reduces the difference between the output of a system and some reference input in the presence of disturbances.&lt;SEP&gt;Feedback control is a process in control systems where the output is fed back into the input to reduce errors and improve system performance.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-3480cf8d746209a8fad2e41342750188&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-241ff02c460b748c0a8d1135d536c107</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Speed Control System">
  <data key="d0">Speed Control System</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">A system designed to manage and regulate the speed of a motor or mechanism, often depicted using block diagrams.&lt;SEP&gt;The Speed Control System is a mechanism that adjusts fuel supply to an engine based on the difference between desired and actual speeds.&lt;SEP&gt;The speed control system is a mechanical setup involving components like springs, pistons, and cylinders, designed to regulate engine speed.&lt;SEP&gt;The speed control system is a mechanism described in the document, utilizing proportional-plus-integral control action for managing speed.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-45a3caf311e2ab85a8b8795fbe5c9365&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3480cf8d746209a8fad2e41342750188&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a49db1d02aaff7365470c4256ccfb82f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-62a1cbf10c33f82d65ca8e2a6d86b67b</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Temperature Control System">
  <data key="d0">Temperature Control System</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The Temperature Control System measures and adjusts the temperature of an electric furnace to reach a desired value using a controller.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-3480cf8d746209a8fad2e41342750188</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Fuel System">
  <data key="d0">Fuel System</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The Fuel System is a critical component of an engine, involving the flow of fuel through various components to reach the engine.&lt;SEP&gt;The Fuel System is a critical component of an internal combustion engine, involving the flow of fuel through various components such as the fuel tank, filter, pump, and injector nozzle.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-3480cf8d746209a8fad2e41342750188&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9c56ce168f150f0001a3bcd278699053</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Fuel Tank">
  <data key="d0">Fuel Tank</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The Fuel Tank is a component of the fuel system that stores fuel for the engine.&lt;SEP&gt;The Fuel Tank stores fuel for the engine and is located at the bottom left corner of the diagram.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-3480cf8d746209a8fad2e41342750188&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9c56ce168f150f0001a3bcd278699053</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Fuel Filter">
  <data key="d0">Fuel Filter</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The Fuel Filter is a component that removes impurities from fuel before it enters the rest of the system.&lt;SEP&gt;The Fuel Filter removes impurities from the fuel before it enters the rest of the system.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-3480cf8d746209a8fad2e41342750188&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9c56ce168f150f0001a3bcd278699053</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Fuel Pump">
  <data key="d0">Fuel Pump</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The Fuel Pump is a component that moves fuel from the tank to the engine.&lt;SEP&gt;The Fuel Pump is a mechanical device that draws fuel from the tank and pumps it through the system.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-3480cf8d746209a8fad2e41342750188&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9c56ce168f150f0001a3bcd278699053</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Electric Furnace System">
  <data key="d0">Electric Furnace System</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The Electric Furnace System comprises various components like the A/D converter, interface, amplifier, controller, and relay, illustrating their interconnections.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9c56ce168f150f0001a3bcd278699053</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Business System">
  <data key="d0">Business System</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Business System refers to the framework within which businesses operate, involving various processes and structures.&lt;SEP&gt;The Business System is a closed-loop system consisting of dynamic groups, requiring feedback methods and optimization techniques to improve performance.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-faf6d229bd33d9a677ddb2c4f3fe8552&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9c56ce168f150f0001a3bcd278699053</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Engineering Organizational System">
  <data key="d0">Engineering Organizational System</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">An engineering organizational system comprised of major groups such as management, research and development, preliminary design, experiments, product design and drafting, fabrication and assembling, and testing, all interconnected to complete the operation.&lt;SEP&gt;Engineering Organizational System is a structured framework within engineering disciplines, focusing on the organization and management of engineering tasks and processes.&lt;SEP&gt;The Engineering Organizational System is composed of major groups such as management, R&amp;D, design, fabrication, and testing, interconnected for operation.&lt;SEP&gt;The Engineering Organizational System is likely a framework or methodology for structuring engineering processes and systems.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-faf6d229bd33d9a677ddb2c4f3fe8552&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0f0248523559d3c95f73c89fa69d7df2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d427a9d59bf3e814fd05720d1b33d0f8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9c56ce168f150f0001a3bcd278699053</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Fuel Injector Nozzle">
  <data key="d0">Fuel Injector Nozzle</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The Fuel Injector Nozzle injects fuel into the engine's combustion chamber at high pressure.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9c56ce168f150f0001a3bcd278699053</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Air Intake Manifold">
  <data key="d0">Air Intake Manifold</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The Air Intake Manifold draws in air from outside the vehicle and directs it into the engine's cylinders.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9c56ce168f150f0001a3bcd278699053</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Engine Block">
  <data key="d0">Engine Block</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The Engine Block houses the combustion process, consisting of cylinders, pistons, and other components.&lt;SEP&gt;Typically located at the bottom or rear of an internal combustion engine, not visible in the image.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-45a3caf311e2ab85a8b8795fbe5c9365&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9c56ce168f150f0001a3bcd278699053</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="A/D Converter">
  <data key="d0">A/D Converter</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The A/D Converter connects to the electric furnace, converting analog signals to digital.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9c56ce168f150f0001a3bcd278699053</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Interface">
  <data key="d0">Interface</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The Interface connects various components of the electric furnace system, facilitating communication.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9c56ce168f150f0001a3bcd278699053</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Amplifier">
  <data key="d0">Amplifier</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Amplifier is a component that increases the magnitude of actuating signals from the automatic controller without altering signal characteristics.&lt;SEP&gt;An amplifier drives the motor by amplifying signals in a control system.&lt;SEP&gt;An amplifier in control systems enhances the control signal before it affects the plant, ensuring the signal is at a sufficient power level for operation.&lt;SEP&gt;An amplifier in the servo system that amplifies error voltage and applies it to the motor's armature circuit.&lt;SEP&gt;The Amplifier boosts signals within the electric furnace system.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-421dc885fedffcbdd1023734df76f5f1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d8575f9998ffcb80f46b6bfa2a8177e8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8537419dec5c5aa7a095925b682e6a09&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7291b615cce3723bc1941e91175ccf27&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9c56ce168f150f0001a3bcd278699053</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Controller">
  <data key="d0">Controller</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The Controller is a critical component of a control system, responsible for managing and regulating the behavior and response of various systems, such as space vehicles, air compressors, electric furnaces, and more. It processes input signals, often from sensors, and feedback from observers and plants to generate control signals that adjust the system's inputs accordingly. The Controller utilizes proportional, integral, and derivative gains to stabilize and enhance system performance, often represented by transfer functions like Gc(s) or denoted by '-K'. It plays a pivotal role in achieving desired outcomes by comparing measured values, such as temperature, to setpoints and adjusting outputs, such as heater outputs, to maintain optimal conditions. Additionally, the Controller can introduce specific dynamics, such as a zero at -1 and a pole at infinity, to influence the system's behavior effectively.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-36e714a094e63aef48fac634eeddad6c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f1897ad76eeb7398a9874d0d8978aa7c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b1abf1bd63b798f8e59025a047459778&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-026e0fcd969517dd30ca84cdf6678f77&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-219f7fb5973694e4ed3fc49b9c5d4c60&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9e6b7d8c1849925b0d0f1ad9793983e9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2602a3aebfee4edc08475d0264ce7134&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-32e837730a9d05b1df592f0ae66e9fc2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a7067d3378c6320f2be99583ea919091&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3ab453e611d8eb756961ea34b36c8dd8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3054b048b0a72e379da574dd802acf5c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-681185e57d810ae79c2ee826ac8ba784&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0279e47752e3eff4e78f68ce4e1c721f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-421dc885fedffcbdd1023734df76f5f1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-faf6d229bd33d9a677ddb2c4f3fe8552&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-caeb6d047b3cd8348a555d68841dee56&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-12d9f86a0ab5347bed74adfcf7735e09&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2350dc025ef445f345d067152812c1c9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9c56ce168f150f0001a3bcd278699053</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Relay">
  <data key="d0">Relay</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The Relay acts as a switch within the electric furnace system, controlling electrical flow.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9c56ce168f150f0001a3bcd278699053</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Robust Control System">
  <data key="d0">Robust Control System</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">A control system design approach that incorporates uncertainty or error between the actual plant and its mathematical model to ensure satisfactory performance.&lt;SEP&gt;A control system designed to ensure robust stability and performance by satisfying inequalities that involve transfer functions like G(s), K(s), and W(s).</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0f0248523559d3c95f73c89fa69d7df2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-21f396695d06e061d0a62e0e0e209ea4</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Research and Development">
  <data key="d0">Research and Development</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">A critical phase in product development involving data gathering and prototype creation to support the product's concept and functionality.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0f0248523559d3c95f73c89fa69d7df2</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Product Design and Testing">
  <data key="d0">Product Design and Testing</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Stages of product development that involve creating detailed designs, fabricating components, and conducting tests to ensure performance and safety standards.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0f0248523559d3c95f73c89fa69d7df2</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Management">
  <data key="d0">Management</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">A group responsible for planning, budgeting, and resource allocation within the engineering organizational system.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0f0248523559d3c95f73c89fa69d7df2</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Preliminary Design">
  <data key="d0">Preliminary Design</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The stage in product development where the overall structure and layout of the product are outlined.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0f0248523559d3c95f73c89fa69d7df2</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Experiments">
  <data key="d0">Experiments</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The phase in product development where experiments are conducted to validate the preliminary design and gather performance data.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0f0248523559d3c95f73c89fa69d7df2</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Product Design and Drafting">
  <data key="d0">Product Design and Drafting</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The stage in product development where a detailed product design is created, including drafting plans for manufacturing and assembly.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0f0248523559d3c95f73c89fa69d7df2</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Fabrication and Assembly">
  <data key="d0">Fabrication and Assembly</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The process of creating parts and components according to specifications and assembling them into a functional unit.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0f0248523559d3c95f73c89fa69d7df2</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Testing">
  <data key="d0">Testing</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The final stage in product development where the product is subjected to various tests to ensure it meets performance standards and safety requirements.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0f0248523559d3c95f73c89fa69d7df2</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Mathematical Model">
  <data key="d0">Mathematical Model</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">A mathematical model is a representation of a system using mathematical concepts and language to study its behavior and predict future states.&lt;SEP&gt;A representation of a system using mathematical expressions to analyze and predict system behavior.&lt;SEP&gt;A representation of the actual plant used in the design of control systems, which includes uncertainty or error.&lt;SEP&gt;Mathematical models are representations using mathematical language and concepts to describe and predict the behavior of real-world systems.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-80d07add21c293cc285a7efb53eea548&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-026e0fcd969517dd30ca84cdf6678f77&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0f0248523559d3c95f73c89fa69d7df2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-80377cf40599b7bb019354876db314d5</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Actual Plant">
  <data key="d0">Actual Plant</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The real-world system or process that the control system aims to manage, differing from its mathematical model.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0f0248523559d3c95f73c89fa69d7df2</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Uncertainty or Error">
  <data key="d0">Uncertainty or Error</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The difference between the actual plant and its mathematical model, considered in the design of robust control systems.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0f0248523559d3c95f73c89fa69d7df2</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Controller K(s)">
  <data key="d0">Controller K(s)</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">A component in control systems designed to manage the behavior and performance of the system.&lt;SEP&gt;A component of the control system designed to satisfy certain inequalities ensuring robust stability and performance.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0f0248523559d3c95f73c89fa69d7df2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6ee28303feb0c79b4fc2e2ca88dd29b6</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Feedback Control System">
  <data key="d0">Feedback Control System</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">A Feedback Control System is a sophisticated mechanism designed to regulate its state or output by utilizing feedback. This system achieves desired performance by adjusting inputs based on observed outputs, thus maintaining a prescribed relationship between the output and the reference input. It typically consists of components such as a proportional controller, a plant, and output measurement, which together analyze and design feedback control systems. By using the difference between the output and the reference input as a means of control, such systems effectively regulate behavior according to desired specifications, as seen in applications like room temperature regulation. Feedback control systems are integral to maintaining desired system outputs, ensuring that adjustments are continually made based on feedback to achieve optimal performance.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-620d81e2a5a2e80e3b426e6ed3a7d42b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-22085333984fa377791f298c557d58ea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d427a9d59bf3e814fd05720d1b33d0f8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-21f396695d06e061d0a62e0e0e209ea4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8230d3f2f6d179c68a787d4d88412e94&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5ab66ab2ea102011daec41a066d3877c</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Closed-Loop Control System">
  <data key="d0">Closed-Loop Control System</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">A Closed-Loop Control System is a dynamic system that utilizes feedback to control its state or output. It operates by receiving continuous feedback from sensors, which allows for ongoing monitoring and adjustment to ensure optimal performance. This type of control system architecture uses sensor feedback to adjust the input signal, thereby reducing error and achieving the desired output. The system continuously monitors its output and uses this information to maintain desired performance levels. Additionally, the Closed-Loop Control System is characterized by its transfer function and is often analyzed using MATLAB to evaluate different input responses.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-bb0309faec8e535ff54384234330d2c8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0b429aa524429e2d3a0bb88587574c34&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3ab453e611d8eb756961ea34b36c8dd8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-faf6d229bd33d9a677ddb2c4f3fe8552&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-21f396695d06e061d0a62e0e0e209ea4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7291b615cce3723bc1941e91175ccf27</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Open-Loop Control System">
  <data key="d0">Open-Loop Control System</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">A control system where the output has no effect on the control action and is not fed back for comparison with the input, relying on time-based operations.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-21f396695d06e061d0a62e0e0e209ea4</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Human Body">
  <data key="d0">Human Body</data>
  <data key="d1">geo</data>
  <data key="d2">The human body is described as a highly advanced feedback control system that maintains constant body temperature and blood pressure through physiological feedback.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-21f396695d06e061d0a62e0e0e209ea4</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Room Temperature Control System">
  <data key="d0">Room Temperature Control System</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">An example of a feedback control system that regulates room temperature by comparing actual temperature with desired temperature.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-21f396695d06e061d0a62e0e0e209ea4</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Thermostat">
  <data key="d0">Thermostat</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">A device used in room temperature control systems to turn heating or cooling equipment on or off based on temperature feedback.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-21f396695d06e061d0a62e0e0e209ea4</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Traffic Control System">
  <data key="d0">Traffic Control System</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">An open-loop control system that operates traffic signals on a time basis without feedback from traffic conditions.&lt;SEP&gt;Traffic Control System is a system designed to manage and regulate traffic flow on roads.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-21f396695d06e061d0a62e0e0e209ea4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-76155cfacd298ba99866d06587bc0e7d</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Closed-loop Control System">
  <data key="d0">Closed-loop Control System</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">A closed-loop control system uses feedback to control the state or output of a system, enhancing stability and performance.&lt;SEP&gt;A control system designed to regulate output by manipulating input, using feedback to achieve stable operation and track desired values.&lt;SEP&gt;Closed-loop control systems are systems where the output is continuously monitored and adjusted to meet desired performance specifications, often involving higher cost and power.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5518828c63927a29cbd23a5354674537&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c42bcac51d49ccc3eae60bc8173be4e9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-136efeec2362cbbe6ae14a66f80a408f</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Open-loop Control System">
  <data key="d0">Open-loop Control System</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Open-loop control systems are simpler and less expensive systems where the output is not monitored or adjusted, leading to potential errors from disturbances.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c42bcac51d49ccc3eae60bc8173be4e9</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Compensation">
  <data key="d0">Compensation</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Compensation in control systems involves methods like feedback, parallel, and series compensation to improve system performance.&lt;SEP&gt;Compensation refers to the modification of system dynamics to meet specific performance specifications, often involving the addition of compensators.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c42bcac51d49ccc3eae60bc8173be4e9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-faf6d229bd33d9a677ddb2c4f3fe8552</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Compensator">
  <data key="d0">Compensator</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">A Compensator is a device, component, or algorithm utilized in control systems to modify system dynamics and behavior in order to meet specific performance specifications. It functions by adjusting the output of a system, influencing the shape and position of closed-loop poles, and altering the transfer function. Compensators can take various forms, including lag, lead, and lag-lead types, and are employed to adjust system parameters, thereby improving the performance of the compensated system compared to an uncompensated system. The primary goal of a compensator is to enhance the control system's performance by modifying its characteristics to achieve desired outcomes.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-862057e40203dab14f5b486a9fcc1a5a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-97ff6cf0253e8f192c45acefe4824e0b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-610f25557b703d8c29a8efc2d292eb77&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c7a1931ad2ca3ae471cd3b57aa9a81eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-37c3fea424ed4692d82113ae9fd3f941&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7a366ed13150829b18c3d5756f1ce91c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-faf6d229bd33d9a677ddb2c4f3fe8552&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a8712fa7ddd9dc034d222eaf660d62f1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c42bcac51d49ccc3eae60bc8173be4e9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c3a55af272f2092fd9d053a8d89af4b4</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Transient Response">
  <data key="d0">Transient Response</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Transient Response refers to the behavior of a system, particularly a control system, in reaction to changes or disturbances in input. It is characterized by the system's initial reaction before reaching a steady state. This response is crucial for understanding system dynamics and is often analyzed in the context of a unit-step input. Key aspects of transient response include metrics such as rise time, maximum overshoot, settling time, and the influence of pole locations on the system's behavior. The analysis of transient response involves studying how quickly and effectively a system transitions from its initial state to a final steady state, often exhibiting oscillations during this period. This analysis provides valuable insights into the system's frequency response and overall stability.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d25f3afc6ec15e59ee641a894432f365&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-03cc52015d502a70a9646315437485f3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3b0fa6b31797f4f5fd2bb265e6ef2851&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-026e0fcd969517dd30ca84cdf6678f77&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eee931e9f23a29ab6271ed415dea0168&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-76155cfacd298ba99866d06587bc0e7d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a218a11cf9e9a1be2297492e35c21b7b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5ccf7d143be8a45626048d565c73daab&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-602e1bf386d85476513e06ee772f8a95&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ff7353abf978480820979c540d814ee3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c42bcac51d49ccc3eae60bc8173be4e9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fd104ba0a5d1c1ab6f6f3b08f2715be8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8f1e322d677e03984e2e1a0f412e6c1e</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Steady-state Error">
  <data key="d0">Steady-state Error</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Steady-state error is the difference between the desired and actual output in a control system when it reaches equilibrium.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c42bcac51d49ccc3eae60bc8173be4e9</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Performance Specifications">
  <data key="d0">Performance Specifications</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Performance Specifications are criteria that a system must meet to ensure desired performance and stability.&lt;SEP&gt;Performance specifications define the desired characteristics of a control system, such as damping ratio, overshoot, and settling time.&lt;SEP&gt;Performance specifications define the required tasks and performance levels for a control system, including transient and steady-state requirements.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c42bcac51d49ccc3eae60bc8173be4e9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7fae43bfb1918de812ae799078e161e9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b1dc98a9423242280b516d7ca4564e43</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Design Procedures">
  <data key="d0">Design Procedures</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Design procedures in control systems involve setting up mathematical models, adjusting compensators, and testing system performance.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c42bcac51d49ccc3eae60bc8173be4e9</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="W. R. Evans">
  <data key="d0">W. R. Evans</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">W. R. Evans is credited with developing the root-locus method for control system analysis around 1950.&lt;SEP&gt;W. R. Evans is the developer of the root-locus method, extensively used in control engineering for analyzing and designing control systems.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-72d535e84ba337e6877808174fd78fb5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d25f3afc6ec15e59ee641a894432f365</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Nyquist, Bode, Nichols, Hazen">
  <data key="d0">Nyquist, Bode, Nichols, Hazen</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Nyquist, Bode, Nichols, and Hazen are individuals who contributed to the development of the frequency-response method of control system analysis and design during 1940-1950.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d25f3afc6ec15e59ee641a894432f365</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Kalman">
  <data key="d0">Kalman</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Kalman introduced important concepts in modern control theory, such as controllability and observability.&lt;SEP&gt;Kalman introduced the concepts of controllability and observability, which are crucial in control systems design.&lt;SEP&gt;Kalman is credited with introducing the principle of duality, which relates to the concepts of controllability and observability in system dynamics.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e5be935af21c311ce3f7db40fa58876e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8a707fa18fd529fe3c1718e4570e7abb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d25f3afc6ec15e59ee641a894432f365</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Root-Locus Method">
  <data key="d0">Root-Locus Method</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The Root-Locus Method is a graphical technique widely used in control engineering and control systems design to analyze and reshape the transient response characteristics of closed-loop systems. This method involves plotting the roots of the characteristic equation of a system as its parameters, such as gain, vary. By visualizing the paths of system poles and zeros, engineers can determine system stability, gain parameters, and the locations of closed-loop poles. The Root-Locus Method is instrumental in designing compensators, including lag compensators, by addressing angle deficiencies at desired closed-loop pole positions. Through this approach, engineers can analyze system dynamics, stability, and behavior, ultimately facilitating the design of systems with desired performance characteristics.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-fef16edcd77ce0c9e06d5d42a6b361da&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f4c46665e037287407ddbff2463962aa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d25f3afc6ec15e59ee641a894432f365&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7fb6f6f4567355162f36e936387a3dc4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-72d535e84ba337e6877808174fd78fb5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-486351ecd11a42798affd79b7f5d143b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a19f7091cfb7ee780a1c0268e833e55f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5bc3dd38e993fe4446dce1bb2dde017f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a8712fa7ddd9dc034d222eaf660d62f1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-971e697aef8fd265a121f8524f1dc978&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0bfe9ed85a05514b41c868536d544660&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a5f65ec7a6f616b6865aa6c974202119&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bb6a1e10709bc450dad65923e1301042&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c7a1931ad2ca3ae471cd3b57aa9a81eb</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Frequency-Response Method">
  <data key="d0">Frequency-Response Method</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Frequency-response method is a technique used to analyze and design control systems by examining their behavior across different frequencies.&lt;SEP&gt;The Frequency-Response Method is a technique used in control systems analysis to assess system behavior.&lt;SEP&gt;The frequency-response method analyzes control systems by examining their response to sinusoidal inputs across various frequencies.&lt;SEP&gt;The frequency-response method is a technique used in control systems design to analyze and improve system behavior.&lt;SEP&gt;The frequency-response method is a traditional approach to control system analysis and design, focusing on system response to frequency variations.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-131d68e1d8fb1dac5267fbb38c0b52ef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d25f3afc6ec15e59ee641a894432f365&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-691177b7d933c82cade5a9fd3f182f4a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a2ddda49062cacf815e40ae9bc8167c7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c7a1931ad2ca3ae471cd3b57aa9a81eb</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="State-Space Method">
  <data key="d0">State-Space Method</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The state-space method is a modern approach to control system analysis, emphasizing concepts like controllability and observability.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d25f3afc6ec15e59ee641a894432f365</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Quadratic Optimal Regulator Problem">
  <data key="d0">Quadratic Optimal Regulator Problem</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">A mathematical problem involving the optimization of a control system to achieve the best performance based on a specific index.&lt;SEP&gt;The quadratic optimal regulator problem involves minimizing a performance index that is a quadratic function of state and control variables.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-420f00ddead05e80938b75e022567c58&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d25f3afc6ec15e59ee641a894432f365</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Mathematical Modeling">
  <data key="d0">Mathematical Modeling</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Mathematical Modeling refers to the use of mathematical equations and representations to analyze mechanical and electrical systems, as discussed in Chapter 3.&lt;SEP&gt;Mathematical modeling involves creating abstract representations of systems using mathematical concepts to predict and analyze behaviors.&lt;SEP&gt;Mathematical modeling involves creating equations to represent the dynamics of control systems, crucial for analysis and design.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a99de50670eed69da300e8009547bd36&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-44c29d5a44ee2d4ec0fe3c41c508ed96&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d25f3afc6ec15e59ee641a894432f365</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Steady-State Response">
  <data key="d0">Steady-State Response</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The Steady-State Response refers to the behavior of a system as time approaches infinity, after the initial transients have decayed and the system has settled into a consistent pattern. This response is characterized by the long-term behavior of control systems, where transient effects have diminished, leaving only constant or periodic behavior. It describes how the output of a control system behaves when it reaches equilibrium following a change in input, providing insight into the system's behavior once it has stabilized.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-053e8edb648cfc91fffeac7f5faec45b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5b3b6091500d4d6709d33e0c55198fc0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d25f3afc6ec15e59ee641a894432f365&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-026e0fcd969517dd30ca84cdf6678f77&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eee931e9f23a29ab6271ed415dea0168&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-76155cfacd298ba99866d06587bc0e7d</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Newton's Laws">
  <data key="d0">Newton's Laws</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Newton's Laws are physical laws governing mechanical systems, often used to derive differential equations for modeling such systems.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d34b551b0c812125805bc0bfa51cbbdb</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Kirchhoff's Laws">
  <data key="d0">Kirchhoff's Laws</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Kirchhoff's Laws are fundamental principles governing electrical circuits, including the law of current and voltage conservation.&lt;SEP&gt;Kirchhoff's Laws are used in electrical systems to derive differential equations that describe the system's dynamics.&lt;SEP&gt;Kirchhoff's laws are fundamental principles in electrical circuits, including the current law and voltage law, used for mathematical modeling.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-6cb2119ae5def40cdccd4f628a1d279a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a99de50670eed69da300e8009547bd36&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d34b551b0c812125805bc0bfa51cbbdb</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="State-Space Representation">
  <data key="d0">State-Space Representation</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">State-Space Representation is a comprehensive mathematical model used to describe the dynamics of physical systems, particularly in control systems and optimal control problems. It represents the system in terms of state variables, input, output, and their relationships through equations. This model is expressed using matrices, specifically involving matrices A, B, C, and D, which describe the system's dynamics, control, output, and direct transmission. The state-space representation effectively models nth-order systems of linear differential equations and is useful for system analysis and design. It provides a framework for understanding the time evolution and transformations of system dynamics, as illustrated in various mechanical systems, such as those depicted in figures like Figure 3–22 and Figure 3–32.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-120a948013a5d40b2a2baaea8da7dcb9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f2bf8d3735dca25672b127e3b08be5b7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5e25465698e9728fefd91973fb2caccd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5c59263c3980c23b4b65459cfd0557ec&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-04a1c0b94bb767b480f265211df2d3c7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d34b551b0c812125805bc0bfa51cbbdb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8b159c6715de41e6ec8764fe45124ff9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3b5c28d651a853ceed5a2bb812c9fd2d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e37fd32e67cfb4b06fcec5633146e195&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-180f42dfceafd3c905e78519e41eebca&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c35c8384335c3eddd1b49dc9cad5f9cf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8185f3f5e2ea2ff5f1955f91117943ca&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-80377cf40599b7bb019354876db314d5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e75920e90983d6045aaa3f10230d2c07&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-30afd1ea0d3bf0f6ff1f6ab5550759e8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f599210548e4a595268cbd4a1d4b5e3c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7d61c2c5328f39dc98d3d82a63c5fe2b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-86ce58db4d75e50b5241f0be0e62c4c5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-201ad2bff594c69ddcdebbbde0310588&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bb63a19b57948f30bda30ff5517c1eba&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e406a25bb828f53448bdbdb2984a1636&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-76155cfacd298ba99866d06587bc0e7d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-65dda872b092acba83b3de464ee4c24b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cd465393991808ab3570984282218782&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ae9d94f8cb3004790ff22f37200c200d</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Transfer-Function Representation">
  <data key="d0">Transfer-Function Representation</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Transfer-Function Representation is a mathematical model used in control systems to describe the input-output relationship.&lt;SEP&gt;Transfer-function representation is a mathematical model that relates the input to the output of a linear time-invariant system using a ratio of polynomials.&lt;SEP&gt;Transfer-function representation is a mathematical model used for transient-response or frequency-response analysis of linear, time-invariant systems.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-65dda872b092acba83b3de464ee4c24b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d34b551b0c812125805bc0bfa51cbbdb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ae9d94f8cb3004790ff22f37200c200d</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Linear Lumped-Parameter Model">
  <data key="d0">Linear Lumped-Parameter Model</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">A linear lumped-parameter model uses ordinary differential equations to describe systems, often simplifying by ignoring nonlinearities and distributed parameters.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d34b551b0c812125805bc0bfa51cbbdb</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Linear Time-Invariant Systems">
  <data key="d0">Linear Time-Invariant Systems</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">LTI systems are a class of systems in signal processing characterized by linearity and time-invariance.&lt;SEP&gt;Linear time-invariant systems are described by constant-coefficient differential equations and follow the principle of superposition.&lt;SEP&gt;Linear time-invariant systems have properties where the response to derivative and integral inputs can be derived from the original signal response.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a6b0fb37f85a26f35ea31ded16be6773&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d34b551b0c812125805bc0bfa51cbbdb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1e045a2c6fa20bf1beb094683eabf565</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Linear Time-Varying Systems">
  <data key="d0">Linear Time-Varying Systems</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Linear time-varying systems are described by differential equations with coefficients as functions of time, such as spacecraft control systems.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d34b551b0c812125805bc0bfa51cbbdb</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Spacecraft Control System">
  <data key="d0">Spacecraft Control System</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">A spacecraft control system is a time-varying system where the mass changes due to fuel consumption, affecting system dynamics.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d34b551b0c812125805bc0bfa51cbbdb</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Mathematical Models">
  <data key="d0">Mathematical Models</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Mathematical models are used to represent the behavior of physical systems, including thermal, liquid-level, and flow systems.&lt;SEP&gt;Mathematical models represent systems using mathematical expressions, often through differential equations to describe dynamics.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d34b551b0c812125805bc0bfa51cbbdb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-30f7b7fbdbec5ce9d814319c46f46972</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Differential Equations">
  <data key="d0">Differential Equations</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Differential equations are mathematical equations used to model the dynamics of systems, derived from physical laws.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d34b551b0c812125805bc0bfa51cbbdb</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Causality Principle">
  <data key="d0">Causality Principle</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The causality principle states that a system's current output depends on past inputs but not future inputs.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d34b551b0c812125805bc0bfa51cbbdb</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Optimal Control Problems">
  <data key="d0">Optimal Control Problems</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Optimal control problems involve finding the best control strategy for a system, often using state-space representation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d34b551b0c812125805bc0bfa51cbbdb</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Transient-Response Analysis">
  <data key="d0">Transient-Response Analysis</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Transient-response analysis examines how a system responds to changes in input over time.&lt;SEP&gt;Transient-response analysis involves studying the behavior of systems in response to changes, focusing on closed-loop poles and their effects.&lt;SEP&gt;Transient-response analysis involves studying the time-dependent behavior of a system when subjected to external changes, such as step, impulse, or ramp inputs.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-855e35313c15abd36b6e1671061bc057&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d34b551b0c812125805bc0bfa51cbbdb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e75920e90983d6045aaa3f10230d2c07</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Linear Systems">
  <data key="d0">Linear Systems</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Linear Systems are systems where relationships are represented by linear equations, but they are truly linear only within limited operating ranges.&lt;SEP&gt;Linear systems adhere to the principle of superposition, where the response to multiple inputs is the sum of individual responses.&lt;SEP&gt;Linear systems refer to mathematical models of systems that can be described using linear equations and matrices.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d34b551b0c812125805bc0bfa51cbbdb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ba9e6dce047704f9bd794f5f4ed1e2a7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b1e646fef80acb88f1ef990e507e7d48</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Transfer Function">
  <data key="d0">Transfer Function</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">A Transfer Function is a mathematical representation that describes the relationship between the input and output of a system. It is widely used in control systems, signal processing, and electrical circuit analysis to understand and design system behavior. This representation is crucial for analyzing system dynamics, stability, and performance, often expressed in the frequency domain or Laplace domain. Transfer Functions are typically represented as ratios of polynomials, such as G(s) or K/(s + 1), and can be used to calculate steady-state outputs and frequency responses. In control theory, they are particularly important for linear time-invariant systems, allowing engineers to model how input signals are transformed into output signals. Transfer Functions can also be derived from state-space equations and are integral to compensator design and system analysis, including the observer controller and pneumatic controllers. They provide insights into how systems respond to various inputs, characterized by magnitude, phase-angle properties, and time constants.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-6a256259f60bf17c11f960427ffa11ff&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-04598b6f66264ff6abc8a7ed77980955&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a8c47160042a9398aa709e25eded3b0c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-026e0fcd969517dd30ca84cdf6678f77&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d34b551b0c812125805bc0bfa51cbbdb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0bfe9ed85a05514b41c868536d544660&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-696e4f2dc4a3654f0413002d870333af&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5df7e896b70c3eb7634619fc0cc39c79&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8185f3f5e2ea2ff5f1955f91117943ca&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bc420a4e236b18e1114725fd8e9d12b5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-27ec4c68f753870a17122f4f0f1865d3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-23de25452ffebaa5122b237d5ec13605&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-562db1bae3e6a30605a16f1c339c6e3d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-955b6d1a9437f260081a7c8bfe86f0af&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c2db63f41e22678b776f42091570392a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-30afd1ea0d3bf0f6ff1f6ab5550759e8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f220f834d3bf211bf1917f262c17e60d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a104cc76c4ded119be1e066101146986&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-486351ecd11a42798affd79b7f5d143b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-855fbfd6cc8ae599aa41c969d6418c33&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b2a20c61ffd29ee123aa4008d1f4ba5f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2f9976290aaf5bc6b5c7806f43bcf303&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-925099acac18f050f91f2ea8c404ad69&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1b54450b954742096d3c9cc14431cc98&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e66f9ddf363acc92f98e91b488e76691&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-95dada5b51e73c98f754b3ae69765f91&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-87701236b0d6c49823799b31bf3b78b0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5c59263c3980c23b4b65459cfd0557ec&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9e6b7d8c1849925b0d0f1ad9793983e9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d8a3a4cd5f5a625120a672d9302619e4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2e3282077e1fe73808ab0efe43eaa3b8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-576041b0bc4054622300c536ef926759&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1022a5cf276c07ee56ffc50c13799b6d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-550487a7cadc4b8eeb83211703d232ec&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b1dc98a9423242280b516d7ca4564e43&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4a53699f6a5efb94ddd426141d45cba8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-de8767d6e1a854115045ae33d4513569&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f22851addd0d0d43a22e89bf8e277faf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3f800baebc1ea569d8d6199edc2fa8f3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-817b95a5144d0dce10f2c3a02063acbe&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7bd9d9a9ccff6dcc7aa39644785f5940&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c90e64ef4d3ea69c9b12410204a70bfb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c16de2cbe7e947bb8bc1c1fd886e33c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5bd763e4c9aeb66f33aa71ed14c8efd2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-98b7da91ec0504b2cb24aec37c61430a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-62a1cbf10c33f82d65ca8e2a6d86b67b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6b139b34ebbeaf26c5893fedc131b125&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9cff8b4398a7527756d649d91c3b5802&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5d0a99065421970bde5dae1689b5a3b1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-338b0c8db14460db8abd15b27fa186d5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b5d990bcffe0df8aa354e86649b81ea4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-577f4c0582663d9533ba9e75104e8038&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f2bf8d3735dca25672b127e3b08be5b7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4bf77eaf82ba369d832435b0e6f076eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c90a28b31b0ff2333ffc79bc30b633af&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-34c379ec40e0f14314e81b71389bf1c6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3c0ec79f7d7ca60c30e8465fa9c98468&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7f571134d683a0689226e17f5c8f12d2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1a7e6ee6eefc1562c87e6317d173028b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-193a843542d1b083c02404daa115957e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f02c1788b14b17fe251de4fbe29938db&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-182dc59d89ae078fb7ffb41be064595c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-50c4cfa192898028842df51ccba67794&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-61e97a29a080915ebe1313dbe1f7440e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c35c8384335c3eddd1b49dc9cad5f9cf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-da45ed46b3417493dc6e2a6d545bd8ed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cba4dc46aaf3979ba1619c43d08d1720&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e0c2c5d2084cf4dc42eac20165445910&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e75920e90983d6045aaa3f10230d2c07&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ccb516dd7fa038fc65484f2cc9669722&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ff7353abf978480820979c540d814ee3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8ec8d588e92dca4c7eedd0f19d3797cb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-289220cc5e6fcc41f8da8923ec0e0105&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-48f6fb89274b6880175da471dd77be24&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1c56a057e86a61de468030b87f550fa4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b900da580f82b4cfa8b1797c2b38f7d9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c4592f010cac7379fc36d89be453d068&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa37a4412b916a1ceb0df1a83526840b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e7ee96b6c7a32a3b2a58081228221eda&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f4c46665e037287407ddbff2463962aa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-af4a694419897c9fd7aca9d74f8703e5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fc700758bd11889bde17fb78bf15e8bf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-96901c8e0575c3c8488667c451ac4616&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-471b11b0e8da61744401ac854b4948f8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1344a7537de0ac1c48323314da3e0fa4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bb690227c0aeb29078796de6d1611d85&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7d685d32ace4fbb1daf3dfa98d88fd29&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f104d09d0c472d244729f4c0273b66e0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-053e8edb648cfc91fffeac7f5faec45b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-431aa652eeb49ebed96997dcdfc7e03d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c890c6f04ba9977fdf49ac7e887daf46&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-080439bdd387ba07fa0b308cc5ef8916&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5ae5ca3b7a89038d8b6dbc78ab1fcc29&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-180f42dfceafd3c905e78519e41eebca&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-264d0096e1fd7c9a5357d21df0d1368f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-23aaf2333ba5ac6c78dcc6d9ef3999d9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-484e9407831fd22fae06241d1b7e75e5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-31df32fb8d3f0302ba80571341b33840&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1431c462e05df5434bfc4728f13fa990&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-191e311819ac0dda5c8069a7a8cfa1fd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-016ebe44f8f410b4e420081ccf7a65f9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-997ccbc3904fc620e72b5c76955406ba&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e8fbd6e1853dfec68a3d5baf7fb35d91&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a99de50670eed69da300e8009547bd36&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-86ce58db4d75e50b5241f0be0e62c4c5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e5ae34faa3613c5b2f380b5bfb27f630&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b1e646fef80acb88f1ef990e507e7d48&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fab839ec7d08f56111abaab896a33188&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3402b465fcfb648982e49c37a455c189&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-646ed44ca6a37b5351e0aef0dd18547a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-76155cfacd298ba99866d06587bc0e7d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b773a713efcb75c181833b78c35c4f74&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-670bc9d19f233014556f6058e19da1a2</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Impulse-Response Function">
  <data key="d0">Impulse-Response Function</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Impulse-response function describes a system's output when presented with a brief input signal.&lt;SEP&gt;The Impulse-Response Function, also known as the weighting function, is the response of a linear time-invariant system to a unit-impulse input, providing a full description of the system's dynamic characteristics.&lt;SEP&gt;The impulse-response function, denoted as g(t), is the response of a linear time-invariant system to a unit-impulse input when initial conditions are zero. It provides complete information about the system dynamics.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c4592f010cac7379fc36d89be453d068&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d34b551b0c812125805bc0bfa51cbbdb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c890c6f04ba9977fdf49ac7e887daf46</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Laplace Transform">
  <data key="d0">Laplace Transform</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The Laplace Transform is a mathematical operation that transforms a function of time into a function of complex frequency, often represented in the s-domain. This technique is widely used to convert differential equations into algebraic equations, facilitating their analysis and solution. It is particularly useful in engineering and physics for analyzing dynamic systems, control systems, and linear time-invariant systems. The Laplace Transform aids in the design and analysis of control systems by transforming time-domain signals into the frequency domain, enabling easier manipulation and understanding of system behavior. It is also employed in signal processing to model and analyze system components and their frequency responses.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-053e8edb648cfc91fffeac7f5faec45b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5b187039cce28a12c36cf813e9f926ff&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a34c150b7357f195267cedd77a6ab328&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c890c6f04ba9977fdf49ac7e887daf46&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dd181394db0cba7b325f62f7a3a1029a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ad53164b58b57cd02287907a08cfbb42&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-284ad82d5a54aa2c3d44aa8399b9ac0c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cf864ce976b642afda7aa4f65e82808d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5b3b6091500d4d6709d33e0c55198fc0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-63d648e6a3fb274cf615d8ac91567e7d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3ab453e611d8eb756961ea34b36c8dd8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-27ec4c68f753870a17122f4f0f1865d3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ed23283c207553099a8ea220b94e1005&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5c123c891021bce024d99af35fd34585&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-51b7aad7be1a3e458fc1c221ae0abd4d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c4592f010cac7379fc36d89be453d068&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7bd9d9a9ccff6dcc7aa39644785f5940&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7ea08bc01558a1369597e1539971f894&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2f9976290aaf5bc6b5c7806f43bcf303&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f7f67cd40a9e32be93dc80ac45982fc3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fc700758bd11889bde17fb78bf15e8bf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fab839ec7d08f56111abaab896a33188&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cd566e31ec5118dde48a4f149d4c5469&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dfe09b7b703c389e8f2522a6967742f4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-925099acac18f050f91f2ea8c404ad69&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-62a1cbf10c33f82d65ca8e2a6d86b67b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c1b12ce7d358a5298c40691eca86f278&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1d179610b2c623234f00bb642b238054&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a5b38ba15c11c9500daafd6a0fa68942&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ba4c929673f3e997b800f45ab5b8f8b5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ce82309cc277894effb1a9e9bc5be0e3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9571f0c498fec38011c9f30cf7ab85ab</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Convolution Integral">
  <data key="d0">Convolution Integral</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The Convolution Integral is a mathematical operation used to express the output of a linear time-invariant system as the integral of the product of the input and the system's impulse response.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c890c6f04ba9977fdf49ac7e887daf46</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Linear Time-Invariant System">
  <data key="d0">Linear Time-Invariant System</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">A Linear Time-Invariant System is a system whose output is linearly related to its input and whose properties do not change over time.&lt;SEP&gt;A linear time-invariant system is a type of system where its characteristics do not change over time, allowing for predictable responses to inputs.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c4592f010cac7379fc36d89be453d068&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c890c6f04ba9977fdf49ac7e887daf46</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Differential Equation">
  <data key="d0">Differential Equation</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">A Differential Equation is a mathematical equation that relates a function with its derivatives, often used to model the behavior of dynamic systems.&lt;SEP&gt;A differential equation is a mathematical equation that relates some function with its derivatives.&lt;SEP&gt;A third-order differential equation system illustrating relationships between variables y and u with coefficients a1, a2, a3, b0, b1, b2, and b3.&lt;SEP&gt;Differential equations are equations involving derivatives of a function and are used to describe various physical systems.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-1d179610b2c623234f00bb642b238054&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c284789e81baac7fc644cb784a374af&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b900da580f82b4cfa8b1797c2b38f7d9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c890c6f04ba9977fdf49ac7e887daf46</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Algebraic Equation">
  <data key="d0">Algebraic Equation</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">An Algebraic Equation is a mathematical statement of equality involving variables and constants, used to represent system dynamics in the Laplace domain.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c890c6f04ba9977fdf49ac7e887daf46</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Automatic Control Systems">
  <data key="d0">Automatic Control Systems</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Automatic control systems consist of components that manage and direct the behavior of other systems using feedback. They are represented using block diagrams to illustrate signal flow and control actions.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c4592f010cac7379fc36d89be453d068</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Block Diagram">
  <data key="d0">Block Diagram</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">A Block Diagram is a graphical representation used in fields such as control engineering and signal processing to illustrate the components and interconnections within a system. It visually depicts the architecture of control systems and signal processing systems, showing the flow of signals and the relationships between various components, such as input, output, and feedback loops. Block Diagrams often use blocks and arrows to indicate signal direction and interactions, highlighting the unilateral property of signal flow. They are used to model, simulate, and analyze complex systems, simplifying the representation of dynamic behavior and mathematical models. Through schematic and pictorial representations, Block Diagrams help in understanding the structure and function of systems, facilitating analysis and optimization.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-bde48fe7be57bee4bb18f23aaf9bf6a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-577f4c0582663d9533ba9e75104e8038&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f104d09d0c472d244729f4c0273b66e0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-51c9b9d9d69283b7b46094d2f229a5f2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5b187039cce28a12c36cf813e9f926ff&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-adb718308239fee157c153fdd63d6ea6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f1897ad76eeb7398a9874d0d8978aa7c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7124a0bbfe59c091664e7c98717fbde3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-95a8d8a1b3cfb0710c46f13ae2e3b48e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9e97cb703344a8d83dd7f1085591043d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-04a1c0b94bb767b480f265211df2d3c7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3c0ec79f7d7ca60c30e8465fa9c98468&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8c259f83cd332a43d6788c73b5342ba4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dfdd97bb0a977764c4e6f1642a1944c6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2bcf605f83825a20ced09f3cfbb124a8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-da4cffe0215472e30c589d1be7e6d819&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9571f0c498fec38011c9f30cf7ab85ab&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-db115ced573d3098e7cb02b4ddf8625f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c7a34174c65e8a6a1a11ac9ee67b0bbe&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5ab66ab2ea102011daec41a066d3877c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-45a3caf311e2ab85a8b8795fbe5c9365&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-61e97a29a080915ebe1313dbe1f7440e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4178d8ac5083f4ba940096cd490ec0cf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a49db1d02aaff7365470c4256ccfb82f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8bb8d79a4bce9c3a0571e1ec2592d1a4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-586f97239ba09b5129d50fc14028adbc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3054b048b0a72e379da574dd802acf5c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-51b0d24e65c00006971d0aaf3046f316&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0f3bd095a4a20703f7118bb28e95675a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9b285f50932bff8d3ec6715415855398&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9ba0b5a3e032183793285284f3603fd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0690050aa436f47134092f69ec8ed18b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8ec8d588e92dca4c7eedd0f19d3797cb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-191e311819ac0dda5c8069a7a8cfa1fd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d6018e5e7fd6c2f5cfc4bd67745b544a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ef73554446b2eed91c76e39c989b498d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-273f66b6e44351603fc5160de59ad839&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-241ff02c460b748c0a8d1135d536c107&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-486351ecd11a42798affd79b7f5d143b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-51ed07bf7b1b56e1246deb3b4a2fd5cb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-35ca799f31090a72592882ce4794e5f5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8bf9e3ab65c6a99b8b53253749e48338&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c4592f010cac7379fc36d89be453d068&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a6b4cd2d53cd90421b101e0434c0e9d1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7d61c2c5328f39dc98d3d82a63c5fe2b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d0ef5febc821439635564c2c2af2c325&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a081030b2e96d508f9daac35a26c2be8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0b0975c8819fd43e77406652cac9bd23&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aa017cb19dfa7211c0c2b6a589a80f57&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-12d9f86a0ab5347bed74adfcf7735e09&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c284789e81baac7fc644cb784a374af&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2f9976290aaf5bc6b5c7806f43bcf303&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5bd763e4c9aeb66f33aa71ed14c8efd2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-232100dc2d77888cd2276dfa3e1c28a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cd437ba37843beb72e13fcc99049d49c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a5b3fa9ac1d3ce61d8dbdd21acb60d10&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f8b50aac79f087fdc5dd68834ab09cb3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6d8c0bd0542b3fd879eac68953207dee&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9cff8b4398a7527756d649d91c3b5802&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ce82309cc277894effb1a9e9bc5be0e3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3d9ad31b90878cd9ce75a3c06dde5440&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7e83e0b0f093a4a5ee928c26cb73ad75&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9838df333da6616701c1cce66c00e732</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Signal">
  <data key="d0">Signal</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">A signal is a function representing varying quantities, such as voltage or current, used in analyzing system responses.&lt;SEP&gt;Signals are arrows in a block diagram that represent the flow of information from input to output through functional blocks. They carry the dimension of input signals and are processed by transfer functions.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c4592f010cac7379fc36d89be453d068&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a59bbd3f86b4923fd22b10788ad73d53</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Equation (2–2)">
  <data key="d0">Equation (2–2)</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Equation (2–2) provides the impulse response of a system, crucial for analyzing system dynamics.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c4592f010cac7379fc36d89be453d068</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="G(s)">
  <data key="d0">G(s)</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">G(s) is a transfer function extensively used in control systems to model and analyze the behavior and response of the system to input signals. It represents the system's dynamics and is utilized to understand how the system reacts in the frequency domain. G(s) is a complex function, discussed in the context of analytic functions and poles, and is often expressed in the Laplace domain, which helps in characterizing the system's response characteristics. 

In specific applications, G(s) can be defined in various forms, such as the open-loop transfer function, which is given by K divided by the product of (T1s + 1) and (T2s + 1), or as 1/(s^2 + 0.8s + 1), which is used to analyze system stability and characteristics. It can represent the main system or the controller within the control system, responsible for generating control actions based on the error signal. 

G(s) is also integral to frequency response analysis and is frequently utilized in MATLAB programs to assess system stability. It can be composed of multiple transfer functions, such as G1(s) and G2(s), to facilitate a comprehensive analysis of the system's stability. Overall, G(s) plays a crucial role in both continuous-time and discrete-time analysis, transforming input signals according to specific rules to contribute to the overall system analysis.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-37fb7998dd9fe418c1b904b5fad6ac47&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5b3b6091500d4d6709d33e0c55198fc0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-76bc75a542f1bbffc63059af2a43bbeb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-989ef45348532327c1757ed1c157833e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9b8dd02e28c6fb3ac089290386b4c847&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9e830557547fd0c9d37cbd07da38c0e6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-021d406fb1875a04e6ca85d6a4589897&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5ce576a87f38c3c3621dc3b3d8f16913&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c4592f010cac7379fc36d89be453d068&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e67cb8477adc7c0ef97482e4e7be7fa4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7f571134d683a0689226e17f5c8f12d2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a480968a716a749c7646332b8d7dec12&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bb6a1e10709bc450dad65923e1301042&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3dac0ceabd7b52fc29d821c18da7fcb1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fd104ba0a5d1c1ab6f6f3b08f2715be8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f86521265de1eaaef568da798d2e3a53</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
