</node>
<node id="Branch Point">
  <data key="d0">Branch Point</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">A branch point is a location in a block diagram where a signal splits to go to multiple blocks or summing points.&lt;SEP&gt;Branch Point is a concept in complex analysis where a function behaves differently when encircling a point.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e8171fcc2a296f92a6bcda8de5f8a214&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-faf6d229bd33d9a677ddb2c4f3fe8552</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Closed-Loop System">
  <data key="d0">Closed-Loop System</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">A Closed-Loop System is a type of control system characterized by the feedback of its output into the input to reduce errors, enhance stability, and achieve desired performance. This system configuration allows for automatic adjustments by comparing the output with the desired input, thus regulating the plant's operation and maintaining desired behavior. The system is often analyzed for stability using criteria such as the Nyquist Stability Criterion and can be defined by transfer functions, such as C(s)/R(s) = 1/(s^2 + 2ζs + 1), which are used for unit-step response analysis. Closed-Loop Systems are designed to suppress disturbances and the effects of manufacturing tolerances, ensuring that the system remains stable and performs as intended.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-053e8edb648cfc91fffeac7f5faec45b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9067de99754a18f08e6cfd3a76b28877&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-696e4f2dc4a3654f0413002d870333af&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c85e4e0b258fa41e4bb98a0ed524e05c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-50c4cfa192898028842df51ccba67794&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c7a34174c65e8a6a1a11ac9ee67b0bbe&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1022a5cf276c07ee56ffc50c13799b6d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-094d0f53918a4b57661980c588c5dd88&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-61e97a29a080915ebe1313dbe1f7440e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-91a2a152c44c8f2298858dd4c49a71a1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7fb6f6f4567355162f36e936387a3dc4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c0b45e8c05ce6d6a2e33cb4a65bc30d4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-72d535e84ba337e6877808174fd78fb5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-021d406fb1875a04e6ca85d6a4589897&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4a3189f7cb3fb2865b828460dc211a36&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9b6f2069d9a69517d279f1786974d9e9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f220f834d3bf211bf1917f262c17e60d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b780aae2274d6d12811004661f2bb425&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f599210548e4a595268cbd4a1d4b5e3c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ed1ff4c236c16a58ee408da4a88effc4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1c56a057e86a61de468030b87f550fa4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4ef7861303c3473cd3d47c68848af64d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f59f8623e4ff8e64d49175daeeadad05&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5bd763e4c9aeb66f33aa71ed14c8efd2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6b1619851fc851316cb57de0ea4fe27f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a5b3fa9ac1d3ce61d8dbdd21acb60d10&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e8171fcc2a296f92a6bcda8de5f8a214</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Sensor">
  <data key="d0">Sensor</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">A sensor is a crucial component in control systems, serving as a measuring element that converts output variables into comparable signals for feedback. This feedback is essential for ensuring accurate control and regulation within the system. Sensors are typically represented by a circle symbol in diagrams, although they can also be depicted as a rectangle with an arrow pointing to the left. They are used to detect changes in the environment or system, such as measuring pressure in a chamber, and send electrical signals to the control system. The sensor measures the output of the plant and provides this information back to the controller, generating an error signal necessary for the controller to produce control signals. This continuous feedback loop allows for ongoing monitoring and adjustment of the system's actual output, ensuring optimal performance.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-32e837730a9d05b1df592f0ae66e9fc2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0279e47752e3eff4e78f68ce4e1c721f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b1abf1bd63b798f8e59025a047459778&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3c317b9ff2b2110c0c0b14f7d7fbc89c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3ab453e611d8eb756961ea34b36c8dd8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3054b048b0a72e379da574dd802acf5c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9b847793aceb37d63da174b89e67a501&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2ade22120b73dcc1eb2fff81533ae0bd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7291b615cce3723bc1941e91175ccf27</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Open-Loop Transfer Function">
  <data key="d0">Open-Loop Transfer Function</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The Open-Loop Transfer Function is a mathematical representation of a control system's behavior before feedback is applied. It describes the relationship between the input and output of the system, highlighting the system's dynamics without feedback. This function is crucial for initial stability analysis and system design, influencing phase angle, magnitude, and pole positions. It is represented by expressions such as G(s) = K/s(s+1)(s+2) or \(G_{c}(s)G(s)\), and is essential for understanding system dynamics, stability, and performance. The Open-Loop Transfer Function is used in control theory for methods like the Nyquist Criterion and root-locus design, providing a foundation for assessing system stability and performance.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0948b1e428ea01364b1b1ec1955431f0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-96a98f812e32e184c15978c0d7771176&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a3d1b21ce83b859c71c888d5c7aae6ba&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-87b0e0bfbf5774f7c90a99f131e0bb53&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-12f9482a2de5a4049338d2f20af641c2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a8712fa7ddd9dc034d222eaf660d62f1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-004c5161c3b1d015eb48ffa3f8bf337c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7fb6f6f4567355162f36e936387a3dc4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-72d535e84ba337e6877808174fd78fb5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3ab453e611d8eb756961ea34b36c8dd8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9e943fefbd15dc3bfc97bcb9515cd1eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4a3189f7cb3fb2865b828460dc211a36&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5bc3dd38e993fe4446dce1bb2dde017f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ff7353abf978480820979c540d814ee3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f22851addd0d0d43a22e89bf8e277faf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1c56a057e86a61de468030b87f550fa4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-189305ffdfb5a3f7a9695e56559bcef3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-241ff02c460b748c0a8d1135d536c107&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8221452e166faf8569286a459dc76b3e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f59f8623e4ff8e64d49175daeeadad05&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-971e697aef8fd265a121f8524f1dc978&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-36751d4e3f942193eb2e541b3e4dd23b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7617e3bbfe2c63a69317713315650eb3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-896487ddef7a3f411006102c6c7a9b2b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6b1619851fc851316cb57de0ea4fe27f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a5b3fa9ac1d3ce61d8dbdd21acb60d10&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-feb8129fd0acf4bdee571b9fbeafe347&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-40ee0f407a8a7e48b91d23a82652414f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-731aadaa135cc2ef1c9482aab829f4e1</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Feedforward Transfer Function">
  <data key="d0">Feedforward Transfer Function</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">A function representing the ratio of the output to the actuating error signal in a control system.&lt;SEP&gt;Feedforward Transfer Function is a mathematical expression used to describe the input-output relationship in a control system.&lt;SEP&gt;The Feedforward Transfer Function is a mathematical representation of the input-output relationship in a control system.&lt;SEP&gt;The Feedforward Transfer Function is part of the control system that contributes to the overall system response.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-400e4b0a84de7cb82216914ca205adc9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dd970bcee6ec8782e0e15281c48fc6f7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3ab453e611d8eb756961ea34b36c8dd8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a19f7091cfb7ee780a1c0268e833e55f</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Closed-Loop Transfer Function">
  <data key="d0">Closed-Loop Transfer Function</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The Closed-Loop Transfer Function is a mathematical representation of a control system that describes the relationship between the input and output within a closed-loop configuration. This function is crucial for analyzing the system's dynamics, stability, and performance, as it incorporates both feedback and feedforward elements. By factoring in feedback loops, the closed-loop transfer function evaluates the overall system response to various inputs, including unit-step disturbance inputs. This representation is key in understanding the behavior of control systems and is used to assess their output response to specific input signals, ensuring effective system stability and performance analysis.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-441dbc13e7ef7807ed52d100c9a4f041&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0948b1e428ea01364b1b1ec1955431f0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-96a98f812e32e184c15978c0d7771176&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-332d7d5b6322874bc053c36563dd2b71&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-692c80987f518b576a9461026d5a6aad&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a3d1b21ce83b859c71c888d5c7aae6ba&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ed88cc0500a21b898e5a196a843b2781&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8a2cf2ba37e76669ac29da1b6aa8f4af&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5cf6791e215ed7abe6fbda5eedbb50b9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3ab453e611d8eb756961ea34b36c8dd8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-181fee5061e5d9adf0f04d196cc15e7f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8c39c97551687b0bf865de0bebf31277&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fd104ba0a5d1c1ab6f6f3b08f2715be8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8f1e322d677e03984e2e1a0f412e6c1e</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
