<node id="Automatic Controller">
  <data key="d0">Automatic Controller</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">An automatic controller is a crucial component in industrial control systems that compares actual plant output with reference input, determining deviations and producing control signals to reduce these deviations.&lt;SEP&gt;Automatic Controller is a component in a control system that receives reference inputs, produces error signals, and generates actuating signals based on errors.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-8537419dec5c5aa7a095925b682e6a09&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7291b615cce3723bc1941e91175ccf27</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Plant/Process">
  <data key="d0">Plant/Process</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">Plant/Process is a component in a control system that receives amplified signals from the amplifier and responds accordingly, forming part of a closed-loop system.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-8537419dec5c5aa7a095925b682e6a09</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Error Detector">
  <data key="d0">Error Detector</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">Error Detector is a component that compares reference inputs with actual outputs from the plant, producing error signals for the automatic controller.&lt;SEP&gt;The error detector is a component in control systems that compares reference input with actual output, producing an error signal for the controller.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-8537419dec5c5aa7a095925b682e6a09&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7291b615cce3723bc1941e91175ccf27</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="G1(s)">
  <data key="d0">G1(s)</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">A specific transfer function characterized by the equation G1(s) = 1 + Ts / (1 + T1s), exhibiting even symmetry.&lt;SEP&gt;G1(s) denotes a component or system that modifies the signal flow, situated below R(s) on the left.&lt;SEP&gt;G1(s) is a block representing a transfer function in control systems, used in signal flow diagrams.&lt;SEP&gt;G1(s) is a component of the transfer function, part of the equation G(s) = G1(s)G2(s).&lt;SEP&gt;G1(s) refers to a component in a control system, potentially unstable, that processes input signals.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5518828c63927a29cbd23a5354674537&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-562db1bae3e6a30605a16f1c339c6e3d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8537419dec5c5aa7a095925b682e6a09&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a480968a716a749c7646332b8d7dec12&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c85e4e0b258fa41e4bb98a0ed524e05c</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="G2(s)">
  <data key="d0">G2(s)</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">A specific transfer function characterized by the equation G2(s) = (1 - Ts) / (1 + T1s), exhibiting even symmetry.&lt;SEP&gt;G2(s) denotes a component or system that modifies the signal flow, situated below R(s) on the right.&lt;SEP&gt;G2(s) is a component of the transfer function with poles in the right-half s plane, affecting system stability.&lt;SEP&gt;G2(s) is another block representing a transfer function in control systems, used in signal flow diagrams.&lt;SEP&gt;G2(s) refers to a component in a control system that can be chosen to stabilize the system by compensating for the instability of G1(s).</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5518828c63927a29cbd23a5354674537&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-562db1bae3e6a30605a16f1c339c6e3d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8537419dec5c5aa7a095925b682e6a09&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a480968a716a749c7646332b8d7dec12&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c85e4e0b258fa41e4bb98a0ed524e05c</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="R(s)">
  <data key="d0">R(s)</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">R(s) is a crucial component in control and signal processing systems, representing the reference input or external input signal. It is typically depicted in the top-left corner of block diagrams as the starting point of the system's signal flow. In control systems, R(s) serves as the reference or setpoint that the system aims to track, influencing the behavior of system components to achieve the desired output, often denoted as C(s). It is processed within the system, particularly into blocks like G1, and is essential for the operation of transfer functions. R(s) is also represented in the Laplace domain, indicating the characteristics of the input signal. Positioned below and to the right of Disturbance D(s) in some diagrams, R(s) is integral to the system's operation, acting as both an intermediate signal and a label for specific components or outputs within the diagram.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-80115d2c91ecc61916cf6b117778b14b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-799625a96afba94ba9a1c4461a64170d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3c0ec79f7d7ca60c30e8465fa9c98468&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8c259f83cd332a43d6788c73b5342ba4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dfdd97bb0a977764c4e6f1642a1944c6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c85e4e0b258fa41e4bb98a0ed524e05c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-952ac88fc20d94741d460ff36bcefd2c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fd104ba0a5d1c1ab6f6f3b08f2715be8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c0b45e8c05ce6d6a2e33cb4a65bc30d4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6d4db33b4e6388556833f75db47703f2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b987c9f4905e5b5d570e0dc620016e58&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-273f66b6e44351603fc5160de59ad839&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d30d89b485b27ebd673bf1d2c57c24a7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-efbee75e0f53cdd0a07a900923cddb09&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d0ef5febc821439635564c2c2af2c325&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3dac0ceabd7b52fc29d821c18da7fcb1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f8b50aac79f087fdc5dd68834ab09cb3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8537419dec5c5aa7a095925b682e6a09&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f87ef3fd6a4c1856d15e4ad169e98183&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d64c265c70a4b363e906e1040d781b2b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e67cb8477adc7c0ef97482e4e7be7fa4</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="C(s)">
  <data key="d0">C(s)</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">C(s) is a critical component in control systems, representing the final processed signal and the output in a signal flow diagram. It serves as the controlled variable, which is regulated to match the reference input, and is the final destination in the block diagram, signifying the output signal in the closed-loop control system. Represented in the Laplace domain, C(s) is the output signal produced by the processing of G(s) and results from the input processed through the gain element K. It is achieved through the application of transfer functions and the processing of input signals, ultimately representing the desired output. C(s) reflects the overall response of the system to both the reference input and disturbances, and it is the final output of the system after being processed through the G3 gain block, indicating the output signal characteristics.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c0b45e8c05ce6d6a2e33cb4a65bc30d4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d30d89b485b27ebd673bf1d2c57c24a7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3dac0ceabd7b52fc29d821c18da7fcb1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f87ef3fd6a4c1856d15e4ad169e98183&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-efbee75e0f53cdd0a07a900923cddb09&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b987c9f4905e5b5d570e0dc620016e58&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e67cb8477adc7c0ef97482e4e7be7fa4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8537419dec5c5aa7a095925b682e6a09&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8c259f83cd332a43d6788c73b5342ba4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3c0ec79f7d7ca60c30e8465fa9c98468&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d0ef5febc821439635564c2c2af2c325&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dfdd97bb0a977764c4e6f1642a1944c6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c85e4e0b258fa41e4bb98a0ed524e05c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d64c265c70a4b363e906e1040d781b2b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-80115d2c91ecc61916cf6b117778b14b</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Block Diagrams">
  <data key="d0">Block Diagrams</data>
  <data key="d1">event</data>
  <data key="d2">Block Diagrams are visual representations used to illustrate the flow of signals in control systems, showing different configurations of blocks and arrows.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-8537419dec5c5aa7a095925b682e6a09</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Signal Flow">
  <data key="d0">Signal Flow</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Signal Flow refers to the movement and direction of signals within the control system as depicted in the block diagram.&lt;SEP&gt;Signal Flow refers to the path and connections of signals within a control system, depicted in block diagrams.&lt;SEP&gt;Signal flow refers to the movement and transformation of input signals through various components in the block diagram.&lt;SEP&gt;Signal flow refers to the movement of information or signals between components in a system, often depicted by arrows.&lt;SEP&gt;The movement of signals through the blocks in a control system, indicating how data is processed.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-232100dc2d77888cd2276dfa3e1c28a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3c0ec79f7d7ca60c30e8465fa9c98468&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8537419dec5c5aa7a095925b682e6a09&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d0ef5febc821439635564c2c2af2c325&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d1c6b6475b03e27e6db3ac883a90115c</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Closed-loop System">
  <data key="d0">Closed-loop System</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">A Closed-loop System is a control system configuration characterized by the use of feedback to ensure stability and desired performance. In this system, the output is continuously monitored and fed back into the input, allowing for automatic adjustments to maintain stability in the face of disturbances and achieve desired results. The feedback loop is integral to the system's operation, enabling it to compare the actual output with the desired output and make necessary adjustments to minimize the effect of variations in component parameters. This system is designed to stabilize an otherwise unstable open-loop system, and its transfer function is nearly equal to the inverse of the feedback function when the open-loop gain is high. The Closed-loop System is often analyzed using phase angles, frequency response, and Bode diagrams, and is depicted in block diagrams featuring an input signal R(s) and an output signal C(s) with a transfer function K/(s+1)(s+5). Overall, the Closed-loop System is engineered to achieve continuous regulation, ensuring the system responds correctly to changes and maintains the desired level of performance.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-2f7f4ddee3ffc4ad47062df678fff024&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-37fb7998dd9fe418c1b904b5fad6ac47&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-577f4c0582663d9533ba9e75104e8038&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4b00b4278d0056b00df089ee6c703432&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-92e23c313eaf5a6d348bce8a1588be0f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-958b33ac0865635b15e9b137a06a79e4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f8b50aac79f087fdc5dd68834ab09cb3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-60969709c0f545814b039c0d77d040ef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-219f7fb5973694e4ed3fc49b9c5d4c60&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-75625ca25735063f07915ffbf3fb4581&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0e2d5c8521bc1f35d5a5daaf0b7fe7eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8537419dec5c5aa7a095925b682e6a09&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1fc8172454011b7e111636e450b0431d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ce82309cc277894effb1a9e9bc5be0e3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d64c265c70a4b363e906e1040d781b2b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e67cb8477adc7c0ef97482e4e7be7fa4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5743b50920c06e8fe8331392a198cbcc</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Actuator">
  <data key="d0">Actuator</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">An actuator is a device represented by a square symbol in diagrams, used to move or control a mechanism or system.&lt;SEP&gt;An actuator is a device responsible for moving or controlling a mechanism or system.&lt;SEP&gt;An actuator is a power device used in control systems to produce input to the plant based on control signals, which can be electric, hydraulic, or pneumatic in nature.&lt;SEP&gt;The Actuator is a component in control systems that responds to the output from the state feedback gain matrix.&lt;SEP&gt;The actuator in the control system is represented by an integrator with a transfer function, responsible for converting control signals into mechanical motion.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f1df4ff44b7c85b353430b33b36adb1b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9b847793aceb37d63da174b89e67a501&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ed23283c207553099a8ea220b94e1005&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7291b615cce3723bc1941e91175ccf27&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7e83e0b0f093a4a5ee928c26cb73ad75</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Two-Position Controller">
  <data key="d0">Two-Position Controller</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">A two-position or on-off controller is a type of control system where the actuating element has only two fixed positions, typically used for simple and cost-effective control.&lt;SEP&gt;Two-Position Controller is a type of controller that operates by switching between two discrete states.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-76155cfacd298ba99866d06587bc0e7d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7291b615cce3723bc1941e91175ccf27</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Proportional Controller">
  <data key="d0">Proportional Controller</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">A Proportional Controller is a type of industrial controller used in control systems to regulate variables by adjusting the control signal proportionally to the error signal. It is characterized by a gain, denoted as 'K' or 'Kp', which stabilizes the system by providing proportional gain. This controller takes the error signal as input and produces an output signal proportional to this error, making it an integral component in feedback loops for continuous monitoring and adjustment. Often used for simple control tasks, the Proportional Controller is an amplifier with adjustable gain that can affect steady-state error by providing torque to counteract disturbance torque. It is commonly used in conjunction with integral control for more complex control actions.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-204365918a7a13528bbf287196b97d31&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-620d81e2a5a2e80e3b426e6ed3a7d42b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cd437ba37843beb72e13fcc99049d49c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6f67e9b469ce0543584be32ab4c6f89c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-12881ab3d33126ccd266da1257ad2563&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ff388ffb7bf75847e1985f1473658eaf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1e045a2c6fa20bf1beb094683eabf565&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8c259f83cd332a43d6788c73b5342ba4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7291b615cce3723bc1941e91175ccf27&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-db868c08afc68de9d8850f06c963dfd1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-de8767d6e1a854115045ae33d4513569&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3d9ad31b90878cd9ce75a3c06dde5440</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Integral Controller">
  <data key="d0">Integral Controller</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">A controller that reduces steady-state errors by adding integral control action, potentially leading to system instability if not properly managed.&lt;SEP&gt;A type of industrial automatic controller where control actions are based on the integral of the error signal.&lt;SEP&gt;An Integral Controller is a type of controller that integrates the error signal over time to eliminate steady-state error in control systems.&lt;SEP&gt;An integral controller is a type of industrial controller that adjusts its output based on the integral of the error signal, helping eliminate steady-state error.&lt;SEP&gt;The Integral Controller adjusts its output at a rate proportional to the error signal, integrating over time.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-6f67e9b469ce0543584be32ab4c6f89c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cd437ba37843beb72e13fcc99049d49c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ff388ffb7bf75847e1985f1473658eaf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8c259f83cd332a43d6788c73b5342ba4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7291b615cce3723bc1941e91175ccf27</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Proportional-Plus-Integral Controller">
  <data key="d0">Proportional-Plus-Integral Controller</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">A proportional-plus-integral controller combines proportional and integral control actions to improve system stability and reduce steady-state error.&lt;SEP&gt;A type of controller that combines proportional control with integral action, used to eliminate steady-state speed error in control systems.&lt;SEP&gt;The Proportional-Plus-Integral Controller adds integral control to eliminate offset due to torque disturbance.&lt;SEP&gt;The Proportional-Plus-Integral Controller combines proportional and integral control actions for enhanced system regulation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-204365918a7a13528bbf287196b97d31&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a49db1d02aaff7365470c4256ccfb82f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ff388ffb7bf75847e1985f1473658eaf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7291b615cce3723bc1941e91175ccf27</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Proportional-Plus-Derivative Controller">
  <data key="d0">Proportional-Plus-Derivative Controller</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">A proportional-plus-derivative controller combines proportional and derivative control actions to enhance system response and stability.&lt;SEP&gt;A proportional-plus-derivative controller is a type of controller used in control systems to achieve desired damping ratios and natural frequencies.&lt;SEP&gt;The Proportional-Plus-Derivative Controller combines proportional and derivative control actions to address error signal changes.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-bb2cafb27b000379bc871ea7923a83dd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ff388ffb7bf75847e1985f1473658eaf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7291b615cce3723bc1941e91175ccf27</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Proportional-Plus-Integral-Plus-Derivative Controller">
  <data key="d0">Proportional-Plus-Integral-Plus-Derivative Controller</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">A proportional-plus-integral-plus-derivative controller incorporates all three control actions to optimize system performance and stability.&lt;SEP&gt;This controller combines proportional, integral, and derivative actions, offering comprehensive control capabilities.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-ff388ffb7bf75847e1985f1473658eaf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7291b615cce3723bc1941e91175ccf27</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Pneumatic Controller">
  <data key="d0">Pneumatic Controller</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">A pneumatic controller is a versatile device used to regulate pressure, flow, and other parameters in pneumatic systems. It operates by leveraging pneumatic relays and amplifiers to manage control actions, often featuring components such as bellows, resistances, actuating error signals, orifices, and flappers. This device is characterized by a proportional relationship between input and output pressures, which is crucial for maintaining the desired operational conditions. Pneumatic controllers are particularly useful in environments where electrical devices are unsuitable, utilizing pressurized air as a power source. They often incorporate feedback mechanisms to adjust sensitivity and control, ensuring precise management of pressure and flow within industrial processes. Although primarily pneumatic, some controllers integrate electronic components, such as sensors and actuators, to regulate airflow based on input signals, enhancing their functionality and adaptability. Pneumatic controllers are depicted in schematic diagrams that illustrate their operation and effectiveness in controlling pressure and motion within a system.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-344b982779dae543f1e2fa4c92c88748&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4e3f6409d4b23a0e16d4df95cc418876&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9b847793aceb37d63da174b89e67a501&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e29b944093645b10134bb068a0e85609&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-243f218034c813f63a13349f4953ce1b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-db868c08afc68de9d8850f06c963dfd1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7291b615cce3723bc1941e91175ccf27&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9ba0b5a3e032183793285284f3603fd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e5ae34faa3613c5b2f380b5bfb27f630</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Hydraulic Controller">
  <data key="d0">Hydraulic Controller</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">A hydraulic controller uses pressurized fluid, typically oil, as a power source, suitable for applications requiring high force or torque.&lt;SEP&gt;Hydraulic controllers are devices used to manage and control the operation of hydraulic systems, including various types such as integral, proportional, and jet-pipe controllers.&lt;SEP&gt;The hydraulic controller is a system component that acts as a proportional controller, with its gain adjustable by changing the lever ratio.&lt;SEP&gt;The hydraulic controller processes the input from the fly-ball governor and adjusts the power piston to control engine speed.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-45a3caf311e2ab85a8b8795fbe5c9365&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d427a9d59bf3e814fd05720d1b33d0f8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7291b615cce3723bc1941e91175ccf27&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-da45ed46b3417493dc6e2a6d545bd8ed</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Electronic Controller">
  <data key="d0">Electronic Controller</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">An electronic controller uses electrical power to perform control actions, commonly used in modern industrial applications for precision and reliability.&lt;SEP&gt;Electronic Controller is a device that uses electronic circuits to manage and control system operations.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-faf6d229bd33d9a677ddb2c4f3fe8552&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7291b615cce3723bc1941e91175ccf27</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Reference Input">
  <data key="d0">Reference Input</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">A reference input is provided by an error measuring device in a control system.&lt;SEP&gt;Reference Input is the desired value or setpoint that the control system aims to achieve.&lt;SEP&gt;Reference input is the desired target or setpoint for the control system, where PID control generally provides faster response compared to I-PD control.&lt;SEP&gt;Reference input is the desired value in a control system that the automatic controller uses to compare against the actual output.&lt;SEP&gt;The desired output of the system, set to zero in this case, representing the target value for the control system.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4b00b4278d0056b00df089ee6c703432&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-421dc885fedffcbdd1023734df76f5f1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7291b615cce3723bc1941e91175ccf27&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9a954f327a9c49380980f7f1e64531f7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5743b50920c06e8fe8331392a198cbcc</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Actuating Error Signal">
  <data key="d0">Actuating Error Signal</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">An actuating error signal is the difference between the reference input and the actual output, used by controllers to adjust system performance.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7291b615cce3723bc1941e91175ccf27</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Feedback Path">
  <data key="d0">Feedback Path</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The feedback path in a control system is the route through which sensor data is relayed back to the controller for performance adjustments.&lt;SEP&gt;The feedback path is a configuration in control systems where disturbances enter the system after the main control process.&lt;SEP&gt;The feedback path is a loop in the control system that returns the output signal to the input to regulate the system's behavior.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-3c317b9ff2b2110c0c0b14f7d7fbc89c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-64543aa197d39924b6592a9f9b8937d3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7291b615cce3723bc1941e91175ccf27</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Differential Gap">
  <data key="d0">Differential Gap</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">A differential gap is the range through which the actuating error signal must move before switching occurs in a two-position controller.&lt;SEP&gt;Differential Gap refers to the difference between two values or states in a system.&lt;SEP&gt;The differential gap represents the difference between actual and desired values in a control system, illustrated as a rectangular box with an arrow pointing towards it.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-faf6d229bd33d9a677ddb2c4f3fe8552&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-79898654d152629166799ec58407db89&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7291b615cce3723bc1941e91175ccf27</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Adder (Summing Junction)">
  <data key="d0">Adder (Summing Junction)</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The adder or summing junction is a component in the control system that calculates the total input to the differential gap, represented by a circle with an arrow.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-79898654d152629166799ec58407db89</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Transfer Function (G(s))">
  <data key="d0">Transfer Function (G(s))</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Mathematical representation of the relationship between the input and output of a system in the Laplace domain.&lt;SEP&gt;The transfer function G(s) is a mathematical representation of the system's output behavior in response to input changes, depicted as a rectangle.&lt;SEP&gt;The transfer function G(s) represents the relationship between the input and output of a system in the Laplace domain.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-ccfe5606d87942d95cd99b0924a1abcd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fab839ec7d08f56111abaab896a33188&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-79898654d152629166799ec58407db89</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Electromagnetic Valve">
  <data key="d0">Electromagnetic Valve</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Electromagnetic Valve is a valve operated by electromagnetic forces, used for controlling fluid flow.&lt;SEP&gt;The Electromagnetic Valve is part of the fluid dynamics system, shown in the technical diagrams.&lt;SEP&gt;The electromagnetic valve controls the inflow rate in a liquid-level control system, operating in two positions: open or closed.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-faf6d229bd33d9a677ddb2c4f3fe8552&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-79898654d152629166799ec58407db89&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ff388ffb7bf75847e1985f1473658eaf</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Liquid-Level Control System">
  <data key="d0">Liquid-Level Control System</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">A liquid-level control system manages the inflow and outflow rates to maintain a steady-state head using a hydraulic controller.&lt;SEP&gt;A system that uses an electromagnetic valve to control the inflow rate, causing the output signal to oscillate between two limits.&lt;SEP&gt;The Liquid-Level Control System is depicted in technical diagrams, representing fluid dynamics and thermodynamics.&lt;SEP&gt;The liquid-level control system, shown in Figure 4–43, uses a proportional controller with a fixed set point.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-fdb46bdfea03c8ad1daae623cf71c4a1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-12881ab3d33126ccd266da1257ad2563&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-79898654d152629166799ec58407db89&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ff388ffb7bf75847e1985f1473658eaf</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Error Signal (e)">
  <data key="d0">Error Signal (e)</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The error signal is an input to the adder and differential gap, representing the difference between actual and desired values.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-79898654d152629166799ec58407db89</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Controlled Variable (y)">
  <data key="d0">Controlled Variable (y)</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The controlled variable is the output of the transfer function, representing the result of the control system's operations.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-79898654d152629166799ec58407db89</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Input Signal (u)">
  <data key="d0">Input Signal (u)</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The input signal is fed into the differential gap and represents the error signal processed by the system.&lt;SEP&gt;The input signal, denoted as 'u', is a key variable in the control system equations influencing the state variables.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-2602a3aebfee4edc08475d0264ce7134&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-79898654d152629166799ec58407db89</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Exponential Curves">
  <data key="d0">Exponential Curves</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The exponential curves represent the filling and emptying processes in the liquid-level control system.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-79898654d152629166799ec58407db89</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Movable Iron Core">
  <data key="d0">Movable Iron Core</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The Movable Iron Core is a component of a mechanical device depicted in a cross-sectional diagram, connected to a magnetic coil.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-ff388ffb7bf75847e1985f1473658eaf</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Magnetic Coil">
  <data key="d0">Magnetic Coil</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The Magnetic Coil is attached to the Movable Iron Core and is a key component in the mechanical device illustrated.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-ff388ffb7bf75847e1985f1473658eaf</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Signal Flow Diagram">
  <data key="d0">Signal Flow Diagram</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The Signal Flow Diagram illustrates the flow of signals through a control system, with blocks representing specific operations.&lt;SEP&gt;The Signal Flow Diagram visually represents the relationships between system variables and operations through blocks and arrows.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-204365918a7a13528bbf287196b97d31&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ff388ffb7bf75847e1985f1473658eaf</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Disturbance D(s)">
  <data key="d0">Disturbance D(s)</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">An external disturbance affecting the system's dynamics.&lt;SEP&gt;Disturbance D(s) represents a noise or interference signal affecting the system, located at the top-left corner of the block diagram.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c85e4e0b258fa41e4bb98a0ed524e05c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-35ca799f31090a72592882ce4794e5f5</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="H(s)">
  <data key="d0">H(s)</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">H(s) is a transfer function in control systems, often representing feedback elements in a system.&lt;SEP&gt;H(s) is an element in the internal feedback loop of control systems, used to modify loop characteristics.&lt;SEP&gt;H(s) is the transfer function of the controlled plant or process in a control system, representing how inputs affect the system's behavior and output.&lt;SEP&gt;H(s) represents a system or component that further processes the signal, located at the bottom-right corner of the block diagram.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c85e4e0b258fa41e4bb98a0ed524e05c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fd104ba0a5d1c1ab6f6f3b08f2715be8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e67cb8477adc7c0ef97482e4e7be7fa4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-76bc75a542f1bbffc63059af2a43bbeb</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="C_D(s)">
  <data key="d0">C_D(s)</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">C_D(s) represents the response of the system to the disturbance input D(s).</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c85e4e0b258fa41e4bb98a0ed524e05c</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="C_R(s)">
  <data key="d0">C_R(s)</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">C_R(s) represents the response of the system to the reference input R(s).</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c85e4e0b258fa41e4bb98a0ed524e05c</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Unity Feedback">
  <data key="d0">Unity Feedback</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">A feedback mechanism where the feedback signal is equal to the output, often used to equalize input and output.&lt;SEP&gt;Unity Feedback refers to a control system configuration where the feedback loop has a gain of one, simplifying stability analysis.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f59f8623e4ff8e64d49175daeeadad05&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ce82309cc277894effb1a9e9bc5be0e3</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="R C Circuit">
  <data key="d0">R C Circuit</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">A basic electrical circuit consisting of resistors (R) and capacitors (C), used to illustrate dynamic behavior and block diagram representations.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-ce82309cc277894effb1a9e9bc5be0e3</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Summing Operation">
  <data key="d0">Summing Operation</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">An operation in circuit analysis where multiple inputs are combined into a single output, represented in block diagrams.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-ce82309cc277894effb1a9e9bc5be0e3</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Feedback Loop">
  <data key="d0">Feedback Loop</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">A Feedback Loop is a critical system configuration in control systems where part of the output is fed back into the input to influence and adjust the overall system behavior and stability. This mechanism allows for continuous monitoring and adjustment, ensuring desired system performance and stability. The feedback loop is a structural element that connects the output signal back to the input signal, enabling self-regulation and correction. It often involves components such as K/s, T, and Gc1(s), and is used to maintain control and stability by allowing the controller to monitor the output and adjust its input accordingly. This closed-loop system is essential for regulating system behavior, particularly in scenarios where the output of a plant is fed back to a PID controller to calculate error and adjust control signals. In essence, a feedback loop facilitates a cycle of continuous correction and adjustment, making it a fundamental component in maintaining the stability and performance of control systems.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f1897ad76eeb7398a9874d0d8978aa7c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-219f7fb5973694e4ed3fc49b9c5d4c60&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ccfe5606d87942d95cd99b0924a1abcd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5743b50920c06e8fe8331392a198cbcc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-69b1697f44d2725bfd2f580c242ea9b0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b987c9f4905e5b5d570e0dc620016e58&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7fae43bfb1918de812ae799078e161e9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a12a1fcc8b96ef105600adda81cf5b8f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0690050aa436f47134092f69ec8ed18b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0b0975c8819fd43e77406652cac9bd23&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c0dd560e406cc5769efa44c166402303&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-03cc52015d502a70a9646315437485f3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5f3d1f90ecef2ea0c231698609f06418&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cd437ba37843beb72e13fcc99049d49c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fc700758bd11889bde17fb78bf15e8bf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-115c47567f4cb2d5b0df6b61937e6281&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9cff8b4398a7527756d649d91c3b5802&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9a954f327a9c49380980f7f1e64531f7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ce82309cc277894effb1a9e9bc5be0e3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9571f0c498fec38011c9f30cf7ab85ab&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f86521265de1eaaef568da798d2e3a53</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Circuit Diagrams">
  <data key="d0">Circuit Diagrams</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Circuit Diagrams visually represent the configuration and connections of components like resistors and capacitors in the PID controller.&lt;SEP&gt;Visual representations of electrical circuits, each featuring components like resistors, capacitors, and inductors.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-491a839e54fff3c9e325de4c696089cb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ce82309cc277894effb1a9e9bc5be0e3</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
