</node>
<node id="Conventional Control Theory">
  <data key="d0">Conventional Control Theory</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Conventional Control Theory involves traditional methods of analyzing and designing control systems.&lt;SEP&gt;Conventional Control Theory is applicable to linear time-invariant single-input, single-output systems, using a complex frequency-domain approach.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-faf6d229bd33d9a677ddb2c4f3fe8552&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ed88cc0500a21b898e5a196a843b2781</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="State">
  <data key="d0">State</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">State refers to the smallest set of variables that, along with the input, completely determines the behavior of a dynamic system over time.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-ed88cc0500a21b898e5a196a843b2781</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="State Space Analysis">
  <data key="d0">State Space Analysis</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">State Space Analysis is a method used in modern control theory to describe systems using state variables, state vector, and state space.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-ed88cc0500a21b898e5a196a843b2781</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
