<node id="State Variables">
  <data key="d0">State Variables</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">State Variables are integral components of a dynamic system, serving as the smallest set of variables that determine the system's state. They are essential for representing the state of a system, providing crucial information for control, estimation, and prediction of future states based on initial conditions and inputs. State Variables are typically part of the system's equations, often defined as x1, x2, x3, and x4, and represent various aspects such as angle, angular velocity, cart position, and cart velocity in systems like the inverted pendulum or mechanical systems. These variables can be transformed into different sets while conveying the same information, and they are used in state-space representation, which is vital for understanding system dynamics. In control theory, State Variables are outputs of integrators in the system equations, representing the system's state and are involved in mathematical equations involving derivatives and coefficients. They are elements that need to be estimated or measured for effective control and are used in block diagrams for system analysis.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5b187039cce28a12c36cf813e9f926ff&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6f805ff2e429256924500b3bb528e1bb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ed88cc0500a21b898e5a196a843b2781&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-34c379ec40e0f14314e81b71389bf1c6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2602a3aebfee4edc08475d0264ce7134&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ac56253b9f22aee18032b4cf99f1d27d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6cb2119ae5def40cdccd4f628a1d279a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e6e564eb7fea62b35d7d86dfce4ed165&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-22085333984fa377791f298c557d58ea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-80377cf40599b7bb019354876db314d5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-681185e57d810ae79c2ee826ac8ba784&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0f3bd095a4a20703f7118bb28e95675a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-70007355d6d14c2b8730b0480830d394&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-caeb6d047b3cd8348a555d68841dee56&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-37b28f4da7cad971f45737e82572ff87&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c0dd560e406cc5769efa44c166402303&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dfe09b7b703c389e8f2522a6967742f4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-925099acac18f050f91f2ea8c404ad69&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a5b38ba15c11c9500daafd6a0fa68942&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a273a3722c026cffba1fca5be00c72f3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cd465393991808ab3570984282218782&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3ad73b7beb45180631dd72fbab2b6c9b</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Modeling in State Space">
  <data key="d0">Modeling in State Space</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Modeling in State Space involves analyzing control systems using state space methods and state variables.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-ed88cc0500a21b898e5a196a843b2781</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Complex Systems">
  <data key="d0">Complex Systems</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Complex Systems refer to systems with multiple inputs and outputs, often requiring modern control theory for analysis.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-ed88cc0500a21b898e5a196a843b2781</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="State Vector">
  <data key="d0">State Vector</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The State Vector is a mathematical representation that encapsulates all necessary information to describe the current status of a system. It is a collection of variables, often referred to as state variables, that uniquely determine the system's state at any given time. The State Vector is typically represented as an n-dimensional vector, denoted as \( \mathbf{X} \) or \( x \), and plays a crucial role in the analysis of dynamic systems and control systems. It is used in mathematical modeling to represent the state of a system in state space, and serves as an estimated representation of the plant's state, which is used as input for the controller. The State Vector is essential for analyzing system dynamics in various systems, such as System S1 and System S2, and is particularly important in control system analysis, where it describes the state of the plant in a servo system.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-70007355d6d14c2b8730b0480830d394&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e5be935af21c311ce3f7db40fa58876e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fab839ec7d08f56111abaab896a33188&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3402b465fcfb648982e49c37a455c189&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8a707fa18fd529fe3c1718e4570e7abb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-31df32fb8d3f0302ba80571341b33840&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-87f3dae9453dc00aeaa24b0a25b4f9e5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c5f8bc3750c711a5de6e8037c4fda4c0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8258faffcaea77860b8003c251cb0674&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b773a713efcb75c181833b78c35c4f74&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ba9e6dce047704f9bd794f5f4ed1e2a7</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="State-Space Equations">
  <data key="d0">State-Space Equations</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Equations derived to describe the dynamics of the inverted pendulum system.&lt;SEP&gt;State-space equations describe the system in terms of state variables and input-output relationships.&lt;SEP&gt;State-space equations involve input, output, and state variables, providing a mathematical representation of dynamic systems.&lt;SEP&gt;State-space equations represent a mathematical model of a physical system, defining the plant's dynamics in terms of matrices A, B, C, and D.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-70007355d6d14c2b8730b0480830d394&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c0dd560e406cc5769efa44c166402303&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b31537ba5725a3819547235d3a68c08e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8ec8d588e92dca4c7eedd0f19d3797cb</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Integrators">
  <data key="d0">Integrators</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Integrators are elements in a control system that serve as memory devices, with their outputs considered as state variables.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-70007355d6d14c2b8730b0480830d394</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Dynamic System">
  <data key="d0">Dynamic System</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">A dynamic system is a system characterized by change, often described using state variables and state-space models.&lt;SEP&gt;A system being modeled by the unit-acceleration response function, showing efficient energy transfer with some damping or resistance.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-6cf775bf4a61982df0debfac0b4f0b51&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-70007355d6d14c2b8730b0480830d394</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Input Variables">
  <data key="d0">Input Variables</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Input variables are external variables that influence the state of a dynamic system, often denoted as u(t).&lt;SEP&gt;Input variables like u and y are used in the flowchart to represent the starting points of the mathematical operations.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-70007355d6d14c2b8730b0480830d394&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cf54b3d3fa835e379c76e3ccf4ecfa3f</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Output Variables">
  <data key="d0">Output Variables</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Output Variables are measurable components of the system's output vector, potentially affected by noise.&lt;SEP&gt;Output variables are the measurable results of a system that can be observed directly.&lt;SEP&gt;Output variables are the responses of a system, often influenced by both state and input variables, denoted as y(t).</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-70007355d6d14c2b8730b0480830d394&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ac56253b9f22aee18032b4cf99f1d27d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-caeb6d047b3cd8348a555d68841dee56</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Complex System">
  <data key="d0">Complex System</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The text describes a complex system involving interconnected components and processes, represented by blocks and arrows indicating information flow.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-11af566a12a0ac730b5036ce4fb24d37</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Block A">
  <data key="d0">Block A</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Block A is one of the components in the system, involved in the flow of information.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-11af566a12a0ac730b5036ce4fb24d37</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Block B">
  <data key="d0">Block B</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Block B is another component in the system, participating in the information exchange.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-11af566a12a0ac730b5036ce4fb24d37</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Block C">
  <data key="d0">Block C</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Block C is part of the system, contributing to the signal flow.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-11af566a12a0ac730b5036ce4fb24d37</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Block D">
  <data key="d0">Block D</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Block D is a component in the system, engaged in the interconnected processes.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-11af566a12a0ac730b5036ce4fb24d37</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Block X">
  <data key="d0">Block X</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Block X is one of the components in the system, involved in the flow of information.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-11af566a12a0ac730b5036ce4fb24d37</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="State Equation">
  <data key="d0">State Equation</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The State Equation is a mathematical representation that describes the dynamics and behavior of a system in terms of state variables and input. It is often used in state-space representation and involves differential equations and matrix operations. The State Equation defines the relationship between the state vector, control input, and system matrices, specifically involving matrices A and B, and the control signal u(t). This formulation provides a comprehensive depiction of the system's state dynamics through matrix equations, capturing the intricate interactions within a dynamical system.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7c284789e81baac7fc644cb784a374af&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0e2680709d2b529eb353b1601aca0714&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7901b85c41f86e4c8c10a0b273e9c765&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-11af566a12a0ac730b5036ce4fb24d37&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3402b465fcfb648982e49c37a455c189&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-12121eebc019284d55e0c84cd019271d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0abcb038b73f1c1825b1810df74969ca&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6aa727c1ad6dddc07c40ad930aae27a5</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Output Equation">
  <data key="d0">Output Equation</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Mathematical representation defining the output variable y in terms of state variables and input u.&lt;SEP&gt;Output equations relate the state variables of a system to its outputs, often used in control systems.&lt;SEP&gt;The Output Equation relates the output vector y to the state vector x using matrix C.&lt;SEP&gt;The output equation describes the system's output, involving functions and matrices.&lt;SEP&gt;The output equation is part of the state-space representation that defines the relationship between state variables and the system's output.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7c284789e81baac7fc644cb784a374af&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7901b85c41f86e4c8c10a0b273e9c765&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-11af566a12a0ac730b5036ce4fb24d37&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b900da580f82b4cfa8b1797c2b38f7d9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-80377cf40599b7bb019354876db314d5</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Time-Varying System">
  <data key="d0">Time-Varying System</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">A time-varying system is one where vector functions involve time explicitly.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-11af566a12a0ac730b5036ce4fb24d37</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Linearized State Equation">
  <data key="d0">Linearized State Equation</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The linearized state equation is a simplified version of the state equation about the operating state.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-11af566a12a0ac730b5036ce4fb24d37</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Linearized Output Equation">
  <data key="d0">Linearized Output Equation</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The linearized output equation is a simplified version of the output equation about the operating state.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-11af566a12a0ac730b5036ce4fb24d37</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="State Matrix">
  <data key="d0">State Matrix</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">State Matrix is a mathematical representation used in state-space analysis for control systems.&lt;SEP&gt;The State Matrix is part of the state equation, representing the system's dynamics in matrix form.&lt;SEP&gt;The state matrix is part of the linearized state equation, affecting the system's dynamics.&lt;SEP&gt;The state matrix represents the coefficients of the system's differential equations, defining the dynamics of the system.&lt;SEP&gt;The state matrix, denoted as AA, is part of the system's mathematical representation, defining the dynamics of the system's state variables.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-11af566a12a0ac730b5036ce4fb24d37&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-76155cfacd298ba99866d06587bc0e7d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-50a65ab288a27fc3df2630882da9d54d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c2db63f41e22678b776f42091570392a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-361321c070809524321c98fac3cc3b3b</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Input Matrix">
  <data key="d0">Input Matrix</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The input matrix is part of the linearized state equation, influencing the system's response to inputs.&lt;SEP&gt;The input matrix represents how the inputs affect the state variables of the system.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c2db63f41e22678b776f42091570392a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-11af566a12a0ac730b5036ce4fb24d37</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Output Matrix">
  <data key="d0">Output Matrix</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The output matrix is part of the linearized output equation, determining the system's output.&lt;SEP&gt;The output matrix represents how the states are transformed into outputs.&lt;SEP&gt;The output matrix, denoted as CC, determines how the state variables affect the system's output.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c2db63f41e22678b776f42091570392a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-11af566a12a0ac730b5036ce4fb24d37&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-361321c070809524321c98fac3cc3b3b</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Direct Transmission Matrix">
  <data key="d0">Direct Transmission Matrix</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Direct Transmission Matrix is a matrix used to describe the direct relationship between input and output in control systems.&lt;SEP&gt;The direct transmission matrix is part of the linearized output equation, affecting direct signal transmission.&lt;SEP&gt;The direct transmission matrix, denoted as DD, represents direct paths from input to output in the system.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-faf6d229bd33d9a677ddb2c4f3fe8552&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-11af566a12a0ac730b5036ce4fb24d37&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-361321c070809524321c98fac3cc3b3b</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Linear System">
  <data key="d0">Linear System</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">A linear system refers to a system where the principle of superposition applies, meaning the output is directly proportional to the input.&lt;SEP&gt;Linear System is a mathematical model of a system based on linear equations, where the principle of superposition applies.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-232100dc2d77888cd2276dfa3e1c28a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a34c150b7357f195267cedd77a6ab328</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Time-Invariant System">
  <data key="d0">Time-Invariant System</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">A time-invariant system is one where the system's behavior and characteristics do not change over time.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-232100dc2d77888cd2276dfa3e1c28a0</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Spring-Mass System">
  <data key="d0">Spring-Mass System</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The Spring-Mass System is a fundamental concept in physics and engineering, serving as a mechanical model to analyze the dynamics of systems involving masses and springs. It consists of a mass attached to a spring, which exhibits oscillatory motion when displaced. This system is utilized to study and model phenomena such as vibrations, oscillations, harmonic motion, and waves. The analysis of a spring-mass system often involves examining force-time and displacement-time graphs to understand the behavior of the mass under various forces and elastic constraints. Additionally, it can include damping elements to further explore dynamic characteristics under different loads. The system's specific spring constants play a crucial role in determining its behavior and response to external influences.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-232100dc2d77888cd2276dfa3e1c28a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dd181394db0cba7b325f62f7a3a1029a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3b5c28d651a853ceed5a2bb812c9fd2d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a99de50670eed69da300e8009547bd36&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-204404dff5633144a45a65a52892839a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bde48fe7be57bee4bb18f23aaf9bf6a0</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Mechanical System">
  <data key="d0">Mechanical System</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The Mechanical System is a dynamic setup involving various physical components used to analyze motion, forces, and interactions within the system. It primarily consists of two masses, labeled 'm1' and 'm2', which are connected by springs and dampers (or dashpots). These components are subjected to external forces, such as 'u', to illustrate mechanical networks and analyze force and displacement. The system is often depicted in diagrams, such as those in Figures 3–22, 3–23(a), and 5–31, which show the configuration and connections, including gears and levers. This system is used as an example to demonstrate the application of state-space equations and transfer functions, with its behavior described by state equations involving variables like position and velocity. These equations are represented in mathematical form and through block diagrams, highlighting signal processing and transformation within the system. The Mechanical System serves as a valuable tool for illustrating the dynamics and interactions of mechanical components in various scenarios.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f104d09d0c472d244729f4c0273b66e0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-232100dc2d77888cd2276dfa3e1c28a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fab839ec7d08f56111abaab896a33188&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c7d78a410e7ab8ccfaa475dbd51f6e95&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dd181394db0cba7b325f62f7a3a1029a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-34c379ec40e0f14314e81b71389bf1c6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-23de25452ffebaa5122b237d5ec13605&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fd87696d65a75079f4edb623b979b62f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3b5c28d651a853ceed5a2bb812c9fd2d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7d61c2c5328f39dc98d3d82a63c5fe2b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7ea08bc01558a1369597e1539971f894&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0c92f84562a769fed69ae650fcaf4187&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9ba0b5a3e032183793285284f3603fd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8ec8d588e92dca4c7eedd0f19d3797cb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7b84f68967e01f789185103a6da424fa</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Equation (2-14)">
  <data key="d0">Equation (2-14)</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Equation (2-14) is the state equation for a linear, time-invariant system, describing the system's internal state dynamics.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-232100dc2d77888cd2276dfa3e1c28a0</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Equation (2-15)">
  <data key="d0">Equation (2-15)</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Equation (2-15) is the output equation for a linear, time-invariant system, describing the relationship between input and output.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-232100dc2d77888cd2276dfa3e1c28a0</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
