  <data key="d2">Figure 2-14 is a block diagram representation of a linear, continuous-time control system in state space.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-232100dc2d77888cd2276dfa3e1c28a0</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Figure 2-15">
  <data key="d0">Figure 2-15</data>
  <data key="d1">event</data>
  <data key="d2">Figure 2-15 illustrates a mechanical system, specifically a spring-mass system, used to derive system equations.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-232100dc2d77888cd2276dfa3e1c28a0</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Signal Processing">
  <data key="d0">Signal Processing</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Signal Processing is a field that involves the analysis, interpretation, and manipulation of signals, which can include sound, images, and scientific measurements. It is often used in engineering and technology to optimize filter designs and enhance signal quality. The techniques employed in signal processing include analyzing, modifying, and synthesizing signals, frequently utilizing mathematical concepts such as Fourier transforms and the complex plane. These methods are depicted in diagrams and equations, illustrating the mathematical foundations of the field. Transformations such as filtering, modulation analysis, and amplification are commonly applied to improve and refine signal quality.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b2a20c61ffd29ee123aa4008d1f4ba5f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-98b7da91ec0504b2cb24aec37c61430a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-76bc75a542f1bbffc63059af2a43bbeb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-232100dc2d77888cd2276dfa3e1c28a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9e97cb703344a8d83dd7f1085591043d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-958b33ac0865635b15e9b137a06a79e4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-021d406fb1875a04e6ca85d6a4589897&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a273a3722c026cffba1fca5be00c72f3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ac56253b9f22aee18032b4cf99f1d27d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0f3bd095a4a20703f7118bb28e95675a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a6b0fb37f85a26f35ea31ded16be6773&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ab41d39cf3a8cfab5c7e12464cab8a94&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8a2cf2ba37e76669ac29da1b6aa8f4af</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Control System">
  <data key="d0">Control System</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">A Control System is a sophisticated mechanism or process that manages, commands, directs, or regulates the behavior of other devices or systems through the use of control loops. It typically involves a configuration of interconnected components, including a controller, plant, and often a sensor, operating in a feedback loop to maintain desired performance and stability. Control systems are designed to regulate outputs to achieve desired behaviors, often employing feedback mechanisms and transfer functions. These systems can be described and analyzed through block diagrams, root-locus plots, and mathematical equations, which illustrate the interactions between components like input signals, gain blocks, and output signals. Control systems are used in various applications, such as regulating the temperature in heat exchangers, managing aircraft altitude, and ensuring dynamic responses and stability in engineering and signal processing. They may involve complex arrangements of physical components, software, or algorithms, and often include proportional, integral, and derivative control actions to optimize performance in response to changes in input signals.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0948b1e428ea01364b1b1ec1955431f0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6a256259f60bf17c11f960427ffa11ff&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f1897ad76eeb7398a9874d0d8978aa7c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-026e0fcd969517dd30ca84cdf6678f77&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ccfe5606d87942d95cd99b0924a1abcd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6a6c1f27816ba00765df34580fd4352b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bc420a4e236b18e1114725fd8e9d12b5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0f3bd095a4a20703f7118bb28e95675a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f220f834d3bf211bf1917f262c17e60d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-486351ecd11a42798affd79b7f5d143b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bde48fe7be57bee4bb18f23aaf9bf6a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9838df333da6616701c1cce66c00e732&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-45d58fa0ad7e2ced2585b4512c2f59e7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-62f88176acf0b93c66f5a8735ba14f56&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-adb718308239fee157c153fdd63d6ea6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-181fee5061e5d9adf0f04d196cc15e7f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-570cd2cd2a847eac2402e00924491700&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-32e837730a9d05b1df592f0ae66e9fc2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4178d8ac5083f4ba940096cd490ec0cf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7d424f9b5c8e1c4caa0b22f889469da1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c0b45e8c05ce6d6a2e33cb4a65bc30d4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-72d535e84ba337e6877808174fd78fb5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5cf6791e215ed7abe6fbda5eedbb50b9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b467c546fd593046d07eac1e9826b33c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ed23283c207553099a8ea220b94e1005&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9b6f2069d9a69517d279f1786974d9e9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-51ed07bf7b1b56e1246deb3b4a2fd5cb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-35ca799f31090a72592882ce4794e5f5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7bd9d9a9ccff6dcc7aa39644785f5940&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0b0975c8819fd43e77406652cac9bd23&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c90e64ef4d3ea69c9b12410204a70bfb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-03cc52015d502a70a9646315437485f3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-232100dc2d77888cd2276dfa3e1c28a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f1e2f536a2499c393f143367bb8b5da9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cd566e31ec5118dde48a4f149d4c5469&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a273a3722c026cffba1fca5be00c72f3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5d0a99065421970bde5dae1689b5a3b1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0eb8913e939d945dc7d0ee9f203204fb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cd576c10a0963ba5c33eef163a461a22&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-577f4c0582663d9533ba9e75104e8038&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9b87b5f3d20e81642454297b93cc16f3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fd21bbb7bb0f22b9146dce0bacde7f33&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3c0ec79f7d7ca60c30e8465fa9c98468&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a8712fa7ddd9dc034d222eaf660d62f1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2afbc3d7bb9c389990910a284d3b9dfd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7947832f5a2e447102e9db53f42dc041&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-64543aa197d39924b6592a9f9b8937d3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-51b0d24e65c00006971d0aaf3046f316&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0690050aa436f47134092f69ec8ed18b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ff7353abf978480820979c540d814ee3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0279e47752e3eff4e78f68ce4e1c721f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1c56a057e86a61de468030b87f550fa4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d30d89b485b27ebd673bf1d2c57c24a7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3dac0ceabd7b52fc29d821c18da7fcb1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1a8d984a98f532ef69747b8e72c4a5bd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cd437ba37843beb72e13fcc99049d49c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fc700758bd11889bde17fb78bf15e8bf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f0cf05f458c91801bb85a25015c81170&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f87ef3fd6a4c1856d15e4ad169e98183&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f104d09d0c472d244729f4c0273b66e0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b1abf1bd63b798f8e59025a047459778&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-95a8d8a1b3cfb0710c46f13ae2e3b48e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-db115ced573d3098e7cb02b4ddf8625f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8639c1dbc2042e150feddfc5fe82ffe8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-586f97239ba09b5129d50fc14028adbc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bc4f091219e1ee158ce8e0f022f44c42&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7fae43bfb1918de812ae799078e161e9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a12a1fcc8b96ef105600adda81cf5b8f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8258faffcaea77860b8003c251cb0674&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cf299ff5c8d29fc06d76cf7459e022b6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-191e311819ac0dda5c8069a7a8cfa1fd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ef73554446b2eed91c76e39c989b498d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-241ff02c460b748c0a8d1135d536c107&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e7d38d679f58a32db379c91feb378361&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dce32ac491099b3bb3f7e2e4697b0721&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ac56debe5e5db6d5809231139242ca61&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-731aadaa135cc2ef1c9482aab829f4e1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-30758f9c11d60c2ad7dbc4a84ff615d7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9571f0c498fec38011c9f30cf7ab85ab&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-80115d2c91ecc61916cf6b117778b14b</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Damping Device">
  <data key="d0">Damping Device</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">A damping device is a component that absorbs energy in a mechanical system, reducing oscillations and stabilizing motion.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-232100dc2d77888cd2276dfa3e1c28a0</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Blocks A, B, C, D, X, Y">
  <data key="d0">Blocks A, B, C, D, X, Y</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">These blocks represent distinct components or processes in a system, each with inputs and outputs for signal processing.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-232100dc2d77888cd2276dfa3e1c28a0</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Mathematical Operators">
  <data key="d0">Mathematical Operators</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Mathematical operators such as addition, subtraction, multiplication, and division indicate transformations applied to signals.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-232100dc2d77888cd2276dfa3e1c28a0</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Vector Functions">
  <data key="d0">Vector Functions</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Vector functions are mathematical expressions that describe the relationships between vectors in a system.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-232100dc2d77888cd2276dfa3e1c28a0</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="External Force">
  <data key="d0">External Force</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">A force applied to the inverted pendulum system, causing motion.&lt;SEP&gt;External force refers to an input applied to a mechanical system, affecting its motion and behavior.&lt;SEP&gt;External force refers to any force applied to a system from outside, affecting the system's motion and behavior.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c0dd560e406cc5769efa44c166402303&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-39704bca2c3f08c736c74b4257605c0a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-232100dc2d77888cd2276dfa3e1c28a0</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Displacement">
  <data key="d0">Displacement</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Displacement is measured from the equilibrium position of the mass and is represented by an arrow pointing to the right.&lt;SEP&gt;Displacement is the change in position of a mass within a mechanical system, measured from a reference point.&lt;SEP&gt;The distance the mass moves from its equilibrium position, influencing the restoring force.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-23de25452ffebaa5122b237d5ec13605&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2281e4b3e341841f3ab4fc1ff9c7a634&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-232100dc2d77888cd2276dfa3e1c28a0</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Mass (m)">
  <data key="d0">Mass (m)</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Mass (m) is a fundamental component in various mechanical systems, including the simple harmonic oscillator, where it represents the object that oscillates. As a key parameter in the equations of motion, mass contributes significantly to the system's inertia and dynamics, influencing how the system responds to forces. In the context of a harmonic oscillator, mass affects the frequency of oscillations, often represented by a square symbol. Additionally, mass is related to the quantity of matter in a substance, impacting thermal capacitance. Overall, mass plays a crucial role in determining the behavior and characteristics of mechanical systems.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-232100dc2d77888cd2276dfa3e1c28a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-284988f822bbc861f6935dafb7bde2c7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3f800baebc1ea569d8d6199edc2fa8f3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-201ad2bff594c69ddcdebbbde0310588&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f0cf05f458c91801bb85a25015c81170&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-683d619a6a6debc5b41f0a5860aa0746</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Spring (k)">
  <data key="d0">Spring (k)</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">A spring is a mechanical element that stores elastic potential energy and exerts force to maintain equilibrium.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-232100dc2d77888cd2276dfa3e1c28a0</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Equilibrium Position">
  <data key="d0">Equilibrium Position</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Equilibrium Position is where the net force acting on the mass is zero, with the spring neither stretched nor compressed.&lt;SEP&gt;Equilibrium position is the state where the forces in a system are balanced, resulting in no net motion.&lt;SEP&gt;The equilibrium position is the point at which the mass in the harmonic oscillator system would be if not displaced, serving as the reference point for oscillations.&lt;SEP&gt;The position where the mass is at rest, and the forces are balanced in the simple harmonic oscillator.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-23de25452ffebaa5122b237d5ec13605&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2281e4b3e341841f3ab4fc1ff9c7a634&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-232100dc2d77888cd2276dfa3e1c28a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-284988f822bbc861f6935dafb7bde2c7</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Elastic Potential Energy">
  <data key="d0">Elastic Potential Energy</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Elastic potential energy is stored energy in a spring when it is stretched or compressed.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-232100dc2d77888cd2276dfa3e1c28a0</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Fixed Support">
  <data key="d0">Fixed Support</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Fixed support is a structural element that prevents movement, providing stability to a mechanical system.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-232100dc2d77888cd2276dfa3e1c28a0</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Integrator">
  <data key="d0">Integrator</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The Integrator is a crucial component in control systems that performs the mathematical integration of an input signal, effectively accumulating this signal over time. This accumulation is often represented by the term 1/s in transfer functions. Integrators are commonly used to produce smoother outputs, reduce steady-state errors, and improve the steady-state performance of systems. By providing a nonzero output even with zero error, integrators help cancel disturbance torque and are integral in modeling dynamic systems. In signal processing systems, integrators affect the system's output by processing the input signal. They can be configured using operational amplifiers to produce a voltage output proportional to the integral of the input. While integrators enhance system performance, they may also introduce stability issues when added to the feedforward path of a system.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5f3d1f90ecef2ea0c231698609f06418&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0279e47752e3eff4e78f68ce4e1c721f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-232100dc2d77888cd2276dfa3e1c28a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1c56a057e86a61de468030b87f550fa4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9b87b5f3d20e81642454297b93cc16f3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9b6ab57eb994b3e68768778e5ab127ef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fc700758bd11889bde17fb78bf15e8bf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-920fc0a9384e851f4a7726a4a68c6299&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5cf6791e215ed7abe6fbda5eedbb50b9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5bc3dd38e993fe4446dce1bb2dde017f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4be38bff19f25f3543f1f0ce50c6b6b6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0f3bd095a4a20703f7118bb28e95675a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5027e7ae655fabb2069b339ee5f89f71</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Input Signal">
  <data key="d0">Input Signal</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The Input Signal is a crucial component in control systems, serving as the initial signal that undergoes various processing steps. It is fed into the system through an input port labeled 'R(s)', which represents the reference or setpoint that the controller aims to track. This signal influences the behavior of the entire system, including models such as the space vehicle model. In some contexts, the input signal is also denoted as 'u' or 'u(t)', signifying the control input to the system and representing the desired behavior over time. As the initial data or command introduced into the control system, the Input Signal plays a vital role in initiating the system's response, particularly when processed through components like a PID controller.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-36e714a094e63aef48fac634eeddad6c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6a256259f60bf17c11f960427ffa11ff&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7cb24308be90f4de9c5775f0a3a25ce9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c90a28b31b0ff2333ffc79bc30b633af&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bb690227c0aeb29078796de6d1611d85&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a12a1fcc8b96ef105600adda81cf5b8f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c0dd560e406cc5769efa44c166402303&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ff7353abf978480820979c540d814ee3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8ec8d588e92dca4c7eedd0f19d3797cb</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Integration Component">
  <data key="d0">Integration Component</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The integration component performs integration on the input signal as part of the signal processing sequence.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-8ec8d588e92dca4c7eedd0f19d3797cb</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Scaling and Feedback">
  <data key="d0">Scaling and Feedback</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Scaling and feedback operations are performed on the integrated signal, creating a feedback loop within the system.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-8ec8d588e92dca4c7eedd0f19d3797cb</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Final Processing">
  <data key="d0">Final Processing</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Final processing involves multiplying the signal with b/m, resulting in the output 'x2'.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-8ec8d588e92dca4c7eedd0f19d3797cb</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Input">
  <data key="d0">Input</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">In control systems, the input is the signal that enters the system, often denoted as 'R(s)' in diagrams.&lt;SEP&gt;The input, denoted as u, is a variable that influences the system's behavior and response.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-fab839ec7d08f56111abaab896a33188&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a7067d3378c6320f2be99583ea919091</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Output">
  <data key="d0">Output</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The Output is the final component in the control system, providing the processed signal result.&lt;SEP&gt;The Output is the final result produced by the control system after processing input signals through various operations.&lt;SEP&gt;The output in a control system is the response of the system to the input and control action, often denoted as 'C(s)'.&lt;SEP&gt;The output, denoted as y, is the result or response of the system based on the input and state vector.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9b87b5f3d20e81642454297b93cc16f3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-95a8d8a1b3cfb0710c46f13ae2e3b48e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fab839ec7d08f56111abaab896a33188&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a7067d3378c6320f2be99583ea919091</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Characteristic Polynomial">
  <data key="d0">Characteristic Polynomial</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The Characteristic Polynomial is a mathematical expression associated with a matrix, obtained from the determinant of the matrix minus a scalar times the identity matrix. It plays a crucial role in determining the properties of the matrix, particularly its eigenvalues, which are the roots of the polynomial. This polynomial is invariant under matrix similarity, meaning it remains unchanged when the matrix undergoes similarity transformations. In control systems, the characteristic polynomial, often denoted as φ(s), is utilized to define desired eigenvalues for state observers, thereby influencing system behavior and stability. Additionally, it helps in identifying the poles of the transfer function, which are essential for system analysis. The characteristic polynomial is defined by specific coefficients, which describe the system's properties and contribute to understanding the matrix's behavior and dynamics.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-37b218740e6a890184b54e5af8f87969&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-af4a694419897c9fd7aca9d74f8703e5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a587ec6eec2d51c833758323b1607ad7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0ae81e09d61ed423567970de31eac0a6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fab839ec7d08f56111abaab896a33188&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-faf6d229bd33d9a677ddb2c4f3fe8552&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f56557021ba0f275fa8f2409761b1c6a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9be306b73353043a81456867d4cbe302&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-020147c9c5bc8be22c285fdcc01a4a4c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ba9e6dce047704f9bd794f5f4ed1e2a7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-008977814adc708a3eb952aa138c8696&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6ae57c718178f5bab0ff5576b682cfbf</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Eigenvalues">
  <data key="d0">Eigenvalues</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Eigenvalues are characteristic values of a matrix that provide critical insights into system stability and dynamics. They are scalar values associated with a matrix, often denoted as λ1, λ2, etc., and play a crucial role in defining the behavior of a matrix in linear algebra. These values remain invariant under linear transformations and are essential for understanding system behavior. In control systems, eigenvalues determine the stability and speed of response, influencing the state-transition matrix, especially when they are distinct or have multiplicities. They characterize the behavior of a system matrix, particularly in relation to the poles of the transfer function, and are vital in stability analysis and designing state observers. Additionally, eigenvalues are used in determining the diagonalization of a matrix and can be controlled by state feedback if the system is completely state controllable. In summary, eigenvalues are indispensable parameters that define the characteristic equation of a system, affecting its stability, response, and controllability.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5e25465698e9728fefd91973fb2caccd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e863c1a50016793179faa1e0b91cb9df&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c7b0a8292525cb7105c4e499daefad79&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e37fd32e67cfb4b06fcec5633146e195&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1a7e6ee6eefc1562c87e6317d173028b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ad1dc64fc9b2da439067495b6c1bf166&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8288442d2392d765b02cbc791d1b5378&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9c788b304947ce9ba784353e01e43778&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0ae81e09d61ed423567970de31eac0a6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4f413378cdd2cae9c05a01ccb66eba96&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-292c92c504fb43e76a57c3f50454fcbe&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-be8a262c59d580a999bcba6b1211d485&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0abcb038b73f1c1825b1810df74969ca&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c5e9c5e918d686131ecabed119aa3d7f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2ebe8a3128b2b3aabe653cb149142a6e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ed9a9bbc29b199320adff0a750dad8d5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-37b28f4da7cad971f45737e82572ff87&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fab839ec7d08f56111abaab896a33188&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a4e4ced27e2780342dd0d29aa11c621b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6aa727c1ad6dddc07c40ad930aae27a5</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="State Vector (\mathbf{X})">
  <data key="d0">State Vector (\mathbf{X})</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Represents the state of the system in mathematical modeling, used in equations to describe system dynamics.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-fab839ec7d08f56111abaab896a33188</data>
