<node id="Matrix C">
  <data key="d0">Matrix C</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Matrix C is a critical component in various mathematical and control system applications, particularly within state-space representation. It is primarily used as an output matrix that defines the relationship between the system's state and its output. In linear systems, Matrix C maps the state vector to the output vector, playing a key role in the system equations and influencing the output dynamics. It is involved in the output equation of the system, affecting how the system states impact the outputs.

Matrix C is also used in observer design, related to measurement or feedback, and is part of the full-order observer output equations to relate state variables to outputs. In MATLAB programs, Matrix C can be defined as a 2x2 identity matrix, often used in matrix operations, or as a resultant matrix from calculations involving matrices A and B. It is involved in systems like the inverted-pendulum system and is used in controllability analysis to represent output dynamics.

Additionally, Matrix C is used in System S1 to determine the system's output based on state vectors and is part of the system's output representation, contributing to the calculation of Matrix P. Overall, Matrix C is a versatile matrix that is integral to understanding and controlling the output behavior in various system models.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-45d58fa0ad7e2ced2585b4512c2f59e7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f2bf8d3735dca25672b127e3b08be5b7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5e25465698e9728fefd91973fb2caccd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6f805ff2e429256924500b3bb528e1bb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e87750a7956fffd621ae63f70162b51a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e37fd32e67cfb4b06fcec5633146e195&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f4f9bb3635034fef2739f071dc807bf7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-27adbbe8c3daf01de8b63ffa252de1e6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e036712f5b53112186f8d26bf732a1a5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-040d64011f55ec07f471f024c2b1be29&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c002c8164af0baaf62d1b67662dbbda4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c5c7e0dc112b305ed237d6ecd3031c05&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0f3c134e307adcb9587e648683f4f4b0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0279e47752e3eff4e78f68ce4e1c721f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7abd7647d540dcfe486743c3fa15b49c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-be8a262c59d580a999bcba6b1211d485&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-44fa931a2a4a572fbb711636672bfbd1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6ece149736a8793b07c50dccb1028cee&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3402b465fcfb648982e49c37a455c189&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8a707fa18fd529fe3c1718e4570e7abb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8fb07569a4fb35322f960ef1bd2a2a89&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f55b04c74c20ff1394ec57c53c60a408&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ba9e6dce047704f9bd794f5f4ed1e2a7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d64c265c70a4b363e906e1040d781b2b</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Matrix D">
  <data key="d0">Matrix D</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Matrix D is a 2x2 zero matrix defined in the MATLAB program, primarily used for computational analysis. It is obtained from calculations involving matrices A and B. Within the context of a state-space representation in MATLAB, Matrix D plays a crucial role by defining direct transmission paths from inputs to outputs. It represents the direct feedthrough matrix, illustrating the direct relationship between inputs and outputs in a state-space model. Matrix D influences the output based on the control input and is integral to the linear system equations, representing the input effects on the output vector.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-45d58fa0ad7e2ced2585b4512c2f59e7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0279e47752e3eff4e78f68ce4e1c721f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f2bf8d3735dca25672b127e3b08be5b7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3402b465fcfb648982e49c37a455c189&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c002c8164af0baaf62d1b67662dbbda4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ba9e6dce047704f9bd794f5f4ed1e2a7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d64c265c70a4b363e906e1040d781b2b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0f3c134e307adcb9587e648683f4f4b0</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
