  <data key="d0">Viscous-Friction Coefficient (b1, b2)</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Viscous-friction coefficients b1 and b2 represent the damping characteristics of individual dampers in a mechanical system.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4af75b278219735803bd2d9c41d781d9</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Displacement (x, y, z)">
  <data key="d0">Displacement (x, y, z)</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Displacement refers to the change in position of a point in a mechanical system, often used in equations to describe the motion of components.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4af75b278219735803bd2d9c41d781d9</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Spring-Mass-Dashpot System">
  <data key="d0">Spring-Mass-Dashpot System</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The Spring-Mass-Dashpot System is a mechanical model involving a spring, mass, and damper mounted on a massless cart, used to study translational motion.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-3f800baebc1ea569d8d6199edc2fa8f3</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Massless Cart">
  <data key="d0">Massless Cart</data>
  <data key="d1">geo</data>
  <data key="d2">The Massless Cart is part of the system, providing a platform for the spring and damper without contributing mass.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-3f800baebc1ea569d8d6199edc2fa8f3</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Spring">
  <data key="d0">Spring</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Spring is a versatile mechanical component depicted in various configurations across different systems. It is a coil-shaped element located at the top-left corner of an image, connected to a piston and cylinder. In an inverted pendulum system, Spring functions as a flexible rod, linking a mass to a fixed point. It is also described as an elastic component in a simple harmonic oscillator, storing potential energy when displaced. Spring operates based on Hooke's Law, exerting force represented by the constant 'k' in diagrams. It serves as a mechanical component used to store energy and provide force in mechanical systems, stretching from the top to the right side of a diagram, attached to a rigid support, and exerting a restoring force. In a spring-mass system, Spring connects the mass to a fixed point, offering tension and elasticity. Additionally, it is a linear elastic component situated between a cart and a wall, providing resistance proportional to displacement. Overall, Spring is integral to these systems, contributing stiffness and elastic force, facilitating energy storage, and ensuring mechanical stability.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-45a3caf311e2ab85a8b8795fbe5c9365&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-37fb7998dd9fe418c1b904b5fad6ac47&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2f9976290aaf5bc6b5c7806f43bcf303&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f104d09d0c472d244729f4c0273b66e0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3f800baebc1ea569d8d6199edc2fa8f3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2281e4b3e341841f3ab4fc1ff9c7a634&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dd181394db0cba7b325f62f7a3a1029a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-23de25452ffebaa5122b237d5ec13605&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c0dd560e406cc5769efa44c166402303</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Damper">
  <data key="d0">Damper</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Damper is a device used to reduce oscillations in a system by dissipating energy.&lt;SEP&gt;The Damper is a viscous friction component connected between the cart and the ground, providing resistance proportional to velocity.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-faf6d229bd33d9a677ddb2c4f3fe8552&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3f800baebc1ea569d8d6199edc2fa8f3</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="External Force (u)">
  <data key="d0">External Force (u)</data>
  <data key="d1">event</data>
  <data key="d2">The External Force, denoted as 'u', is an input to the system, pulling the cart along the horizontal surface.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-3f800baebc1ea569d8d6199edc2fa8f3</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Displacement (y)">
  <data key="d0">Displacement (y)</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Displacement 'y' refers to the movement of the mass relative to the ground in the system.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-3f800baebc1ea569d8d6199edc2fa8f3</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Acceleration (a)">
  <data key="d0">Acceleration (a)</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Acceleration 'a' is the rate of change of velocity of the mass in the system.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-3f800baebc1ea569d8d6199edc2fa8f3</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Friction Force">
  <data key="d0">Friction Force</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The Friction Force is proportional to the relative velocity difference in the damper component of the system.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-3f800baebc1ea569d8d6199edc2fa8f3</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Spring Force">
  <data key="d0">Spring Force</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Spring Force is the force exerted by the spring, calculated using Hooke's law.&lt;SEP&gt;Spring force is a force exerted by a spring on any object attached to it, contributing to the horizontal motion in mechanical systems.&lt;SEP&gt;Spring force is a mechanical force acting on the piston, balancing pressure differences.&lt;SEP&gt;The Spring Force is proportional to the displacement difference in the spring component of the system.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-683d619a6a6debc5b41f0a5860aa0746&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3f800baebc1ea569d8d6199edc2fa8f3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-39704bca2c3f08c736c74b4257605c0a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-da45ed46b3417493dc6e2a6d545bd8ed</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Horizontal Surface">
  <data key="d0">Horizontal Surface</data>
  <data key="d1">geo</data>
  <data key="d2">The horizontal surface is the plane along which the cart moves, influencing its motion dynamics.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-683d619a6a6debc5b41f0a5860aa0746</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Damper Force">
  <data key="d0">Damper Force</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Damper Force is the force exerted by the damper, described by Newton's second law for viscous fluids.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-683d619a6a6debc5b41f0a5860aa0746</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="External Force 'u'">
  <data key="d0">External Force 'u'</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">External Force 'u' is the force pulling the cart along the horizontal surface.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-683d619a6a6debc5b41f0a5860aa0746</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
