</node>
<node id="Center of Gravity">
  <data key="d0">Center of Gravity</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The center of gravity is a point representing the mean position of the mass in the pendulum rod, affecting its motion.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a7d35d4c0a78082736add717b2833c58</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Inverted-Pendulum System">
  <data key="d0">Inverted-Pendulum System</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The inverted-pendulum system is a dynamic mechanical system that serves as a classic problem in dynamics and control theory. It involves a pendulum that is balanced on its pivot point and is often mounted on a cart. This system is used to study and demonstrate stability and control challenges, requiring the analysis of interactions between horizontal and vertical motion components. It is commonly employed as a control system design problem to illustrate concepts in control theory and dynamics, with specific parameters such as mass and length playing a crucial role in its dynamic stability.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-2a3e624aa462dbb1e2c8fd4494630bd4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d427a9d59bf3e814fd05720d1b33d0f8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-39704bca2c3f08c736c74b4257605c0a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-201ad2bff594c69ddcdebbbde0310588&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-44fa931a2a4a572fbb711636672bfbd1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-361321c070809524321c98fac3cc3b3b</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Pendulum Bob">
  <data key="d0">Pendulum Bob</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">A small object attached to the end of a string or wire, free to swing back and forth in the pendulum system.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-2a3e624aa462dbb1e2c8fd4494630bd4</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="String/Wire">
  <data key="d0">String/Wire</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The flexible material connecting the pendulum bob to a fixed point, allowing it to swing.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-2a3e624aa462dbb1e2c8fd4494630bd4</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Fixed Point">
  <data key="d0">Fixed Point</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The anchor point from which the string or wire is suspended in the pendulum system.&lt;SEP&gt;The attachment point of the spring, serving as the reference for equilibrium in the simple harmonic oscillator.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-2a3e624aa462dbb1e2c8fd4494630bd4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2281e4b3e341841f3ab4fc1ff9c7a634</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Angle of Deflection">
  <data key="d0">Angle of Deflection</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The angle between the vertical line through the fixed point and the line connecting the fixed point to the pendulum bob.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-2a3e624aa462dbb1e2c8fd4494630bd4</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Tension">
  <data key="d0">Tension</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The force exerted by the string or wire on the pendulum bob, keeping it taut.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-2a3e624aa462dbb1e2c8fd4494630bd4</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Weight (mg)">
  <data key="d0">Weight (mg)</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The downward force acting on the pendulum bob due to gravity.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-2a3e624aa462dbb1e2c8fd4494630bd4</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Centripetal Force">
  <data key="d0">Centripetal Force</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The inward-directed force keeping the pendulum bob moving in a circular path.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-2a3e624aa462dbb1e2c8fd4494630bd4</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Inverted-Pendulum Plant">
  <data key="d0">Inverted-Pendulum Plant</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The inverted-pendulum plant is a system characterized by its open-loop instability, involving state variables and equations for modeling its dynamics.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-6cb2119ae5def40cdccd4f628a1d279a</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Electrical Circuit">
  <data key="d0">Electrical Circuit</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">An electrical circuit is a network comprising various electrical components connected together to perform specific functions. It typically involves components such as resistors, capacitors, and inductors, and is analyzed using principles like Kirchhoff’s laws. The circuit can be depicted in different configurations, including an RLC series circuit and a parallel connection of resistors and capacitors, which can be used to model systems like a thermometer. These circuits are depicted with wires and are used for various applications involving input and output voltage sources.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-12881ab3d33126ccd266da1257ad2563&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-23de25452ffebaa5122b237d5ec13605&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-27ec4c68f753870a17122f4f0f1865d3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6cb2119ae5def40cdccd4f628a1d279a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f02c1788b14b17fe251de4fbe29938db&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5df7e896b70c3eb7634619fc0cc39c79</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="LRC Circuit">
  <data key="d0">LRC Circuit</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The LRC circuit is an electrical circuit including inductance (L), resistance (R), and capacitance (C), modeled using Kirchhoff’s voltage law.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-6cb2119ae5def40cdccd4f628a1d279a</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Theta">
  <data key="d0">Theta</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Theta is a state variable indicating the rotation angle of the pendulum rod about point P.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-6cb2119ae5def40cdccd4f628a1d279a</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="X">
  <data key="d0">X</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">X is a state variable representing the location of the cart in the inverted-pendulum system.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-6cb2119ae5def40cdccd4f628a1d279a</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Kirchhoff's Current Law">
  <data key="d0">Kirchhoff's Current Law</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Kirchhoff's current law states that the algebraic sum of currents entering and leaving a node is zero, used in electrical circuit analysis.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-6cb2119ae5def40cdccd4f628a1d279a</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Kirchhoff's Voltage Law">
  <data key="d0">Kirchhoff's Voltage Law</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Kirchhoff's voltage law states that the algebraic sum of voltages around any loop in an electrical circuit is zero, essential for circuit modeling.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-6cb2119ae5def40cdccd4f628a1d279a</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Inductance">
  <data key="d0">Inductance</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Inductance (L) is a component in electrical circuits measured in henries, contributing to the circuit's dynamic behavior.&lt;SEP&gt;Inductance is a property of electrical components that store energy in a magnetic field when electrical current flows through it, measured in henries.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-6cb2119ae5def40cdccd4f628a1d279a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-27ec4c68f753870a17122f4f0f1865d3</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Resistance">
  <data key="d0">Resistance</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Resistance is a measure of the opposition to flow within various systems, influencing their dynamics and transfer functions. In electrical circuits, resistance (R) is a property of components that opposes the flow of electric current, typically measured in ohms. This characteristic affects the flow of current through the circuit. Similarly, in pressure systems, resistance refers to the opposition to gas flow, characterized by the change in pressure difference relative to the change in flow rate. Additionally, resistance is the opposition to fluid flow, defined as the level difference required for a unit change in flow rate. Overall, resistance plays a crucial role in determining the behavior and efficiency of systems by affecting how easily flow can occur.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-30f7b7fbdbec5ce9d814319c46f46972&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-da45ed46b3417493dc6e2a6d545bd8ed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-026e0fcd969517dd30ca84cdf6678f77&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-27ec4c68f753870a17122f4f0f1865d3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-449bed3c4cecf8b0bddf9a7ce769394b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6cb2119ae5def40cdccd4f628a1d279a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f6fde4856c7b2f76783cc1fa6680d81f</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Capacitance">
  <data key="d0">Capacitance</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Capacitance is a measure of a system's ability to store electrical energy or charge, commonly represented in control system diagrams. It is a fundamental property of electrical components, where it is measured in farads, and is crucial in various systems, including electrical circuits, pressure systems, thermal systems, and water tank systems. In electrical contexts, capacitance refers specifically to the ability to store energy in an electric field. In pressure systems, it describes the capacity to store gas, defined by the change in gas stored relative to the change in gas pressure. Overall, capacitance is a key component in understanding and designing systems that require energy storage.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-30f7b7fbdbec5ce9d814319c46f46972&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-026e0fcd969517dd30ca84cdf6678f77&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-27ec4c68f753870a17122f4f0f1865d3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-449bed3c4cecf8b0bddf9a7ce769394b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-faf6d229bd33d9a677ddb2c4f3fe8552&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6cb2119ae5def40cdccd4f628a1d279a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f6fde4856c7b2f76783cc1fa6680d81f</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
