<node id="Operational Amplifiers">
  <data key="d0">Operational Amplifiers</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Operational Amplifiers are electronic components used in the PID controller for signal processing and amplification.&lt;SEP&gt;Operational amplifiers are electronic components used in the design of the lag-lead compensator circuit.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-491a839e54fff3c9e325de4c696089cb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6b06f11e14613835822cf024d5217ccc</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Proportional Gain">
  <data key="d0">Proportional Gain</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Proportional Gain (Kp) is a parameter in the PID controller that determines the strength of the proportional control action.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-491a839e54fff3c9e325de4c696089cb</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Integral Time">
  <data key="d0">Integral Time</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Integral Time (Ti) is a parameter in the PID controller that defines the duration over which the integral control action accumulates.&lt;SEP&gt;Integral time is represented by the variable T_i in the equations, indicating a time constant in the system's dynamics.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-491a839e54fff3c9e325de4c696089cb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9ba0b5a3e032183793285284f3603fd</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Derivative Time">
  <data key="d0">Derivative Time</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Derivative Time (Td) is a parameter in the PID controller that specifies the response time for the derivative control action.&lt;SEP&gt;Derivative Time is the time constant associated with derivative control action.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-491a839e54fff3c9e325de4c696089cb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-faf6d229bd33d9a677ddb2c4f3fe8552</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Operational-Amplifier Circuits">
  <data key="d0">Operational-Amplifier Circuits</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Operational-amplifier circuits are used as controllers or compensators in various configurations such as non-inverting, inverting, differential, summing, integrator, differentiator, and sample-and-hold.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-920fc0a9384e851f4a7726a4a68c6299</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Non-Inverting Configuration">
  <data key="d0">Non-Inverting Configuration</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">A non-inverting configuration is a type of operational-amplifier setup where the input signal is applied to the non-inverting input, resulting in a positive gain.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-920fc0a9384e851f4a7726a4a68c6299</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Inverting Configuration">
  <data key="d0">Inverting Configuration</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">An inverting configuration is a type of operational-amplifier setup where the input signal is applied to the inverting input, resulting in a negative gain.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-920fc0a9384e851f4a7726a4a68c6299</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Summing Amplifier">
  <data key="d0">Summing Amplifier</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">A summing amplifier is an operational amplifier configuration that combines multiple input signals into a single output signal.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-920fc0a9384e851f4a7726a4a68c6299</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Differentiator">
  <data key="d0">Differentiator</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">A differentiator is an operational amplifier configuration that performs the mathematical differentiation of the input signal, producing a voltage output proportional to the rate of change of the input.&lt;SEP&gt;The Differentiator is a component in the control system that differentiates the integral of the input signal.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9b87b5f3d20e81642454297b93cc16f3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-920fc0a9384e851f4a7726a4a68c6299</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Sample-and-Hold Circuit">
  <data key="d0">Sample-and-Hold Circuit</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">A sample-and-hold circuit is an operational amplifier configuration that samples an input signal and holds its value for a period of time.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-920fc0a9384e851f4a7726a4a68c6299</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Automobile Suspension System">
  <data key="d0">Automobile Suspension System</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">An automobile suspension system is designed to absorb and dampen shock impulses from the road, consisting of both translational and rotational motions of the vehicle's center of mass.&lt;SEP&gt;The Automobile Suspension System is a mechanism designed to absorb road vibrations and maintain vehicle stability, consisting of springs, shocks, and dampers.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-48f6fb89274b6880175da471dd77be24&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-920fc0a9384e851f4a7726a4a68c6299</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Proportional Gain (Kp)">
  <data key="d0">Proportional Gain (Kp)</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Proportional Gain (Kp) adjusts the impact of the proportional term on the control action in a PID controller.&lt;SEP&gt;Proportional gain (Kp) is a parameter in control systems that dictates the response of the controller to the error signal.&lt;SEP&gt;Proportional gain (Kp) is a term used in control systems to adjust the proportional response of the system.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a4349e4239d3b82325e7a8cb18d1189c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-920fc0a9384e851f4a7726a4a68c6299&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5743b50920c06e8fe8331392a198cbcc</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Integral Time (Ti)">
  <data key="d0">Integral Time (Ti)</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Integral time (Ti) is a parameter in control systems used to eliminate steady-state error by integrating the error signal over time.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-920fc0a9384e851f4a7726a4a68c6299</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Derivative Time (Td)">
  <data key="d0">Derivative Time (Td)</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">A parameter in control systems that influences the derivative component's response time.&lt;SEP&gt;Derivative time (Td) is a parameter in control systems that predicts system behavior by considering the rate of change of the error signal.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c7a34174c65e8a6a1a11ac9ee67b0bbe&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-920fc0a9384e851f4a7726a4a68c6299</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Integral Gain (Ki)">
  <data key="d0">Integral Gain (Ki)</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Integral Gain (Ki) determines the rate at which the integral term contributes to the control action in a PID controller.&lt;SEP&gt;Integral gain (Ki) is a parameter in control systems that influences the accumulation of past error values to correct steady-state error.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-920fc0a9384e851f4a7726a4a68c6299&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5743b50920c06e8fe8331392a198cbcc</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Derivative Gain (Kd)">
  <data key="d0">Derivative Gain (Kd)</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Derivative Gain (Kd) controls the influence of the derivative term on the control action in a PID controller.&lt;SEP&gt;Derivative gain (Kd) is a parameter in control systems used to predict future system behavior by considering the rate of error change.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-920fc0a9384e851f4a7726a4a68c6299&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5743b50920c06e8fe8331392a198cbcc</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Control Action">
  <data key="d0">Control Action</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Control action refers to the mathematical expression that describes the behavior of an operational amplifier circuit in response to input signals.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-920fc0a9384e851f4a7726a4a68c6299</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Mathematical Equation">
  <data key="d0">Mathematical Equation</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">An equation involving variables Kc, s, T1, γ, and T2, representing relationships and operations within a control system.&lt;SEP&gt;Mathematical equation provides the formula used to calculate the output voltage in operational amplifier circuits based on input signals.&lt;SEP&gt;The Mathematical Equation describes the relationship between variables and is used to analyze system behavior in control theory.&lt;SEP&gt;The Mathematical Equation involves variables and operators to represent the system's dynamics and calculate various parameters.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-cf299ff5c8d29fc06d76cf7459e022b6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-51ed07bf7b1b56e1246deb3b4a2fd5cb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-920fc0a9384e851f4a7726a4a68c6299&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dce32ac491099b3bb3f7e2e4697b0721</data>
