  <data key="d2">Figure 3–35 is a diagram of a mechanical system used to derive transfer functions.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-201ad2bff594c69ddcdebbbde0310588</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Displacement (x)">
  <data key="d0">Displacement (x)</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Displacement x is the measure of deviation from the equilibrium position in a simple harmonic oscillator.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-201ad2bff594c69ddcdebbbde0310588</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Mass">
  <data key="d0">Mass</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The entity "Mass" is a critical component in both the spring-mass system and the inverted pendulum system. In these configurations, it is often labeled as 'm'. In the spring-mass system, the mass is connected to a spring, enabling it to move horizontally in response to forces applied through the spring, thus playing a key role in the simple harmonic oscillator model. It is typically depicted as a circle at the bottom of diagrams, symbolizing its function within the mechanical system. In the inverted pendulum system, the mass is also attached to a spring, influencing the dynamics of the system by allowing horizontal motion. Overall, "Mass" is essential for understanding the behavior and dynamics in these physical systems.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-2f9976290aaf5bc6b5c7806f43bcf303&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2281e4b3e341841f3ab4fc1ff9c7a634&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-45733531e78141284083da760089b754&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dd181394db0cba7b325f62f7a3a1029a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-23de25452ffebaa5122b237d5ec13605&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c0dd560e406cc5769efa44c166402303</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Restoring Force">
  <data key="d0">Restoring Force</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Restoring Force acts opposite to the direction of displacement, trying to return the mass to its equilibrium position.&lt;SEP&gt;The force exerted by the spring that causes the mass to return to the equilibrium position, driving oscillations.&lt;SEP&gt;The force exerted by the spring to return the mass to its equilibrium position.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-23de25452ffebaa5122b237d5ec13605&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2281e4b3e341841f3ab4fc1ff9c7a634&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-284988f822bbc861f6935dafb7bde2c7</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
