<node id="Absolute Stability">
  <data key="d0">Absolute Stability</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Absolute stability is the condition where a control system remains stable or returns to equilibrium after disturbances.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-eee931e9f23a29ab6271ed415dea0168</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Relative Stability">
  <data key="d0">Relative Stability</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Relative stability involves the degree to which a control system remains stable, often characterized by damped oscillations.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-eee931e9f23a29ab6271ed415dea0168</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Steady-State Error">
  <data key="d0">Steady-State Error</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Steady-State Error is the difference between the desired and actual output of a control system once it has reached equilibrium or steady state, as time approaches infinity. It serves as a measure of system accuracy and performance, indicating how well the system can track changes in its input over time. Lower steady-state error values suggest better system performance and stability. This error is influenced by the system type and input type, and it often persists in systems without an integrator in the feedforward path, known as an offset. Steady-state error can be calculated using the final-value theorem and is frequently minimized in system design to improve accuracy. Integral control can be employed to eliminate or reduce this error, ensuring the system's output better matches the desired input.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0948b1e428ea01364b1b1ec1955431f0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f7f67cd40a9e32be93dc80ac45982fc3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-620d81e2a5a2e80e3b426e6ed3a7d42b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cd437ba37843beb72e13fcc99049d49c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-241ff02c460b748c0a8d1135d536c107&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eee931e9f23a29ab6271ed415dea0168&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ac56debe5e5db6d5809231139242ca61&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5cf6791e215ed7abe6fbda5eedbb50b9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-39fb7e7ecf98024e40979eb23f0ae0a1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-76155cfacd298ba99866d06587bc0e7d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-56f12da94fdc89592ee91860dba06293&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5bc3dd38e993fe4446dce1bb2dde017f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a59bbd3f86b4923fd22b10788ad73d53&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3dac0ceabd7b52fc29d821c18da7fcb1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5706363dcd7b89678a580b4ab70a2f02</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="First-Order System">
  <data key="d0">First-Order System</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">First-order systems are characterized by a simple input-output relationship, often represented by an RC circuit or similar.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-eee931e9f23a29ab6271ed415dea0168</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Second-Order System">
  <data key="d0">Second-Order System</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">A second-order system is a dynamic system characterized by a second-order differential equation, which is often used in control systems and analyzed in control theory. It is defined by its closed-loop transfer function with two poles, and its behavior is typically examined through unit-step response curves and impulse responses. Key parameters of a second-order system include the damping ratio (ζ) and natural frequency (ω_n), which are essential for determining its rise time, peak time, maximum overshoot, and settling time. These systems are frequently used to analyze transient and frequency responses, focusing on their relative stability and damping properties. Second-order systems exhibit complex transient responses and are analyzed for various inputs such as step, ramp, and impulse, making them integral to the study of servo systems and other control applications.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-04598b6f66264ff6abc8a7ed77980955&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-af7ba33d6c623a912a3380defbe28756&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3b0fa6b31797f4f5fd2bb265e6ef2851&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eee931e9f23a29ab6271ed415dea0168&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c1b12ce7d358a5298c40691eca86f278&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1e045a2c6fa20bf1beb094683eabf565&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-535949f72f62ac84bb8daed630bc1653&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4a3189f7cb3fb2865b828460dc211a36&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-810cac8c012efc35656f6fae67fff21c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fd104ba0a5d1c1ab6f6f3b08f2715be8</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Higher-Order System">
  <data key="d0">Higher-Order System</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">A Higher-Order System is characterized by a transfer function with polynomials of degree greater than two, affecting its dynamic response.&lt;SEP&gt;A higher-order system is a complex system with multiple poles and zeros, affecting its response characteristics.&lt;SEP&gt;Higher-order systems have complex dynamics that may include additional zeros and poles, affecting the correlation between transient and frequency responses.&lt;SEP&gt;Higher-order systems have more complex dynamics and require detailed analysis for transient response.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-3b0fa6b31797f4f5fd2bb265e6ef2851&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eee931e9f23a29ab6271ed415dea0168&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2bf2afdc6808412e18506f82583774c6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-03cc52015d502a70a9646315437485f3</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Unit-Step Function">
  <data key="d0">Unit-Step Function</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">A unit-step function is a mathematical function used to test the response of control systems.&lt;SEP&gt;The unit-step function is a mathematical function used to model sudden changes in a system at a specific time.&lt;SEP&gt;The unit-step function is used in equations to model system responses.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d2f61e38a659adbd5279f14d8bc0af8f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eee931e9f23a29ab6271ed415dea0168&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1196f23376113f0a5fd1445c371198e0</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Unit-Ramp Function">
  <data key="d0">Unit-Ramp Function</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">A unit-ramp function is a mathematical function used to analyze system response to gradually changing inputs.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-eee931e9f23a29ab6271ed415dea0168</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Unit-Impulse Function">
  <data key="d0">Unit-Impulse Function</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">A unit-impulse function is used to test the response of systems subjected to shock inputs.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-eee931e9f23a29ab6271ed415dea0168</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
