  <data key="d0">Block Diagram (a)</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Block Diagram (a) represents a first-order system with components such as input block, summing junction, transfer function block, and output block.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-6970497cbdbb9aab39be9459aa607c48</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Block Diagram (b)">
  <data key="d0">Block Diagram (b)</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Block Diagram (b) represents a simplified block diagram with components including an input block, transfer function block, and output block.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-6970497cbdbb9aab39be9459aa607c48</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Exponential Response Curve">
  <data key="d0">Exponential Response Curve</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The Exponential Response Curve describes the behavior of the output c(t) over time, showing how it approaches unity with a specific time constant T.&lt;SEP&gt;The Exponential Response Curve is a mathematical concept describing how a system responds over time, characterized by a specific rate of change.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-6970497cbdbb9aab39be9459aa607c48&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f7f67cd40a9e32be93dc80ac45982fc3</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
