  <data key="d0">Equation (5–2)</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Equation (5–2) represents a mathematical expression involving Laplace transforms and is part of the analysis of a control system.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-6970497cbdbb9aab39be9459aa607c48</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Equation (5–3)">
  <data key="d0">Equation (5–3)</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Equation (5–3) describes the exponential response curve of a system, indicating how the output changes over time.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-6970497cbdbb9aab39be9459aa607c48</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Input Block (R(s))">
  <data key="d0">Input Block (R(s))</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The Input Block labeled R(s) represents the input signal or variable being processed by the system in both block diagrams.&lt;SEP&gt;The Input Block represents the input signal to the system in the block diagram.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-6970497cbdbb9aab39be9459aa607c48&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d6018e5e7fd6c2f5cfc4bd67745b544a</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Summing Junction">
  <data key="d0">Summing Junction</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The Summing Junction is a crucial component in control systems where multiple signals are combined into a single output signal. It is typically represented as a circle with a minus sign and is used to add or subtract input signals, effectively calculating the error signal. This junction plays a significant role in influencing the overall output of the system by combining reference inputs and control signals before further processing. Often depicted in block diagrams, the Summing Junction ensures that both input signals and feedback from the plant's output are integrated, serving as a pivotal point in managing the dynamics of the control system.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5f3d1f90ecef2ea0c231698609f06418&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-846bca86e18f84abac3699480d87b280&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f599210548e4a595268cbd4a1d4b5e3c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3c317b9ff2b2110c0c0b14f7d7fbc89c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-95a8d8a1b3cfb0710c46f13ae2e3b48e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6970497cbdbb9aab39be9459aa607c48&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5d0a99065421970bde5dae1689b5a3b1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-731aadaa135cc2ef1c9482aab829f4e1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9571f0c498fec38011c9f30cf7ab85ab</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Transfer Function Block (1/Ts)">
  <data key="d0">Transfer Function Block (1/Ts)</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The Transfer Function Block labeled 1/Ts represents a mathematical operation applied to the combined signal in Block Diagram (a).</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-6970497cbdbb9aab39be9459aa607c48</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Transfer Function Block (1/Ts + 1)">
  <data key="d0">Transfer Function Block (1/Ts + 1)</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The Transfer Function Block labeled 1/Ts + 1 represents a mathematical operation applied to the input signal in Block Diagram (b).</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-6970497cbdbb9aab39be9459aa607c48</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Output Block (C(s))">
  <data key="d0">Output Block (C(s))</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The Output Block labeled C(s) represents the output signal or variable resulting from the system processing in both block diagrams.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-6970497cbdbb9aab39be9459aa607c48</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Time Constant (T)">
  <data key="d0">Time Constant (T)</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The Time Constant (T) is a parameter in a filter's transfer function that affects the rate at which the system responds to changes in frequency.&lt;SEP&gt;The Time Constant T is a parameter in the exponential response curve that affects the speed of the system's response.&lt;SEP&gt;Time constant, denoted as T, is a constant characterizing the S-shaped response curve, influencing system dynamics.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a12a1fcc8b96ef105600adda81cf5b8f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6970497cbdbb9aab39be9459aa607c48&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4a53699f6a5efb94ddd426141d45cba8</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Time Constant T">
  <data key="d0">Time Constant T</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The Time Constant T is a parameter that influences the speed of response in a system, with smaller values indicating faster responses.&lt;SEP&gt;The time constant T is a parameter in the control system that affects the system's response time and dynamics.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-3c317b9ff2b2110c0c0b14f7d7fbc89c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f7f67cd40a9e32be93dc80ac45982fc3</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Unit-Ramp Response">
  <data key="d0">Unit-Ramp Response</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The Unit-Ramp Response is a concept in system dynamics that evaluates how a control system reacts to a ramp input over time. It is characterized by the system's output behavior, which typically shows a linear increase as the input continuously ramps up. This response is often depicted as a graph illustrating the transient characteristics and steady-state errors of the system, providing insights into its ability to track linear changes and maintain stability. The Unit-Ramp Response is used to assess and compare the performance of compensated and uncompensated systems, highlighting differences in how these systems handle gradual changes in input. By analyzing the Unit-Ramp Response, engineers can gain a deeper understanding of a system's dynamics, stability, and performance over time.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0948b1e428ea01364b1b1ec1955431f0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-96a98f812e32e184c15978c0d7771176&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c7d9a35f388915f791a96d8cdc32529b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ec1fe4f707d8920dbeac015fecfd158e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9d2b1279dd32f77fd27a121af92e7f90&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1b177904d9c062a54730d359827205e3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-61e97a29a080915ebe1313dbe1f7440e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4e42383167231f90ca804ba822f6b73d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-59498f3e851cf3c70c4395e945e7e809&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a2ddda49062cacf815e40ae9bc8167c7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b467c546fd593046d07eac1e9826b33c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4a3189f7cb3fb2865b828460dc211a36&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2bf2afdc6808412e18506f82583774c6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c3a55af272f2092fd9d053a8d89af4b4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8c39c97551687b0bf865de0bebf31277&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d6018e5e7fd6c2f5cfc4bd67745b544a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7eabccf8a49974a1b5a01ded1e337ca1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bb2cafb27b000379bc871ea7923a83dd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4e48cc17fab45352c6a28829079bd2b7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-896487ddef7a3f411006102c6c7a9b2b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-862057e40203dab14f5b486a9fcc1a5a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f7f67cd40a9e32be93dc80ac45982fc3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ac56debe5e5db6d5809231139242ca61&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-646ed44ca6a37b5351e0aef0dd18547a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-76155cfacd298ba99866d06587bc0e7d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bca6ad29eb261282c2df664425ef7d0b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ed1ff4c236c16a58ee408da4a88effc4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1196f23376113f0a5fd1445c371198e0</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Unit-Impulse Response">
  <data key="d0">Unit-Impulse Response</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The Unit-Impulse Response describes how a system reacts to an impulse input and is a crucial tool for characterizing system dynamics. It measures the system's reaction to a brief, instantaneous input signal, which is often used to analyze and understand the behavior of systems over time. In underdamped systems, this response can show oscillatory behavior. The Unit-Impulse Response is mathematically represented by the Laplace transform C(s) and is considered the derivative of the unit-step response, providing valuable insights into the system's dynamics.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-62f88176acf0b93c66f5a8735ba14f56&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f7f67cd40a9e32be93dc80ac45982fc3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5c20e37f9c7b9b4075574a4206786e9c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-76155cfacd298ba99866d06587bc0e7d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c1b12ce7d358a5298c40691eca86f278&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7dee9b6b4fa01e7b310d973887043bfe&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8f1e322d677e03984e2e1a0f412e6c1e</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Impulse Response">
  <data key="d0">Impulse Response</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Impulse Response is a graphical representation illustrating the relationship between input and output values over time, focusing on the system's response to changes.&lt;SEP&gt;Impulse response is a transient-response analysis method that evaluates a system's reaction to a brief input signal, typically resembling a delta function.&lt;SEP&gt;Impulse response is the reaction of a system to an external change, often analyzed using state-space equations.&lt;SEP&gt;The impulse response is a characteristic of a system that describes its reaction to a brief input signal, often used to analyze the behavior of filters.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-1e045a2c6fa20bf1beb094683eabf565&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e75920e90983d6045aaa3f10230d2c07&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c35c8384335c3eddd1b49dc9cad5f9cf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b495866d3d765a6bd3da8989d4656fc9</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Low-Pass Filter">
  <data key="d0">Low-Pass Filter</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">A Low-Pass Filter is an electronic filter that allows signals with a frequency lower than a certain cutoff frequency to pass through and attenuates signals with frequencies higher than the cutoff frequency.&lt;SEP&gt;A low-pass filter allows signals with frequencies lower than a certain cutoff frequency to pass through, while attenuating higher frequencies.&lt;SEP&gt;A low-pass filter is used in audio processing to remove high-frequency noise while preserving lower frequencies, characterized by an exponential decay.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-6b139b34ebbeaf26c5893fedc131b125&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1e045a2c6fa20bf1beb094683eabf565&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4a53699f6a5efb94ddd426141d45cba8</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Inertia">
  <data key="d0">Inertia</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Inertia is a load element in servo systems that affects the dynamics and response of the system.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-1e045a2c6fa20bf1beb094683eabf565</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Damping Ratio (ζ)">
  <data key="d0">Damping Ratio (ζ)</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The Damping Ratio (ζ) is a dimensionless parameter that plays a crucial role in describing how oscillations in a system decay after a disturbance, thereby influencing the system's stability and response characteristics. It affects various aspects of system behavior, including phase margin, overshoot in transient response, and the magnitude of resonant peaks. The damping ratio is integral to the system equation, influencing the shape of response curves and polar plots, particularly in signal processing. Its values, typically ranging from 0 to 1, determine the type of damping behavior exhibited by the system, such as underdamped, critically damped, or overdamped. In many systems, a damping ratio between 0.4 and 0.8 is considered desirable for optimal transient response. In the context of control systems, the damping ratio can be set to specific values, such as 0.5, to achieve desired stability and oscillatory behavior. Overall, the damping ratio is essential for understanding and predicting the dynamic performance of second-order systems and their frequency response characteristics.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-6cf775bf4a61982df0debfac0b4f0b51&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5518828c63927a29cbd23a5354674537&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f4c46665e037287407ddbff2463962aa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-af7ba33d6c623a912a3380defbe28756&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d293ccd91839b1545fb3a78b69f822a2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8639c1dbc2042e150feddfc5fe82ffe8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7d95cccfff7173da89105e8a30a753bb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a218a11cf9e9a1be2297492e35c21b7b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c1b12ce7d358a5298c40691eca86f278&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9e6b7d8c1849925b0d0f1ad9793983e9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-535949f72f62ac84bb8daed630bc1653&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a59bbd3f86b4923fd22b10788ad73d53&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-182dc59d89ae078fb7ffb41be064595c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ff7353abf978480820979c540d814ee3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fd104ba0a5d1c1ab6f6f3b08f2715be8</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Undamped Natural Frequency (ω_n)">
  <data key="d0">Undamped Natural Frequency (ω_n)</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The undamped natural frequency (ω_n) is the frequency at which a system oscillates when not subjected to damping.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-fd104ba0a5d1c1ab6f6f3b08f2715be8</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Attenuation (σ)">
  <data key="d0">Attenuation (σ)</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Attenuation (σ) refers to the reduction in amplitude of oscillations in a system, related to damping and natural frequency.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-fd104ba0a5d1c1ab6f6f3b08f2715be8</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Critically Damped System">
  <data key="d0">Critically Damped System</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">A critically damped system is one where the damping ratio is equal to one, resulting in the fastest return to equilibrium without oscillation.&lt;SEP&gt;Critically Damped System is a system where damping is optimized to prevent oscillations and reach equilibrium quickly.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-faf6d229bd33d9a677ddb2c4f3fe8552&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fd104ba0a5d1c1ab6f6f3b08f2715be8</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Underdamped System">
  <data key="d0">Underdamped System</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">An Underdamped System is a dynamic system where the unit-impulse response oscillates about zero, characterized by a damping ratio less than one.&lt;SEP&gt;An underdamped system exhibits oscillations with a transient response characterized by peaks and valleys before settling.&lt;SEP&gt;An underdamped system is characterized by a damping ratio less than one, leading to oscillatory transient responses.&lt;SEP&gt;Underdamped System is a system that exhibits oscillations before reaching a steady state.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-602e1bf386d85476513e06ee772f8a95&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fd104ba0a5d1c1ab6f6f3b08f2715be8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-76155cfacd298ba99866d06587bc0e7d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7dee9b6b4fa01e7b310d973887043bfe</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Overdamped System">
  <data key="d0">Overdamped System</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">An overdamped system displays a sluggish response with a longer settling time due to minimal oscillations.&lt;SEP&gt;An overdamped system has a damping ratio greater than one, causing slow return to equilibrium without oscillation.&lt;SEP&gt;An overdamped system is characterized by a damping ratio greater than unity, leading to a response without oscillations.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-602e1bf386d85476513e06ee772f8a95&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fd104ba0a5d1c1ab6f6f3b08f2715be8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-691177b7d933c82cade5a9fd3f182f4a</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Damped Natural Frequency">
  <data key="d0">Damped Natural Frequency</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Damped Natural Frequency is the frequency at which a damped system oscillates.&lt;SEP&gt;Damped Natural Frequency is the frequency at which a system oscillates when damping is present.&lt;SEP&gt;Damped natural frequency is the frequency observed in a system with damping, always lower than the undamped natural frequency.&lt;SEP&gt;The frequency of oscillation when damping is present in the system.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-63d648e6a3fb274cf615d8ac91567e7d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2281e4b3e341841f3ab4fc1ff9c7a634&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-faf6d229bd33d9a677ddb2c4f3fe8552&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5c123c891021bce024d99af35fd34585</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Damping Ratio">
  <data key="d0">Damping Ratio</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The Damping Ratio is a dimensionless parameter used in control systems to describe how oscillations in a system decay after a disturbance, impacting both system stability and oscillation frequency. It characterizes the oscillatory behavior of a system, influencing whether the system is underdamped, critically damped, or overdamped, and affects the resonant peak magnitude. In the context provided, the Damping Ratio is specified with values of 0.4, 0.5, and 0.491, indicating variations in how quickly oscillations diminish and the level of system response. Represented by the symbol ζ, the Damping Ratio plays a crucial role in determining the attenuation factor and stability of dynamic systems.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f104d09d0c472d244729f4c0273b66e0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-36e714a094e63aef48fac634eeddad6c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2281e4b3e341841f3ab4fc1ff9c7a634&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-181fee5061e5d9adf0f04d196cc15e7f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-602e1bf386d85476513e06ee772f8a95&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-810a0f1b2efa8cfcd9d3dfe3cd72ff62&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dd970bcee6ec8782e0e15281c48fc6f7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7f571134d683a0689226e17f5c8f12d2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1ef9a9b21f3821e990c650615c128744&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0b429aa524429e2d3a0bb88587574c34&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7dee9b6b4fa01e7b310d973887043bfe&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cba4dc46aaf3979ba1619c43d08d1720&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5c123c891021bce024d99af35fd34585&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8b59eec3640756dae81f5633965096e1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8c39c97551687b0bf865de0bebf31277&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0a41b322606e11a7a7d3758a0cae0e29&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4c51a48b42789ee6452aabf9684ca8e1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-241ff02c460b748c0a8d1135d536c107&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bb2cafb27b000379bc871ea7923a83dd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-faf6d229bd33d9a677ddb2c4f3fe8552&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2172726751d3bb09e7c6c3c9d2e73db9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-204404dff5633144a45a65a52892839a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8f1f8bc5cab7c0486a4991400b504ed8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-59a053a060eb4f14d77b34ae1061cace&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-862057e40203dab14f5b486a9fcc1a5a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-37c3fea424ed4692d82113ae9fd3f941&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a19f7091cfb7ee780a1c0268e833e55f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9571f0c498fec38011c9f30cf7ab85ab</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Transient Oscillation">
  <data key="d0">Transient Oscillation</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Transient Oscillation refers to the temporary oscillation occurring in a system before reaching steady state.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5c123c891021bce024d99af35fd34585</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Natural Frequency">
  <data key="d0">Natural Frequency</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Natural Frequency is a characteristic of oscillatory systems, related to the rate at which a system naturally oscillates.&lt;SEP&gt;Natural frequency is the frequency at which a system tends to oscillate in the absence of any driving or damping force.&lt;SEP&gt;Natural frequency refers to the frequency at which a system would oscillate if damping were absent.&lt;SEP&gt;Natural frequency, denoted as ωn, is a parameter likely representing angular velocity or frequency in the context of oscillatory systems.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-602e1bf386d85476513e06ee772f8a95&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7dee9b6b4fa01e7b310d973887043bfe&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-862057e40203dab14f5b486a9fcc1a5a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-63d648e6a3fb274cf615d8ac91567e7d</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Critically Damped">
  <data key="d0">Critically Damped</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Critically damped describes a system where damping is such that the system returns to equilibrium as quickly as possible without oscillating.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-63d648e6a3fb274cf615d8ac91567e7d</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Overdamped">
  <data key="d0">Overdamped</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Overdamped refers to a system with damping greater than critical, causing it to return to equilibrium slowly without oscillating.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-63d648e6a3fb274cf615d8ac91567e7d</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="c(t)">
  <data key="d0">c(t)</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">c(t) refers to the title of a graph representing functions or variables related to time response and unit-step response curves.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-04598b6f66264ff6abc8a7ed77980955</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Figure 5-7">
  <data key="d0">Figure 5-7</data>
  <data key="d1">event</data>
  <data key="d2">Figure 5-7 displays a family of unit-step response curves for a system with various values of damping ratio (ζ).&lt;SEP&gt;Figure 5-7 is a reference to a graph that depicts settling time in relation to damping ratio and tolerance band.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d293ccd91839b1545fb3a78b69f822a2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-04598b6f66264ff6abc8a7ed77980955</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Greek Letters (ζ)">
  <data key="d0">Greek Letters (ζ)</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Greek letters, particularly ζ, are used in the graph to represent damping ratio in the context of system response.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-04598b6f66264ff6abc8a7ed77980955</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Unit-Step Response Curves">
  <data key="d0">Unit-Step Response Curves</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The Unit-Step Response Curves are graphical representations that illustrate how a system responds to a unit-step input over time. These curves depict the transient response and dynamic behavior of a system, providing insight into control system performance. They are generated by MATLAB Program 5–25 and show variations in output for various damping ratios across four different cases.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-6cf775bf4a61982df0debfac0b4f0b51&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-51c9b9d9d69283b7b46094d2f229a5f2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5cb3f412352d8203e1efad181e8a8650&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bb0309faec8e535ff54384234330d2c8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-af7ba33d6c623a912a3380defbe28756&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-04598b6f66264ff6abc8a7ed77980955</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Transient-Response Specifications">
  <data key="d0">Transient-Response Specifications</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Transient-response specifications are parameters such as rise time, peak time, maximum overshoot, and settling time used to evaluate the performance of dynamic systems.&lt;SEP&gt;Transient-response specifications include delay time, rise time, peak time, maximum overshoot, and settling time, crucial for assessing system performance.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-8c63b00814925f096e3dbd5f7a444599&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-04598b6f66264ff6abc8a7ed77980955</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="C(s)/R(s)">
  <data key="d0">C(s)/R(s)</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">C(s)/R(s) is a transfer function representing the relationship between the output and input of a system in the Laplace domain.&lt;SEP&gt;C(s)/R(s) is a transfer function used to describe the relationship between the output and input of a system, often approximated for analysis.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4aecdf0555d89c0ebec0aa1b1b9980a6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-04598b6f66264ff6abc8a7ed77980955</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Unit-Step Response">
  <data key="d0">Unit-Step Response</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The Unit-Step Response is a crucial analysis tool in control systems engineering, used to evaluate how a system reacts to a sudden change in input, specifically a step input. This response is typically represented as a graph that illustrates the system's output behavior over time, offering insights into both transient and steady-state characteristics. By examining the Unit-Step Response, engineers can assess system dynamics, including stability, performance, settling time, overshoot, and amplitude changes. It is particularly useful for comparing the behavior of compensated and uncompensated systems, providing a clear visual representation of how the system reaches a steady state. The Unit-Step Response is often analyzed using software tools like MATLAB to further understand the system's dynamic characteristics and ensure optimal design and functionality.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0948b1e428ea01364b1b1ec1955431f0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-62f88176acf0b93c66f5a8735ba14f56&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-96a98f812e32e184c15978c0d7771176&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-04598b6f66264ff6abc8a7ed77980955&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2591937b2f7c7c5341658076669c1b02&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-87b0e0bfbf5774f7c90a99f131e0bb53&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c90a28b31b0ff2333ffc79bc30b633af&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7f99b64d34db6511197d521a024a4930&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ec1fe4f707d8920dbeac015fecfd158e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5ab66ab2ea102011daec41a066d3877c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8f1e322d677e03984e2e1a0f412e6c1e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9d2b1279dd32f77fd27a121af92e7f90&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-61e97a29a080915ebe1313dbe1f7440e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4e42383167231f90ca804ba822f6b73d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5c20e37f9c7b9b4075574a4206786e9c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6a6c1f27816ba00765df34580fd4352b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a2ddda49062cacf815e40ae9bc8167c7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7dee9b6b4fa01e7b310d973887043bfe&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b467c546fd593046d07eac1e9826b33c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8b59eec3640756dae81f5633965096e1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9b6f2069d9a69517d279f1786974d9e9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ccb516dd7fa038fc65484f2cc9669722&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2bf2afdc6808412e18506f82583774c6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c3a55af272f2092fd9d053a8d89af4b4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8c39c97551687b0bf865de0bebf31277&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-016ebe44f8f410b4e420081ccf7a65f9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f599210548e4a595268cbd4a1d4b5e3c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0a41b322606e11a7a7d3758a0cae0e29&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bb2cafb27b000379bc871ea7923a83dd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b55f24e9f9505c14159b689d1ef175f8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4e48cc17fab45352c6a28829079bd2b7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a59bbd3f86b4923fd22b10788ad73d53&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c90e64ef4d3ea69c9b12410204a70bfb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0ea98dac7f834a445156f9d25aec3156&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-896487ddef7a3f411006102c6c7a9b2b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-862057e40203dab14f5b486a9fcc1a5a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f1e2f536a2499c393f143367bb8b5da9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ac56debe5e5db6d5809231139242ca61&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-76155cfacd298ba99866d06587bc0e7d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cd465393991808ab3570984282218782&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ed1ff4c236c16a58ee408da4a88effc4</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Time Response c(t)">
  <data key="d0">Time Response c(t)</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Time response c(t) refers to the behavior of a system over time in response to a unit-step input.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-04598b6f66264ff6abc8a7ed77980955</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Figure 5-6">
  <data key="d0">Figure 5-6</data>
  <data key="d1">event</data>
  <data key="d2">An example system with specific parameters (ζ=0.6 and 2ω_n=5 rad/sec) used to calculate rise time, peak time, maximum overshoot, and settling time.&lt;SEP&gt;Figure 5-6 illustrates the second-order system used to derive the unit-impulse response.&lt;SEP&gt;Figure 5-6 is referenced as part of the system analysis showing unit-step response curves.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c1b12ce7d358a5298c40691eca86f278&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-810cac8c012efc35656f6fae67fff21c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-04598b6f66264ff6abc8a7ed77980955</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Figure 5-8">
  <data key="d0">Figure 5-8</data>
  <data key="d1">event</data>
  <data key="d2">Figure 5-8 graphically displays transient-response specifications such as delay time, rise time, and peak time.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-04598b6f66264ff6abc8a7ed77980955</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Delay Time (t_d)">
  <data key="d0">Delay Time (t_d)</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Delay time is the time required for a system's response to reach half of its final value.&lt;SEP&gt;Delay time is the time required for the response to reach half the final value for the first time in a control system.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a218a11cf9e9a1be2297492e35c21b7b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-04598b6f66264ff6abc8a7ed77980955</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Rise Time (t_r)">
  <data key="d0">Rise Time (t_r)</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Rise time is the duration it takes for a system's response to go from zero to its maximum value.&lt;SEP&gt;Rise time is the time taken for the response to rise from a specific percentage of its final value, commonly used in underdamped and overdamped systems.&lt;SEP&gt;The time taken for a system's response to rise from 0% to 100% of its final value, calculated using specific equations and parameters.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a218a11cf9e9a1be2297492e35c21b7b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-810cac8c012efc35656f6fae67fff21c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-04598b6f66264ff6abc8a7ed77980955</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Peak Time (t_p)">
  <data key="d0">Peak Time (t_p)</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Peak time is the time at which the system's response reaches its maximum value.&lt;SEP&gt;Peak time is the time required for the response to reach the first peak of the overshoot in a control system.&lt;SEP&gt;The time at which the system's response reaches its maximum peak value, determined by specific calculations.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a218a11cf9e9a1be2297492e35c21b7b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-810cac8c012efc35656f6fae67fff21c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-04598b6f66264ff6abc8a7ed77980955</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Maximum Overshoot (M_p)">
  <data key="d0">Maximum Overshoot (M_p)</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">A measure of how much the output of a system exceeds its final steady-state value, influenced by the damping ratio.&lt;SEP&gt;Maximum overshoot is the extent to which a system's response exceeds its final steady-state value.&lt;SEP&gt;Maximum overshoot is the maximum peak value of the response curve measured from unity, indicating the relative stability of the system.&lt;SEP&gt;The maximum peak amplitude of a system's response beyond its final value, expressed as a percentage and calculated using damping ratio and other parameters.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a218a11cf9e9a1be2297492e35c21b7b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-810cac8c012efc35656f6fae67fff21c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-caf32671a4ee19f2726367a3b3826926&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-04598b6f66264ff6abc8a7ed77980955</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Settling Time (t_s)">
  <data key="d0">Settling Time (t_s)</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Settling time is the time required for the response curve to remain within a specified range about the final value.&lt;SEP&gt;Settling time is the time taken for a system's response to stay within a certain percentage of the final value.&lt;SEP&gt;The time required for a system's response to remain within a certain percentage of its final value, calculated using specific criteria.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a218a11cf9e9a1be2297492e35c21b7b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-810cac8c012efc35656f6fae67fff21c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-04598b6f66264ff6abc8a7ed77980955</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Second-Order Systems">
  <data key="d0">Second-Order Systems</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Second-order systems are a class of control systems characterized by specific transient response behaviors, often described using parameters like damping ratio and natural frequency.&lt;SEP&gt;Second-order systems are characterized by their transient response and damping properties, often analyzed using transfer functions.&lt;SEP&gt;Second-order systems refer to a class of systems characterized by a second-order differential equation, often analyzed in control theory for their transient-response specifications.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a218a11cf9e9a1be2297492e35c21b7b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8c63b00814925f096e3dbd5f7a444599&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-04598b6f66264ff6abc8a7ed77980955</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Time-Domain Specifications">
  <data key="d0">Time-Domain Specifications</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Time-domain specifications include metrics such as delay time, rise time, peak time, maximum overshoot, and settling time, which are used to evaluate the performance of control systems.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a218a11cf9e9a1be2297492e35c21b7b</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Natural Frequency (ω_n)">
  <data key="d0">Natural Frequency (ω_n)</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Natural frequency is a parameter of a second-order system indicating the frequency at which the system will oscillate in the absence of damping.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a218a11cf9e9a1be2297492e35c21b7b</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Underdamped Systems">
  <data key="d0">Underdamped Systems</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Underdamped systems are characterized by oscillations in their transient response, often analyzed using parameters like rise time and overshoot.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a218a11cf9e9a1be2297492e35c21b7b</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Overdamped Systems">
  <data key="d0">Overdamped Systems</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Overdamped systems are characterized by a sluggish response without oscillations, often analyzed using parameters like rise time.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a218a11cf9e9a1be2297492e35c21b7b</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Equation (5–10)">
  <data key="d0">Equation (5–10)</data>
  <data key="d1">event</data>
  <data key="d2">Equation (5–10) is a specific mathematical expression used within the text to describe the behavior of second-order systems.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-8c63b00814925f096e3dbd5f7a444599</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Rise Time">
  <data key="d0">Rise Time</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Rise Time is a transient-response specification used to measure the time taken for a system's response to transition from a lower percentage to a higher percentage of its final value. Specifically, it is often defined as the time required for the response to rise from 10% to 90% of its final value. However, it can also be described as the duration needed for the response to increase from 0% to 100% of its final value during a step input. Additionally, Rise Time refers to the time taken for a system's response to go from zero to its peak value, and it is inversely related to bandwidth.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-8c63b00814925f096e3dbd5f7a444599&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-37fb7998dd9fe418c1b904b5fad6ac47&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-caf32671a4ee19f2726367a3b3826926&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-27923b259bf2de83650da5b28f76726e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9067de99754a18f08e6cfd3a76b28877&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a59bbd3f86b4923fd22b10788ad73d53&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f0ec5181a2b3e37816173bb15c763932&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9571f0c498fec38011c9f30cf7ab85ab</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Peak Time">
  <data key="d0">Peak Time</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Peak Time is a transient-response specification that refers to the time taken for a system's response to reach its maximum value during a transient response, particularly after a disturbance or a unit-step input. It is the moment at which the system's response achieves its maximum overshoot, and is crucial for determining the maximum amplitude of a signal during its transient response. Denoted as t_p, Peak Time corresponds to one-half cycle of the frequency of damped oscillation, playing a significant role in analyzing the system's dynamic behavior.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-8c63b00814925f096e3dbd5f7a444599&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-37fb7998dd9fe418c1b904b5fad6ac47&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9067de99754a18f08e6cfd3a76b28877&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-602e1bf386d85476513e06ee772f8a95&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a59bbd3f86b4923fd22b10788ad73d53&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f0ec5181a2b3e37816173bb15c763932&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9571f0c498fec38011c9f30cf7ab85ab&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8f1e322d677e03984e2e1a0f412e6c1e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f220f834d3bf211bf1917f262c17e60d</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Maximum Overshoot">
  <data key="d0">Maximum Overshoot</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Maximum Overshoot is a term used in control systems to describe the extent to which a system's response exceeds its final steady-state value during transient behavior. It is a key transient-response specification and is often represented as \(M_p\). This parameter is crucial in controller design, as it indicates the peak value that the system's output surpasses its target or steady-state value before eventually settling. Maximum Overshoot provides insight into the system's performance during a step input, helping to assess how well the system can handle changes and maintain stability.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-8c63b00814925f096e3dbd5f7a444599&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-37fb7998dd9fe418c1b904b5fad6ac47&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-baf80d031591815111410b79dbb30df9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9067de99754a18f08e6cfd3a76b28877&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-602e1bf386d85476513e06ee772f8a95&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a59bbd3f86b4923fd22b10788ad73d53&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-51b0d24e65c00006971d0aaf3046f316&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f0ec5181a2b3e37816173bb15c763932&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9571f0c498fec38011c9f30cf7ab85ab&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8f1e322d677e03984e2e1a0f412e6c1e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f220f834d3bf211bf1917f262c17e60d</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Settling Time">
  <data key="d0">Settling Time</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Settling Time is a critical measure in control system design, representing the duration required for a system's response to stabilize within a specified tolerance band around its final value after a disturbance or peak. It is often denoted as t_s and is used in both electronic and mechanical systems to evaluate performance. Settling Time is defined as the time taken for the system's transient response to decay and settle within a certain error margin or percentage of the final value, ensuring the system remains stable. This specification is a key performance criterion, as it indicates how quickly the system can achieve and maintain stability following a change or input. In this context, Settling Time is approximately 1.2 seconds, demonstrating the system's efficiency in reaching equilibrium.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-8c63b00814925f096e3dbd5f7a444599&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-37fb7998dd9fe418c1b904b5fad6ac47&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-caf32671a4ee19f2726367a3b3826926&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d293ccd91839b1545fb3a78b69f822a2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0a41b322606e11a7a7d3758a0cae0e29&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9067de99754a18f08e6cfd3a76b28877&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-602e1bf386d85476513e06ee772f8a95&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a59bbd3f86b4923fd22b10788ad73d53&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-51b0d24e65c00006971d0aaf3046f316&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f0ec5181a2b3e37816173bb15c763932&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aa017cb19dfa7211c0c2b6a589a80f57&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c90e64ef4d3ea69c9b12410204a70bfb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9571f0c498fec38011c9f30cf7ab85ab&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-191e311819ac0dda5c8069a7a8cfa1fd</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Zeta (ζ)">
  <data key="d0">Zeta (ζ)</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Zeta (ζ) is the damping ratio, a parameter that influences the transient-response characteristics of a second-order system.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-8c63b00814925f096e3dbd5f7a444599</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Natural Frequency (ωₙ)">
  <data key="d0">Natural Frequency (ωₙ)</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Natural frequency (ωₙ) is a parameter that defines the frequency at which a system oscillates when not subjected to damping.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-8c63b00814925f096e3dbd5f7a444599</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Damped Frequency (ωₐ)">
  <data key="d0">Damped Frequency (ωₐ)</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Damped frequency (ωₐ) is a parameter representing the frequency of oscillation in a damped second-order system.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-8c63b00814925f096e3dbd5f7a444599</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Sigma (σ)">
  <data key="d0">Sigma (σ)</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Sigma (σ) is a parameter related to the exponential decay rate in a second-order system's response.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-8c63b00814925f096e3dbd5f7a444599</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Figure 5–9">
  <data key="d0">Figure 5–9</data>
  <data key="d1">event</data>
  <data key="d2">Figure 5–9 is a diagram illustrating mathematical concepts related to transient-response specifications, including the definition of angle β.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-8c63b00814925f096e3dbd5f7a444599</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Exponential Decay Function">
  <data key="d0">Exponential Decay Function</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The exponential decay function e-ζωnt / √(1 - ζ^2) describes how the amplitude of oscillations decreases over time due to damping.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-602e1bf386d85476513e06ee772f8a95</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Envelope Curves">
  <data key="d0">Envelope Curves</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Envelope curves represent bounds within which the transient response of a system remains, defined by the curves 1 ± e-ζωnt / √(1 - ζ^2).</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-602e1bf386d85476513e06ee772f8a95</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Unit-Step Input">
  <data key="d0">Unit-Step Input</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The unit-step input is a type of input signal commonly used in control systems and system analysis to test and analyze system responses. Characterized by a sudden change in input value from zero to one, it is denoted as u(t) = 1(t), indicating that the change occurs at time t = 0. This input is utilized to examine the transient response and envelope curves of systems, providing valuable insights into system behavior under sudden changes. It is depicted in response curves and frequently used in simulations to better understand and predict system performance.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5bd763e4c9aeb66f33aa71ed14c8efd2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dd181394db0cba7b325f62f7a3a1029a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a5b38ba15c11c9500daafd6a0fa68942&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-602e1bf386d85476513e06ee772f8a95&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5fe015f4be9f2111ba91f577393b86cd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8f1e322d677e03984e2e1a0f412e6c1e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f220f834d3bf211bf1917f262c17e60d</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Undamped Natural Frequency (ωₙ)">
  <data key="d0">Undamped Natural Frequency (ωₙ)</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The undamped natural frequency is a characteristic of a system, indicating the frequency at which it will oscillate in the absence of damping.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d293ccd91839b1545fb3a78b69f822a2</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Tolerance Band">
  <data key="d0">Tolerance Band</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Tolerance band refers to the range within which a system's response is considered acceptable, often expressed as a percentage.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d293ccd91839b1545fb3a78b69f822a2</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Maximum Overshoot (Mₚ)">
  <data key="d0">Maximum Overshoot (Mₚ)</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Maximum overshoot is the extent to which a system exceeds its final steady-state value during transient response.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d293ccd91839b1545fb3a78b69f822a2</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Settling Time (tₛ)">
  <data key="d0">Settling Time (tₛ)</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Settling time is the duration required for a system to settle within a specified tolerance band after a disturbance.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d293ccd91839b1545fb3a78b69f822a2</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
