</node>
<node id="Transient Response Analysis">
  <data key="d0">Transient Response Analysis</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Transient Response Analysis examines how systems respond to changes over time before reaching steady-state conditions.&lt;SEP&gt;Transient Response Analysis involves examining the behavior of systems when subjected to sudden changes or inputs, particularly focusing on higher-order systems.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7dee9b6b4fa01e7b310d973887043bfe&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8f1e322d677e03984e2e1a0f412e6c1e</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Steady-State Response Analysis">
  <data key="d0">Steady-State Response Analysis</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Steady-State Response Analysis focuses on the long-term behavior of systems under constant inputs.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7dee9b6b4fa01e7b310d973887043bfe</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Higher-Order Systems">
  <data key="d0">Higher-Order Systems</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Higher-Order Systems are complex systems whose response is the sum of first-order and second-order systems, often requiring detailed mathematical analysis.&lt;SEP&gt;Higher-order systems with complicated numerator dynamics may have multiple gain crossover frequencies.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c90d44afb796b38b91c19121ba0bf8c5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8f1e322d677e03984e2e1a0f412e6c1e</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Closed-Loop Poles">
  <data key="d0">Closed-Loop Poles</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Closed-Loop Poles are critical points in a control system's transfer function that play a crucial role in determining the system's stability and dynamic response. These poles are located in the complex plane, specifically in the s-plane, and represent the roots of the characteristic equation of the system. When feedback is applied, they become the poles of the system's transfer function, influencing both stability and performance. The placement of these poles is a key aspect of control system design, as they are chosen to achieve desired system behavior and dynamic response characteristics, including transient response. Closed-Loop Poles are also used in pole-placement design and are affected by factors such as the damping ratio and compensator gain. Desired locations of these poles are essential for ensuring system stability, and they can be specified to achieve particular system behavior, such as in the design of observers or through the root-locus plot. Overall, Closed-Loop Poles are integral to the analysis and design of control systems, impacting how the system responds to inputs and its overall performance.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f104d09d0c472d244729f4c0273b66e0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-36e714a094e63aef48fac634eeddad6c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0948b1e428ea01364b1b1ec1955431f0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c90833eb9af9b9addb524390cd11c1e7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9f818528633d493e4d171630e04e1d9b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2591937b2f7c7c5341658076669c1b02&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9e6b7d8c1849925b0d0f1ad9793983e9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-63ee60925a95b0307b6e89fce826e877&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2602a3aebfee4edc08475d0264ce7134&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dd970bcee6ec8782e0e15281c48fc6f7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a8712fa7ddd9dc034d222eaf660d62f1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7f571134d683a0689226e17f5c8f12d2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3372436c8d88f158b8e5ef00d0368411&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f90ff0e91c9dc075f3521e774137ba02&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8f1e322d677e03984e2e1a0f412e6c1e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-52feaf2b43bc62724214f66d8266b585&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-855e35313c15abd36b6e1671061bc057&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7d424f9b5c8e1c4caa0b22f889469da1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-76bc75a542f1bbffc63059af2a43bbeb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-59498f3e851cf3c70c4395e945e7e809&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-681185e57d810ae79c2ee826ac8ba784&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5bc3dd38e993fe4446dce1bb2dde017f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8258faffcaea77860b8003c251cb0674&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ccb516dd7fa038fc65484f2cc9669722&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8c39c97551687b0bf865de0bebf31277&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7fae5f4af39f45021c25745a260390d9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-88ad04f5c92febfb736b3e73f1b155d2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0a41b322606e11a7a7d3758a0cae0e29&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7abd7647d540dcfe486743c3fa15b49c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2160cb6aaf30b68e3cbc695bfa7548cd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-971e697aef8fd265a121f8524f1dc978&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa37a4412b916a1ceb0df1a83526840b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b495866d3d765a6bd3da8989d4656fc9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6da134176f6025e67c01fe493802f375&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c0dd560e406cc5769efa44c166402303&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-03cc52015d502a70a9646315437485f3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-16d9cf4a88154292d8f7994b72a870f0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1acc9332423db1bbcc93fc2b0ec7e34d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-896487ddef7a3f411006102c6c7a9b2b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8f1f8bc5cab7c0486a4991400b504ed8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-59a053a060eb4f14d77b34ae1061cace&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-862057e40203dab14f5b486a9fcc1a5a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3c317b9ff2b2110c0c0b14f7d7fbc89c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-37c3fea424ed4692d82113ae9fd3f941&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a19f7091cfb7ee780a1c0268e833e55f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-feb8129fd0acf4bdee571b9fbeafe347&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1b54450b954742096d3c9cc14431cc98&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9d0329f094fac40719bb16c44f090e26&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a5f65ec7a6f616b6865aa6c974202119&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9838df333da6616701c1cce66c00e732</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Closed-Loop Zeros">
  <data key="d0">Closed-Loop Zeros</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Closed-Loop Zeros are points in the s-plane that can influence the system response by potentially canceling the effects of nearby poles.&lt;SEP&gt;Closed-Loop Zeros are the values of s that make the numerator of the transfer function zero, affecting system stability.&lt;SEP&gt;Closed-loop zeros consist of the zeros of the open-loop transfer function and the poles of the feedback path, crucial for determining system performance.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-35afe3e90a1acbe01560dbd9ac0de547&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8c39c97551687b0bf865de0bebf31277&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8f1e322d677e03984e2e1a0f412e6c1e</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Partial-Fraction Expansion">
  <data key="d0">Partial-Fraction Expansion</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Partial-Fraction Expansion is a mathematical technique employed to decompose complex rational expressions into simpler fractions. This process is particularly useful for facilitating inverse Laplace transforms, integration, and system analysis. It is widely utilized in control theory to enable easier analysis and computation of complex rational functions. By breaking down these expressions, Partial-Fraction Expansion simplifies the analysis and enhances the ability to work with complex mathematical models.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-dfe09b7b703c389e8f2522a6967742f4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4aecdf0555d89c0ebec0aa1b1b9980a6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-50a65ab288a27fc3df2630882da9d54d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b5d990bcffe0df8aa354e86649b81ea4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8f1e322d677e03984e2e1a0f412e6c1e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fb1dbfaf4eaadf823b6bdb3e35219536&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cf864ce976b642afda7aa4f65e82808d</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Controller (G(s))">
  <data key="d0">Controller (G(s))</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The Controller (G(s)) block represents the mathematical model of the controller's behavior in the system.&lt;SEP&gt;The controller G(s) is a component of the control system that processes the input signal and produces a control output.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-35afe3e90a1acbe01560dbd9ac0de547&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-03cc52015d502a70a9646315437485f3</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Plant (H(s))">
  <data key="d0">Plant (H(s))</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The Plant (H(s)) block represents the physical system being controlled in the feedback loop.&lt;SEP&gt;The plant H(s) is the part of the control system that represents the physical system being controlled, such as a motor or process.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-35afe3e90a1acbe01560dbd9ac0de547&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-03cc52015d502a70a9646315437485f3</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Output Signal (C(s))">
  <data key="d0">Output Signal (C(s))</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The output signal C(s) is the result produced by the control system after processing the input signal.&lt;SEP&gt;The output signal, denoted as C(s), is the processed signal that results from the system's operations.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-03cc52015d502a70a9646315437485f3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-799625a96afba94ba9a1c4461a64170d</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Complex-Conjugate Poles">
  <data key="d0">Complex-Conjugate Poles</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Complex-Conjugate Poles are mathematical terms used in transfer functions, representing pairs of poles in a system with complex numbers that have conjugate values. These poles have the same real part but opposite imaginary parts, which makes them mirror images of each other in the complex plane. Complex-Conjugate Poles are known to affect the system's stability and response, yielding second-order terms that influence the system's behavior. Additionally, they play a significant role in frequency response analysis by affecting the slope of the magnitude curve. Despite their importance in analyzing system dynamics, they often represent undesirable characteristics in system response.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-ae064a86d4a638278e16558779a2cbf9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-faf6d229bd33d9a677ddb2c4f3fe8552&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dfdd97bb0a977764c4e6f1642a1944c6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-696e4f2dc4a3654f0413002d870333af&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1ef9a9b21f3821e990c650615c128744&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-03cc52015d502a70a9646315437485f3</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Steady-State Output">
  <data key="d0">Steady-State Output</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The steady-state output is the final output of a system after all transient effects have settled, represented by c(∞) = a.&lt;SEP&gt;The steady-state output refers to the behavior of a system as time approaches infinity, often analyzed in control systems.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-03cc52015d502a70a9646315437485f3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a3d1b21ce83b859c71c888d5c7aae6ba</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Complex Plane">
  <data key="d0">Complex Plane</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The Complex Plane is a fundamental mathematical concept used to represent and visualize complex numbers graphically. It consists of two perpendicular axes: the real axis (often represented by the x-axis) and the imaginary axis (often represented by the y-axis). Each point on this plane corresponds to a complex number, with its position determined by its real and imaginary components. The Complex Plane is instrumental in various fields such as signal processing and control theory, where it is used to plot transfer functions, analyze system stability, and understand the behavior of poles and zeros. It facilitates the visualization of root loci and asymptotes, allowing for the analysis of linear closed-loop systems and their stability. This representation is crucial for comprehending the relationships and dynamics within control systems, particularly in locating open-loop poles and zeros.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c90833eb9af9b9addb524390cd11c1e7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-989ef45348532327c1757ed1c157833e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7124a0bbfe59c091664e7c98717fbde3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-87b0e0bfbf5774f7c90a99f131e0bb53&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-37550db7b78b6550ac3a1bbeaf90fe63&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2e3282077e1fe73808ab0efe43eaa3b8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-962404cbdd61044ac178296d6d459eed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a6b0fb37f85a26f35ea31ded16be6773&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-182dc59d89ae078fb7ffb41be064595c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-855e35313c15abd36b6e1671061bc057&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-61e97a29a080915ebe1313dbe1f7440e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1fcd6b26324b9fd5763b9c96f98d151a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-84a06449dbf5e39bcd3e56867b9d2ab2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-958b33ac0865635b15e9b137a06a79e4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6b6f14da320af4579baf89f7ad82028e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-021d406fb1875a04e6ca85d6a4589897&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-431795c802c4337e55d260c23ac796ea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9e943fefbd15dc3bfc97bcb9515cd1eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b780aae2274d6d12811004661f2bb425&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53be6dee22f26bf32b82d94a32a41d2f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4c51a48b42789ee6452aabf9684ca8e1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8d688cebd148c9433a92b32b02bb240b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8230d3f2f6d179c68a787d4d88412e94&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6da134176f6025e67c01fe493802f375&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3cea608f188f57be123ae911827b07d4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dce32ac491099b3bb3f7e2e4697b0721&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4401dd3428e0cf00f995665a23c44646&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-400e4b0a84de7cb82216914ca205adc9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-136efeec2362cbbe6ae14a66f80a408f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8bb8d79a4bce9c3a0571e1ec2592d1a4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-80115d2c91ecc61916cf6b117778b14b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5027e7ae655fabb2069b339ee5f89f71</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Exponential Transient-Response Terms">
  <data key="d0">Exponential Transient-Response Terms</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Exponential transient-response terms describe the behavior of systems with decay rates influenced by closed-loop poles.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-855e35313c15abd36b6e1671061bc057</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Damped Sinusoidal Transient-Response Terms">
  <data key="d0">Damped Sinusoidal Transient-Response Terms</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Damped sinusoidal transient-response terms describe oscillatory behavior in systems affected by closed-loop poles.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-855e35313c15abd36b6e1671061bc057</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Stability Analysis">
  <data key="d0">Stability Analysis</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Stability analysis involves determining whether a system's closed-loop poles lie in the left-half of the complex plane, indicating stability.&lt;SEP&gt;Stability analysis involves examining the root-locus plot to determine if a system is stable, indicated by the location of poles within the unit circle.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-855e35313c15abd36b6e1671061bc057&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-862057e40203dab14f5b486a9fcc1a5a</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Dominant Closed-Loop Poles">
  <data key="d0">Dominant Closed-Loop Poles</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Dominant Closed-Loop Poles are specific values in a control system that significantly impact its stability and transient response.&lt;SEP&gt;Dominant Closed-Loop Poles are the poles that have the greatest influence on the system's response.&lt;SEP&gt;Dominant closed-loop poles are critical points in the complex plane that largely determine system behavior and response.&lt;SEP&gt;Dominant closed-loop poles are those that have significant influence on the transient-response behavior of a system.&lt;SEP&gt;The poles that have the most significant impact on the system's behavior and stability.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-855e35313c15abd36b6e1671061bc057&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-896487ddef7a3f411006102c6c7a9b2b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b1dc98a9423242280b516d7ca4564e43&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-faf6d229bd33d9a677ddb2c4f3fe8552&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-191e311819ac0dda5c8069a7a8cfa1fd</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Step Response">
  <data key="d0">Step Response</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Step Response is the time behavior of a system when subjected to a step input, which is crucial for analyzing system dynamics. It refers to the system's reaction over time to a unit-step input, characterized by changes in amplitude. This analysis is a type of transient-response analysis that examines how a system reacts to a sudden change in input, specifically a step function. Step Response is often analyzed in control systems and signal processing and can be examined through state-space equations. The Step Response graph illustrates the system's output over time in response to a unit-step input, highlighting characteristics such as overshoot and settling time.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5cb3f412352d8203e1efad181e8a8650&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c35c8384335c3eddd1b49dc9cad5f9cf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-76155cfacd298ba99866d06587bc0e7d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4aecdf0555d89c0ebec0aa1b1b9980a6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e75920e90983d6045aaa3f10230d2c07&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ab41d39cf3a8cfab5c7e12464cab8a94&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f220f834d3bf211bf1917f262c17e60d</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Ramp Response">
  <data key="d0">Ramp Response</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Ramp response is a transient-response analysis technique that studies how a system responds to a gradually increasing input, represented by a ramp function.&lt;SEP&gt;The ramp response is a technique for analyzing system behavior using MATLAB's step-response command.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e75920e90983d6045aaa3f10230d2c07&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5c20e37f9c7b9b4075574a4206786e9c</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Numerator and Denominator Arrays">
  <data key="d0">Numerator and Denominator Arrays</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Numerator and denominator arrays are used in MATLAB to represent the coefficients of the polynomials in the transfer function of a system.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e75920e90983d6045aaa3f10230d2c07</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Plot Command">
  <data key="d0">Plot Command</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Plot Command is a MATLAB function used to create visual representations of data, such as response curves.&lt;SEP&gt;The plot command in MATLAB is used to visualize data, such as system response curves, allowing users to see the behavior of systems over time.&lt;SEP&gt;The plot command is used in MATLAB to generate graphs, taking input data and options for customizing appearance.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-120a948013a5d40b2a2baaea8da7dcb9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e75920e90983d6045aaa3f10230d2c07&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-62f88176acf0b93c66f5a8735ba14f56</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="State Matrix A">
  <data key="d0">State Matrix A</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">State Matrix A is a matrix used in the MATLAB code to define the state of the control system.&lt;SEP&gt;State Matrix A is part of the state space representation, defining system dynamics and interactions between state variables.&lt;SEP&gt;State matrix A is part of the state-space representation of a system, defining the relationship between the system's current state and its rate of change.&lt;SEP&gt;The state matrix A represents the coefficients of the system's differential equations, defining the dynamics of the system.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0ea98dac7f834a445156f9d25aec3156&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b30d3f27196cdc41b81a736db5f1aee7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e75920e90983d6045aaa3f10230d2c07&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c2db63f41e22678b776f42091570392a</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Control Matrix B">
  <data key="d0">Control Matrix B</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Control Matrix B is part of the state space representation, defining how control inputs affect system states.&lt;SEP&gt;Control matrix B is part of the state-space representation, defining how inputs to the system affect its state changes.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b30d3f27196cdc41b81a736db5f1aee7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e75920e90983d6045aaa3f10230d2c07</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Output Matrix C">
  <data key="d0">Output Matrix C</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Output Matrix C is a matrix used in the MATLAB code to define the outputs of the control system.&lt;SEP&gt;Output Matrix C is part of the state space representation, defining how system states are transformed into outputs.&lt;SEP&gt;Output matrix C is part of the state-space representation, defining how the system's state influences its outputs.&lt;SEP&gt;The output matrix C represents how the states are transformed into outputs.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0ea98dac7f834a445156f9d25aec3156&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b30d3f27196cdc41b81a736db5f1aee7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e75920e90983d6045aaa3f10230d2c07&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c2db63f41e22678b776f42091570392a</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Direct Transmission Matrix D">
  <data key="d0">Direct Transmission Matrix D</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Direct Transmission Matrix D is a matrix used in the MATLAB code to define direct transmission characteristics of the control system.&lt;SEP&gt;Direct Transmission Matrix D is part of the state space representation, defining direct paths from inputs to outputs.&lt;SEP&gt;Direct transmission matrix D is part of the state-space representation, defining the direct influence of inputs on outputs without state interaction.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0ea98dac7f834a445156f9d25aec3156&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b30d3f27196cdc41b81a736db5f1aee7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e75920e90983d6045aaa3f10230d2c07</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Example 5–3">
  <data key="d0">Example 5–3</data>
  <data key="d1">event</data>
  <data key="d2">Example 5–3 refers to a specific illustration or case study within the document.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c002c8164af0baaf62d1b67662dbbda4</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Unit-step Response Curves">
  <data key="d0">Unit-step Response Curves</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Unit-step response curves are graphical representations showing how a system responds to a unit step input.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c002c8164af0baaf62d1b67662dbbda4</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="State-space Equations">
  <data key="d0">State-space Equations</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">State-space equations are mathematical models that describe the behavior of dynamic systems using state variables.</data>
