<node id="K">
  <data key="d0">K</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">In control systems, 'K' represents the gain, a crucial parameter that influences the system's response characteristics and stability. It serves as a gain element responsible for amplifying or attenuating the input signal, thereby affecting the control output based on the error signal. 'K' is utilized in various aspects of control systems, including the PID controller's transfer function and the system's characteristic equation, where it plays a significant role in influencing the root-locus branches and system behavior. In mathematical modeling, particularly within MATLAB programs, 'K' is a key variable used in calculations. It is a parameter in the open-loop transfer function, with its value critically determining system stability. The gain 'K' must be maintained within a specified range to ensure system stability, as demonstrated in Example 7–15, where it affects both the root locus and overall system response.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-51c9b9d9d69283b7b46094d2f229a5f2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5f3d1f90ecef2ea0c231698609f06418&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-76bc75a542f1bbffc63059af2a43bbeb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-80115d2c91ecc61916cf6b117778b14b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fb053307052f18015012648da15e3c35&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-021d406fb1875a04e6ca85d6a4589897&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2667d2b35ce8e9f7ed3e633e6f40ebbf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6d4db33b4e6388556833f75db47703f2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a6b4ef5ac67266af313e88e17c7523bc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f87ef3fd6a4c1856d15e4ad169e98183&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a5f65ec7a6f616b6865aa6c974202119&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2d08ffa0936970988485d30a9ea44afb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f220f834d3bf211bf1917f262c17e60d</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Proportional Control">
  <data key="d0">Proportional Control</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Proportional Control is a basic control method where the control signal is directly proportional to the error signal, often leading to steady-state error.&lt;SEP&gt;Proportional control is a control system strategy that uses a proportional response to the error signal to stabilize the system.&lt;SEP&gt;Proportional control is a type of feedback control system that adjusts the control signal proportionally to the error signal, often resulting in a steady-state error or offset in systems without integrating properties.&lt;SEP&gt;Proportional control refers to a method in control systems where the control action is proportional to the error signal, affecting the system's stability and response.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5f3d1f90ecef2ea0c231698609f06418&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5bd763e4c9aeb66f33aa71ed14c8efd2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-39fb7e7ecf98024e40979eb23f0ae0a1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cd566e31ec5118dde48a4f149d4c5469</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Waveform (a)">
  <data key="d0">Waveform (a)</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Waveform (a) is a visual representation with an upward-sloping curve, depicting integral control with nonzero control signal when the actuating error signal is zero.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-620d81e2a5a2e80e3b426e6ed3a7d42b</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Waveform (b)">
  <data key="d0">Waveform (b)</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Waveform (b) is a visual representation with an upward-sloping curve, depicting proportional control with zero control signal when the actuating error signal is zero.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-620d81e2a5a2e80e3b426e6ed3a7d42b</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Integral Control">
  <data key="d0">Integral Control</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Integral control is a control strategy used to eliminate steady-state error by integrating the error over time.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-620d81e2a5a2e80e3b426e6ed3a7d42b</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Offset">
  <data key="d0">Offset</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Offset is the steady-state error present in a system without an integrator, leading to a persistent discrepancy in the desired output.&lt;SEP&gt;Offset is the steady-state error present in a system without integral control when responding to a step input.&lt;SEP&gt;Offset refers to the steady-state error that occurs in a control system when the output does not reach the desired setpoint, often due to the lack of an integrating property in the system.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-cd566e31ec5118dde48a4f149d4c5469&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-620d81e2a5a2e80e3b426e6ed3a7d42b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-39fb7e7ecf98024e40979eb23f0ae0a1</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Integral Control System">
  <data key="d0">Integral Control System</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">An integral control system is a type of control system that eliminates steady-state errors by integrating the error signal over time.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-39fb7e7ecf98024e40979eb23f0ae0a1</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Torque Disturbance">
  <data key="d0">Torque Disturbance</data>
  <data key="d1">event</data>
  <data key="d2">Torque Disturbance refers to the external force affecting the system's performance, which the controller compensates for.&lt;SEP&gt;Torque disturbance refers to an unexpected change in torque affecting the load element in a control system.&lt;SEP&gt;Torque disturbance refers to an unexpected change in torque that affects the performance of a speed control system, requiring the system to adjust to maintain desired output speed.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-204365918a7a13528bbf287196b97d31&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cd566e31ec5118dde48a4f149d4c5469&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-39fb7e7ecf98024e40979eb23f0ae0a1</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Moment of Inertia">
  <data key="d0">Moment of Inertia</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">A physical property of the load element in the control system affecting its dynamic response.&lt;SEP&gt;Moment of inertia is a property of the load element in a control system, affecting its response to torque.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-39fb7e7ecf98024e40979eb23f0ae0a1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cd437ba37843beb72e13fcc99049d49c</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Summer Block">
  <data key="d0">Summer Block</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">A component in the block diagram that sums the input signal with another signal from an unknown source.&lt;SEP&gt;The Summer Block is a component in a signal flow diagram that adds up all the signals entering it, represented by a circle with a plus sign.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-204365918a7a13528bbf287196b97d31&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a49db1d02aaff7365470c4256ccfb82f</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Controller Block">
  <data key="d0">Controller Block</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The Controller Block processes input signals and generates output signals based on internal logic or algorithms, denoted by the letter 'C'.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-204365918a7a13528bbf287196b97d31</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Plant Block">
  <data key="d0">Plant Block</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The Plant Block represents the physical system being controlled, such as a mechanical or electrical device, depicted as a rectangle.&lt;SEP&gt;The plant block is a component of a control system representing the physical system being controlled, described by a transfer function or equation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-204365918a7a13528bbf287196b97d31&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a12a1fcc8b96ef105600adda81cf5b8f</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Error Detector Block">
  <data key="d0">Error Detector Block</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The Error Detector Block calculates the difference between desired and actual outputs of the plant, denoted by the letter 'E'.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-204365918a7a13528bbf287196b97d31</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Laplace Transform Variable">
  <data key="d0">Laplace Transform Variable</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The Laplace Transform Variable 's' is used to analyze continuous-time systems in the frequency domain.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-204365918a7a13528bbf287196b97d31</data>
