  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Chapter 5 discusses transient and steady-state response analyses in control systems.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-204365918a7a13528bbf287196b97d31</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Gain Kp">
  <data key="d0">Gain Kp</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">A gain factor in control systems, calculated as 14,273 for the proportional-plus-derivative controller.&lt;SEP&gt;Gain Kp is a proportional gain value that influences the system's response and oscillatory behavior.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-204365918a7a13528bbf287196b97d31&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1b54450b954742096d3c9cc14431cc98</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Integral Time Constant Ti">
  <data key="d0">Integral Time Constant Ti</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Integral Time Constant Ti is a parameter used in proportional-plus-integral controllers to adjust control actions.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-204365918a7a13528bbf287196b97d31</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Derivative Controller">
  <data key="d0">Derivative Controller</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">A Derivative Controller is a type of controller that predicts future error based on its rate of change, used to improve system stability and response.&lt;SEP&gt;A controller that increases system stability by responding to the rate of change of actuating error, allowing for early corrective actions.&lt;SEP&gt;A derivative controller is a type of controller that uses the derivative of the error signal to provide control action.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-6f67e9b469ce0543584be32ab4c6f89c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d6018e5e7fd6c2f5cfc4bd67745b544a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cd437ba37843beb72e13fcc99049d49c</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Waveform Graph">
  <data key="d0">Waveform Graph</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">A graphical depiction of an oscillating signal associated with the control system, illustrating the changes over time.&lt;SEP&gt;A waveform graph visually represents oscillating signals in a control system, showing changes over time with sinusoidal patterns.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5bd763e4c9aeb66f33aa71ed14c8efd2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cd437ba37843beb72e13fcc99049d49c</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Laplace Variable">
  <data key="d0">Laplace Variable</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">A variable used in the transfer function block to represent the system's behavior in the Laplace domain.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-cd437ba37843beb72e13fcc99049d49c</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Derivative Control">
  <data key="d0">Derivative Control</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Derivative control is a control system method that anticipates error changes by measuring the error velocity, adding damping to the system, and preventing overshoot.&lt;SEP&gt;Derivative control is a method in control systems that uses the derivative of the error signal to improve system stability and response.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5bd763e4c9aeb66f33aa71ed14c8efd2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a3d1b21ce83b859c71c888d5c7aae6ba</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Proportional-Plus-Derivative Control">
  <data key="d0">Proportional-Plus-Derivative Control</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Proportional-plus-derivative control combines proportional and derivative actions to improve system stability and response by providing damping and anticipatory correction.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5bd763e4c9aeb66f33aa71ed14c8efd2</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Inertia Load">
  <data key="d0">Inertia Load</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Inertia load refers to a system component that resists changes in motion, affecting control system dynamics and requiring specific control strategies.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5bd763e4c9aeb66f33aa71ed14c8efd2</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="System Performance">
  <data key="d0">System Performance</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">System performance refers to how effectively a control system responds to inputs and achieves desired outputs, influenced by control methods.&lt;SEP&gt;The efficiency and effectiveness of a system in achieving its intended purpose, often analyzed using frequency response.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-1c56a057e86a61de468030b87f550fa4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5bd763e4c9aeb66f33aa71ed14c8efd2</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Unity-Feedback Control System">
  <data key="d0">Unity-Feedback Control System</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">A Unity-Feedback Control System is a type of control system characterized by a feedback loop with a transfer function or gain of one, which is often referred to as 'unity'. This configuration enables the direct comparison of the output to the input, allowing for the maintenance of desired performance and system stability. The feedback loop in a unity-feedback control system is unaltered, meaning the output is fed back into the input without any modification. This approach is frequently employed in control engineering to simplify system analysis, minimize errors, and improve system accuracy. By having a unity gain in the feedback loop, the system can effectively achieve desired behavior, impacting stability and error constants, which are crucial for performance analysis.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b2a20c61ffd29ee123aa4008d1f4ba5f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4401dd3428e0cf00f995665a23c44646&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a3d1b21ce83b859c71c888d5c7aae6ba&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6b1619851fc851316cb57de0ea4fe27f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-241ff02c460b748c0a8d1135d536c107&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-021d406fb1875a04e6ca85d6a4589897&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5cf6791e215ed7abe6fbda5eedbb50b9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-37550db7b78b6550ac3a1bbeaf90fe63&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4a3189f7cb3fb2865b828460dc211a36&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5bc3dd38e993fe4446dce1bb2dde017f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c7d9a35f388915f791a96d8cdc32529b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1ef9a9b21f3821e990c650615c128744&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-62181afdae55ce4e95ff620bfa6e1818&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ccfe5606d87942d95cd99b0924a1abcd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-731aadaa135cc2ef1c9482aab829f4e1</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Damping Coefficient">
  <data key="d0">Damping Coefficient</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The damping coefficient is a parameter that affects the stability and response of a control system, influencing the damping ratio.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-241ff02c460b748c0a8d1135d536c107</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Loop Transfer Function">
  <data key="d0">Loop Transfer Function</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The loop transfer function is a mathematical expression used to describe the behavior of a control system, particularly in response to inputs and feedback.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-241ff02c460b748c0a8d1135d536c107</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Input Signal R(s)">
  <data key="d0">Input Signal R(s)</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Input Signal R(s) is the initial signal fed into a control system, represented in the Laplace domain. It serves as the reference input signal, typically assumed to be constant at 1, and is depicted in block diagrams as the initial input block. R(s) initiates the signal flow within the system, undergoing processing and feedback adjustments to drive the system's response and produce an output signal.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-094d0f53918a4b57661980c588c5dd88&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-577f4c0582663d9533ba9e75104e8038&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0948b1e428ea01364b1b1ec1955431f0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c282b61c2db78468e0d216e25c2dfdb0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-241ff02c460b748c0a8d1135d536c107&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-60969709c0f545814b039c0d77d040ef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bde48fe7be57bee4bb18f23aaf9bf6a0</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Kp + KdS">
  <data key="d0">Kp + KdS</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Kp + KdS represents a control component in the system, likely a proportional-derivative controller, used to adjust system response.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-241ff02c460b748c0a8d1135d536c107</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Effective Damping Coefficient">
  <data key="d0">Effective Damping Coefficient</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The effective damping coefficient is a parameter that combines natural damping with control gains to affect system stability and response.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-241ff02c460b748c0a8d1135d536c107</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="System Classification">
  <data key="d0">System Classification</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">System classification refers to categorizing control systems based on their ability to handle different types of inputs, like step or ramp inputs.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-241ff02c460b748c0a8d1135d536c107</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Type 0, Type 1, Type 2 Systems">
  <data key="d0">Type 0, Type 1, Type 2 Systems</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">These are classifications of control systems based on their type number, which affects accuracy and stability.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-3dac0ceabd7b52fc29d821c18da7fcb1</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Static Position Error Constant (Kp)">
  <data key="d0">Static Position Error Constant (Kp)</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">A constant that indicates the steady-state error of a system for a unit-step input.&lt;SEP&gt;A measure of the steady-state error for a step input in a feedback control system, finite for type 0 systems and infinite for type 1 or higher systems.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-3dac0ceabd7b52fc29d821c18da7fcb1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-56f12da94fdc89592ee91860dba06293</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Signal Flow Graph">
  <data key="d0">Signal Flow Graph</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">A signal flow graph is a diagram representing the flow of signals through a system, showing elements like input, gain blocks, and transfer functions.&lt;SEP&gt;A visual representation of the relationships between signals and systems, using nodes and arrows to depict signal flow.&lt;SEP&gt;The Signal Flow Graph is a diagrammatic representation of a system's dynamics, illustrating the flow of signals through various blocks and stages.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-3dac0ceabd7b52fc29d821c18da7fcb1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dd970bcee6ec8782e0e15281c48fc6f7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-97ff6cf0253e8f192c45acefe4824e0b</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Type 0 System">
  <data key="d0">Type 0 System</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">A Type 0 System is a classification of control systems based on their error characteristics and response to inputs. It is characterized by a feedback control system with no integration in the feedforward path, which results in a finite static position error constant and a steady-state error for a step input. In terms of frequency response, a Type 0 System exhibits a log-magnitude plot with a horizontal line at low frequencies and a downward slope at higher frequencies. Additionally, it has specific log-magnitude curves and transfer function properties. One of the key characteristics of a Type 0 System is its inability to follow a ramp input in the steady state, leading to an infinite steady-state error. The system's Nyquist plot shows a finite starting point on the positive real axis and a terminal point at the origin, with distinct phase angle characteristics.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-76155cfacd298ba99866d06587bc0e7d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-56f12da94fdc89592ee91860dba06293&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-955b6d1a9437f260081a7c8bfe86f0af&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ccfe5606d87942d95cd99b0924a1abcd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4f64aff6c5ff2d6c3093c613b43ba0c4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5706363dcd7b89678a580b4ab70a2f02</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Type 1 System">
  <data key="d0">Type 1 System</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The Type 1 System is a classification of control systems characterized by a set of specific error and response characteristics. It features a feedback control system with integration in the feedforward path, which allows it to achieve zero steady-state error for a step input and a static velocity error constant equal to the gain K. Additionally, a Type 1 System can follow a ramp input with a finite steady-state error, which is proportional to the input velocity and inversely proportional to the gain. 

In terms of frequency response, Type 1 Systems are characterized by distinct log-magnitude curves, including an initial -20 dB/decade segment that intersects with a line at ω=1. These systems are analyzed for their frequency response patterns, as demonstrated in log-magnitude plots. Furthermore, the Type 1 System is depicted as a continuous loop on the complex plane, indicating a closed curve with specific phase angle characteristics. Overall, Type 1 Systems exhibit unique feedback, error, and frequency response attributes that define their operation within control systems.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-3a5c8adb826b6a09285fe1ad5b9d9b8c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-76155cfacd298ba99866d06587bc0e7d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-56f12da94fdc89592ee91860dba06293&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-955b6d1a9437f260081a7c8bfe86f0af&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ccfe5606d87942d95cd99b0924a1abcd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4f64aff6c5ff2d6c3093c613b43ba0c4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5706363dcd7b89678a580b4ab70a2f02</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Type 2 System">
  <data key="d0">Type 2 System</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The Type 2 System is a classification of control systems characterized by unique error characteristics and response behaviors. It features a feedback control system with a higher integration order in the feedforward path, resulting in a static velocity error constant that tends to infinity. This system can follow a ramp input with zero steady-state error and exhibits a finite steady-state error for unit parabolic input. The Type 2 System is represented as a straight line on the complex plane, which illustrates its linear function with specific phase angle features. Additionally, Type 2 systems are analyzed through their log-magnitude curves, revealing their distinct frequency response characteristics, including unique log-magnitude plots.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-3a5c8adb826b6a09285fe1ad5b9d9b8c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-76155cfacd298ba99866d06587bc0e7d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-56f12da94fdc89592ee91860dba06293&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-955b6d1a9437f260081a7c8bfe86f0af&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4f64aff6c5ff2d6c3093c613b43ba0c4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5706363dcd7b89678a580b4ab70a2f02</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Static Velocity Error Constant (Kv)">
  <data key="d0">Static Velocity Error Constant (Kv)</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The Static Velocity Error Constant (Kv) is a parameter used in control systems to measure the steady-state error in response to a ramp input. It specifically indicates the system's accuracy and speed of response when following a ramp input. In feedback control systems, Kv is zero for type 0 systems and equal to the gain K for type 1 systems. Kv serves as a measure of the steady-state error in velocity for a control system. While there is a discrepancy in the specified values of Kv, which are mentioned as 20 sec⁻¹ and 50 sec⁻¹, it is generally used to quantify the system's capability to minimize steady-state error under ramp input conditions.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4e42383167231f90ca804ba822f6b73d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8639c1dbc2042e150feddfc5fe82ffe8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9e6b7d8c1849925b0d0f1ad9793983e9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-56f12da94fdc89592ee91860dba06293&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ccfe5606d87942d95cd99b0924a1abcd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c7a34174c65e8a6a1a11ac9ee67b0bbe</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Ramp Input">
  <data key="d0">Ramp Input</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">A type of input signal in control systems characterized by a linear increase over time, used to analyze steady-state errors.&lt;SEP&gt;Ramp input is a type of input where a signal increases linearly over time, used to study system dynamics and response.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-56f12da94fdc89592ee91860dba06293&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-317307fe0a6ea411ad5662cfe25988d9</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Unity-Feedback System">
  <data key="d0">Unity-Feedback System</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">A Unity-Feedback System is a type of control system configuration where the output is fed back into the input without any modification or alteration, resulting in a feedback loop with a gain or transfer function of one. This configuration is commonly used to analyze and stabilize system behavior, often employing Nyquist plots for stability analysis. The primary aim of a Unity-Feedback System is to achieve desired system performance by comparing the output with the input.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-8221452e166faf8569286a459dc76b3e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-189305ffdfb5a3f7a9695e56559bcef3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fb053307052f18015012648da15e3c35&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a55275992e9d9009ddd4f2b26d4260c1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9e943fefbd15dc3bfc97bcb9515cd1eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-56f12da94fdc89592ee91860dba06293&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-400e4b0a84de7cb82216914ca205adc9</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Function r(t)">
  <data key="d0">Function r(t)</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">A mathematical function representing a control system response, characterized by a steeper increase over time compared to c(t).</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-56f12da94fdc89592ee91860dba06293</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Function c(t)">
  <data key="d0">Function c(t)</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">A mathematical function representing a control system response, characterized by a slower increase over time compared to r(t).</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-56f12da94fdc89592ee91860dba06293</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Type 3 or Higher System">
  <data key="d0">Type 3 or Higher System</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">A type 3 or higher system can follow a ramp input with zero error and has infinite steady-state error for unit parabolic input.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5706363dcd7b89678a580b4ab70a2f02</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Static Acceleration Error Constant K_a">
  <data key="d0">Static Acceleration Error Constant K_a</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">K_a is a constant defining the steady-state error for a system with unit-parabolic input, indicating positional error.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5706363dcd7b89678a580b4ab70a2f02</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Unit-Ramp Input">
  <data key="d0">Unit-Ramp Input</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">A type of input that results in a linear relationship between the input and output values.&lt;SEP&gt;A unit-ramp input is a type of input signal used to evaluate system response, particularly in assessing steady-state error.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-35ca799f31090a72592882ce4794e5f5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5706363dcd7b89678a580b4ab70a2f02</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Unit-Parabolic Input">
  <data key="d0">Unit-Parabolic Input</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">A unit-parabolic input is a type of input signal used to evaluate system response, particularly in assessing acceleration error.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5706363dcd7b89678a580b4ab70a2f02</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Type 0 system">
  <data key="d0">Type 0 system</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Type 0 systems are incapable of following a parabolic input in the steady state and have infinite steady-state errors.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9b6ab57eb994b3e68768778e5ab127ef</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Type 1 system">
  <data key="d0">Type 1 system</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Type 1 systems are also incapable of following a parabolic input in the steady state and have infinite steady-state errors.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9b6ab57eb994b3e68768778e5ab127ef</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Type 2 system">
  <data key="d0">Type 2 system</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Type 2 systems with unity feedback can follow a parabolic input with a finite error signal.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9b6ab57eb994b3e68768778e5ab127ef</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Type 3 or higher system">
  <data key="d0">Type 3 or higher system</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Type 3 or higher systems with unity feedback follow a parabolic input with zero error at steady state.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9b6ab57eb994b3e68768778e5ab127ef</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Unity-feedback system">
  <data key="d0">Unity-feedback system</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Unity-feedback systems are systems where the output is fed back to the input without any modification, affecting steady-state error performance.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9b6ab57eb994b3e68768778e5ab127ef</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Steady-state error">
  <data key="d0">Steady-state error</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Steady-state error refers to the difference between the desired and actual output of a system when it reaches a steady state.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9b6ab57eb994b3e68768778e5ab127ef</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Error constants">
  <data key="d0">Error constants</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Error constants, denoted as Kp, Kv, and Ka, describe a system's ability to reduce or eliminate steady-state error.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9b6ab57eb994b3e68768778e5ab127ef</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Table 5–1">
  <data key="d0">Table 5–1</data>
  <data key="d1">event</data>
  <data key="d2">Table 5–1 summarizes the steady-state errors for type 0, type 1, and type 2 systems when they are subjected to various inputs.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9b6ab57eb994b3e68768778e5ab127ef</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Figure 5–48">
  <data key="d0">Figure 5–48</data>
  <data key="d1">event</data>
  <data key="d2">Figure 5–48 shows an example of the response of a type 2 system with unity feedback to a parabolic input.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9b6ab57eb994b3e68768778e5ab127ef</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Figure 5–49">
  <data key="d0">Figure 5–49</data>
  <data key="d1">event</data>
  <data key="d2">Figure 5–49 illustrates a system where x(t) is the input displacement and θ(t) is the output angular displacement.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9b6ab57eb994b3e68768778e5ab127ef</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Gear-Train System">
  <data key="d0">Gear-Train System</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The gear-train system is used in servo systems to reduce speed, magnify torque, or optimize power transfer, involving gears with proportional teeth to their radii.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-dd181394db0cba7b325f62f7a3a1029a</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Wall">
  <data key="d0">Wall</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The wall in the spring-mass system acts as a fixed boundary, supporting the spring and mass.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-dd181394db0cba7b325f62f7a3a1029a</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Gear">
  <data key="d0">Gear</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">In the gear-train system, gears are components with teeth used to transfer motion and forces between shafts.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-dd181394db0cba7b325f62f7a3a1029a</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Motor Shaft">
  <data key="d0">Motor Shaft</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The motor shaft in the gear-train system is the driving component that transfers motion to the gear train.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-dd181394db0cba7b325f62f7a3a1029a</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Load Shaft">
  <data key="d0">Load Shaft</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The load shaft in the gear-train system receives motion and force from the motor shaft through the gears.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-dd181394db0cba7b325f62f7a3a1029a</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Differentiating System">
  <data key="d0">Differentiating System</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">A differentiating system is one that outputs the derivative of the input signal.&lt;SEP&gt;Differentiating System is a system that computes the derivative of its input signal.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-faf6d229bd33d9a677ddb2c4f3fe8552&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dd181394db0cba7b325f62f7a3a1029a</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Shaft 1">
  <data key="d0">Shaft 1</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Shaft 1 is connected to an electric motor and is part of the input torque transmission system.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f63499af2a9ae0d75cf56aad0cf76999</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Shaft 2">
  <data key="d0">Shaft 2</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Shaft 2 receives torque from Gear 1 and drives Gear 2 and Gear 3.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f63499af2a9ae0d75cf56aad0cf76999</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Shaft 3">
  <data key="d0">Shaft 3</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Shaft 3 is involved in the transmission of torque to the load through Gear 4.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f63499af2a9ae0d75cf56aad0cf76999</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Shaft 4">
  <data key="d0">Shaft 4</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Shaft 4 is connected to the load and receives torque from Gear 4.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f63499af2a9ae0d75cf56aad0cf76999</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Gear 1">
  <data key="d0">Gear 1</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Gear 1 has a ratio of 1:2 and transmits torque from the motor to Shaft 2.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f63499af2a9ae0d75cf56aad0cf76999</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Gear 2">
  <data key="d0">Gear 2</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Gear 2 is driven by Shaft 2 and is part of the torque transmission system.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f63499af2a9ae0d75cf56aad0cf76999</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Gear 3">
  <data key="d0">Gear 3</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Gear 3 is driven by Shaft 2 and contributes to torque transmission.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f63499af2a9ae0d75cf56aad0cf76999</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Gear 4">
  <data key="d0">Gear 4</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Gear 4 has a ratio of 3:2 and transmits torque to Shaft 4.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f63499af2a9ae0d75cf56aad0cf76999</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Input Torque">
  <data key="d0">Input Torque</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Input Torque is represented by $T_m(t)$ and comes from the motor.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f63499af2a9ae0d75cf56aad0cf76999</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Load Torque">
  <data key="d0">Load Torque</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Load Torque is represented by $T_L(t)$ and is connected to Shaft 4.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f63499af2a9ae0d75cf56aad0cf76999</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="J1">
  <data key="d0">J1</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">J1 represents the moment of inertia associated with shaft 1 in a mechanical system.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d4032b50ce14c0c3568bdbf73522823e</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="J2">
  <data key="d0">J2</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">J2 represents the moment of inertia associated with shaft 2 in a mechanical system.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d4032b50ce14c0c3568bdbf73522823e</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="J3">
  <data key="d0">J3</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">J3 represents the moment of inertia associated with shaft 3 in a mechanical system.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d4032b50ce14c0c3568bdbf73522823e</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="b1">
  <data key="d0">b1</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">b1 represents the viscous friction coefficient associated with shaft 1 in a mechanical system.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d4032b50ce14c0c3568bdbf73522823e</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="b2">
  <data key="d0">b2</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">b2 represents the viscous friction coefficient associated with shaft 2 in a mechanical system.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d4032b50ce14c0c3568bdbf73522823e</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
