<node id="Polynomial Coefficients">
  <data key="d0">Polynomial Coefficients</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Polynomial Coefficients are the numerical factors assigned to the terms in a polynomial equation, crucial for determining system stability.&lt;SEP&gt;Polynomial coefficients are the numerical factors in the terms of a polynomial equation that influence its shape and properties.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7d4f87e9dd3cfeb7f1869f4f209585ee&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-59252f7260535877b27b152342d1fa5d</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Determinants">
  <data key="d0">Determinants</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Determinants are mathematical expressions derived from matrices, used in stability criteria to assess system behavior.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7d4f87e9dd3cfeb7f1869f4f209585ee</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Routh Stability Criterion">
  <data key="d0">Routh Stability Criterion</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The Routh Stability Criterion is a mathematical technique widely used in control theory to assess the stability of a system. It involves analyzing the system's characteristic equation and the denominator of its transfer function. By examining the first column of the Routh array, the criterion helps determine the number of right-half plane zeros of the transfer function, which is crucial for evaluating system stability. Additionally, the method involves checking for sign changes and analyzing the signs and magnitudes of coefficients in a polynomial equation. Overall, the Routh Stability Criterion is a comprehensive tool for determining the stability of control systems.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a7067d3378c6320f2be99583ea919091&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-75625ca25735063f07915ffbf3fb4581&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4a3189f7cb3fb2865b828460dc211a36&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f59f8623e4ff8e64d49175daeeadad05&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a480968a716a749c7646332b8d7dec12&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-85b819ddf3cb3d7c56ca7d6f41195981&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-59252f7260535877b27b152342d1fa5d</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Hurwitz Determinants">
  <data key="d0">Hurwitz Determinants</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Hurwitz Determinants are triangular matrices used in the Hurwitz Stability Criterion to verify stability conditions.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-85b819ddf3cb3d7c56ca7d6f41195981</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Principal Minors">
  <data key="d0">Principal Minors</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Principal minors are determinants of submatrices formed from a larger matrix, used in stability analysis to determine the positivity conditions required for system stability.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-59252f7260535877b27b152342d1fa5d</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Delta Notation">
  <data key="d0">Delta Notation</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Delta notation refers to the use of Greek letter delta (Δ) to represent determinants or differences in mathematical equations, particularly in stability analysis.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-59252f7260535877b27b152342d1fa5d</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Asymptotic Stability">
  <data key="d0">Asymptotic Stability</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">A property of a system where, over time, the system's response approaches a steady state.&lt;SEP&gt;Asymptotic stability refers to a condition where the system returns to equilibrium after a disturbance, often analyzed using stability criteria like Hurwitz and Routh.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-761f357930e8cb4b1f367ec8167a5238&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-59252f7260535877b27b152342d1fa5d</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
