  <data key="d0">Electric Motor Control System</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">An electric motor control system regulates the torque and speed of an electric motor using various components and mathematical operations.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-cd566e31ec5118dde48a4f149d4c5469</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Steady State">
  <data key="d0">Steady State</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Steady state is the condition in a control system where the system's variables remain constant over time, indicating that the system has reached equilibrium.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-cd566e31ec5118dde48a4f149d4c5469</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Control Algorithm">
  <data key="d0">Control Algorithm</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">A control algorithm is a set of rules or calculations used by a controller to process input signals and generate appropriate output signals to achieve desired system behavior.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-cd566e31ec5118dde48a4f149d4c5469</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Time Delay">
  <data key="d0">Time Delay</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Time delay in a control system refers to the lag between the application of an input and the system's response, which can affect system stability and performance.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-cd566e31ec5118dde48a4f149d4c5469</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Unit-Step Disturbance Torque">
  <data key="d0">Unit-Step Disturbance Torque</data>
  <data key="d1">event</data>
  <data key="d2">A unit-step disturbance torque is a sudden change in torque applied to the system, used to test the system's response and stability.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-ed23283c207553099a8ea220b94e1005</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Controller Transfer Function $G_{c}(s)$">
  <data key="d0">Controller Transfer Function $G_{c}(s)$</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">$G_{c}(s)$ is the transfer function of a controller designed to eliminate speed errors due to torque disturbances in the system.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-ed23283c207553099a8ea220b94e1005</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Steady-State Output Velocity">
  <data key="d0">Steady-State Output Velocity</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The steady-state output velocity is the final speed of the system after a disturbance torque is applied, indicating the system's ability to stabilize.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-ed23283c207553099a8ea220b94e1005</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Proportional Mode">
  <data key="d0">Proportional Mode</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Proportional mode is a component of the controller that helps stabilize the system by adjusting the control signal proportionally to the error.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-ed23283c207553099a8ea220b94e1005</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Steady-State Output Speed">
  <data key="d0">Steady-State Output Speed</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The output speed of a control system when it reaches a stable condition, unaffected by transient errors.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a49db1d02aaff7365470c4256ccfb82f</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Input Block">
  <data key="d0">Input Block</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The Input Block is a crucial component in both control and signal processing systems. It serves as the starting point where the input signal is introduced into the system. In a control system's block diagram, the Input Block is typically located at the top-left corner and is responsible for representing the input signal. Although it is labeled differently depending on the context—such as 'K', 'u', 'Ω(r)(s)', 'C(s)', and 'E(s)'—its fundamental role remains the same: to act as the entry point for signals into the system. This component is essential for initiating the processes within the system, ensuring that the input signals are accurately represented and processed.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a49db1d02aaff7365470c4256ccfb82f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-95a8d8a1b3cfb0710c46f13ae2e3b48e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-96901c8e0575c3c8488667c451ac4616&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fd21bbb7bb0f22b9146dce0bacde7f33&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8b159c6715de41e6ec8764fe45124ff9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1a8d984a98f532ef69747b8e72c4a5bd</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Gain Block">
  <data key="d0">Gain Block</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The Gain Block is a component commonly found in control and signal processing systems. It is represented by a rectangle in block diagrams and functions to amplify or attenuate input signals based on a specified gain factor. In the context provided, it is labeled as 'Gc(s)' and can also be represented as a constant multiplier in the system, denoted as 'K + 0.1s + 0.5'. The Gain Block plays a crucial role in adjusting the magnitude of input signals within these systems.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0948b1e428ea01364b1b1ec1955431f0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a49db1d02aaff7365470c4256ccfb82f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6ee28303feb0c79b4fc2e2ca88dd29b6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fd21bbb7bb0f22b9146dce0bacde7f33&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5d0a99065421970bde5dae1689b5a3b1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-731aadaa135cc2ef1c9482aab829f4e1</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Addition Block">
  <data key="d0">Addition Block</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">A component in the block diagram that combines two signals, labeled as 'E(s)'.&lt;SEP&gt;The addition block combines inputs from the input block and a gain block, contributing to the overall control system.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-96901c8e0575c3c8488667c451ac4616&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a49db1d02aaff7365470c4256ccfb82f</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Output Block">
  <data key="d0">Output Block</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Part of the control system that represents the output signal, labeled as '/s(s + 1)'.&lt;SEP&gt;The Output Block represents the final processed signal, located at the bottom-right corner of the diagram and denoted by 'y'.&lt;SEP&gt;The component in a control system's block diagram representing the output signal, labeled as 'T(s)'.&lt;SEP&gt;The output block represents the final component in the control system, labeled as 'U(s)' in the diagram.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-fd21bbb7bb0f22b9146dce0bacde7f33&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8b159c6715de41e6ec8764fe45124ff9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-96901c8e0575c3c8488667c451ac4616&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a49db1d02aaff7365470c4256ccfb82f</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Transfer Function Block">
  <data key="d0">Transfer Function Block</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">A component in the block diagram describing the relationship between input and output signals in the Laplace domain, labeled as 'D(s)'.&lt;SEP&gt;The Transfer Function Block represents the mathematical model that describes how the input signal is transformed into an output signal in a control system.&lt;SEP&gt;The Transfer Function Block represents the transformation of the input signal into the output signal in a control system.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-95a8d8a1b3cfb0710c46f13ae2e3b48e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a49db1d02aaff7365470c4256ccfb82f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-731aadaa135cc2ef1c9482aab829f4e1</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Block 1: 1/J(s)">
  <data key="d0">Block 1: 1/J(s)</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">A component in the block diagram representing a transfer function or controller with proportional control action.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a49db1d02aaff7365470c4256ccfb82f</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Block 2: Kp(s + K/I)">
  <data key="d0">Block 2: Kp(s + K/I)</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">A component in the block diagram representing a transfer function or controller with a gain of Kp, an integrator, and a derivative term.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a49db1d02aaff7365470c4256ccfb82f</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Block 3: D(s)">
  <data key="d0">Block 3: D(s)</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">A component in the block diagram representing the output signal or response of the control system.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a49db1d02aaff7365470c4256ccfb82f</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Proportional-Plus-Derivative Filter">
  <data key="d0">Proportional-Plus-Derivative Filter</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">A type of filter used in control systems to improve response and reduce steady-state error.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5cf6791e215ed7abe6fbda5eedbb50b9</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Unit Ramp Input">
  <data key="d0">Unit Ramp Input</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">A type of input signal that increases linearly over time, used to test system response.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5cf6791e215ed7abe6fbda5eedbb50b9</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Disturbance Torque">
  <data key="d0">Disturbance Torque</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">External torque that affects the performance of a control system, requiring compensation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5cf6791e215ed7abe6fbda5eedbb50b9</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Environmental Changes">
  <data key="d0">Environmental Changes</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Environmental Changes refer to external factors that can affect system gain and time constants, requiring compensation in design.&lt;SEP&gt;Factors that can alter the performance of a control system, affecting parameters like damping and frequency.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5cf6791e215ed7abe6fbda5eedbb50b9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-696e4f2dc4a3654f0413002d870333af</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="First-order System">
  <data key="d0">First-order System</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">A First-order System is depicted in Figure 7–3, characterized by its transfer function G(s) and response to sinusoidal inputs.&lt;SEP&gt;A type of system characterized by a single time constant affecting its response to input changes.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-fa75d98979aa642bf0a23416fce30d35&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f0ec5181a2b3e37816173bb15c763932</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Unit-step Response">
  <data key="d0">Unit-step Response</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">A response of a system to a unit step input, showing how the output signal changes over time.&lt;SEP&gt;The response of a system to a step input, often used to analyze system dynamics and performance.&lt;SEP&gt;The unit-step response is an event where the plant responds to a unit-step input, often used to analyze system behavior.&lt;SEP&gt;Unit-step response is a measure of how a system reacts to a step input, used to evaluate system performance.&lt;SEP&gt;Unit-step response is the output of a system when a step input is applied, shown in the graph.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-1fcd6b26324b9fd5763b9c96f98d151a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-389ed190c89a251c5214072489791123&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a12a1fcc8b96ef105600adda81cf5b8f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f0ec5181a2b3e37816173bb15c763932&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-97ff6cf0253e8f192c45acefe4824e0b</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Unity-feedback Control System">
  <data key="d0">Unity-feedback Control System</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">A control system configuration where the output is fed back to the input to maintain system stability and performance.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f0ec5181a2b3e37816173bb15c763932</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Open-loop Transfer Function">
  <data key="d0">Open-loop Transfer Function</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The Open-loop Transfer Function is a mathematical representation of a control system's behavior before feedback is applied. It describes the relationship between input and output in a system without feedback, and is crucial for stability and performance analysis in control systems. The function is often used to analyze a system's response and is represented by specific mathematical expressions, such as G(s) = 9/(s(s + 0.5)(s^2 + 0.6s + 10)) or G(s) = 10/(s+1). Additionally, it is denoted as G(s)H(s), which is relevant to stability analysis and the Nyquist criterion. Overall, the Open-loop Transfer Function plays a vital role in understanding and analyzing the stability and performance of control systems.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5518828c63927a29cbd23a5354674537&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-016ebe44f8f410b4e420081ccf7a65f9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-958b33ac0865635b15e9b137a06a79e4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-431795c802c4337e55d260c23ac796ea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-75625ca25735063f07915ffbf3fb4581&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2fac437c10d4b085f8ef3459b8dfdb4f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-136efeec2362cbbe6ae14a66f80a408f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6d46c03f5b1a1f1ab07036c79e47616c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1fc8172454011b7e111636e450b0431d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f0ec5181a2b3e37816173bb15c763932&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6dd7424b8787a27ad020fa883c295d8b</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Impulse Force">
  <data key="d0">Impulse Force</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">An impulse force acts on the block, causing it to accelerate along the positive x-direction in the mass-spring system.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-37fb7998dd9fe418c1b904b5fad6ac47</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Mass-Spring System">
  <data key="d0">Mass-Spring System</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">A fundamental concept in physics and engineering, illustrating a block attached to a spring, showing oscillatory behavior.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-37fb7998dd9fe418c1b904b5fad6ac47</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Waveform">
  <data key="d0">Waveform</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">A graphical representation of wave amplitude over time, indicating oscillatory behavior with peaks and troughs.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-37fb7998dd9fe418c1b904b5fad6ac47</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Damped Oscillation">
  <data key="d0">Damped Oscillation</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">A type of oscillatory motion where the amplitude decreases over time, as shown in the waveform graph.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-37fb7998dd9fe418c1b904b5fad6ac47</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Damping Ratio ($\zeta$)">
  <data key="d0">Damping Ratio ($\zeta$)</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Damping Ratio ($\zeta$) is a parameter that indicates how oscillations in a system decay after a disturbance.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-37fb7998dd9fe418c1b904b5fad6ac47</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Natural Frequency ($\omega_{n}$)">
  <data key="d0">Natural Frequency ($\omega_{n}$)</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Natural Frequency ($\omega_{n}$) is the frequency at which a system tends to oscillate in the absence of damping.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-37fb7998dd9fe418c1b904b5fad6ac47</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Wave Amplitude">
  <data key="d0">Wave Amplitude</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Wave Amplitude is the maximum displacement of a wave from its equilibrium position.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-37fb7998dd9fe418c1b904b5fad6ac47</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Tachometer Feedback">
  <data key="d0">Tachometer Feedback</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">A method of stabilizing control systems by using feedback from a tachometer to adjust system behavior.&lt;SEP&gt;Tachometer Feedback is a method used to improve system stability by feeding back velocity information.&lt;SEP&gt;Tachometer feedback is a method used in control systems to provide speed information for better regulation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-37fb7998dd9fe418c1b904b5fad6ac47&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9571f0c498fec38011c9f30cf7ab85ab&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a7067d3378c6320f2be99583ea919091</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Advanced Controller">
  <data key="d0">Advanced Controller</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">An advanced controller incorporates integral and derivative terms in addition to the proportional term, offering faster response times and better disturbance rejection.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9571f0c498fec38011c9f30cf7ab85ab</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
