<node id="Integrator Block">
  <data key="d0">Integrator Block</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">An integrator block in a control system computes the integral of its input signal, contributing to the overall system response.&lt;SEP&gt;The integrator block is part of the output processing section in the control system, characterized by a transfer function that allows tracking changes over time.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a4349e4239d3b82325e7a8cb18d1189c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9571f0c498fec38011c9f30cf7ab85ab</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Differentiator Block">
  <data key="d0">Differentiator Block</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">A differentiator block calculates the derivative of its input signal, enhancing the system's response to changes.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9571f0c498fec38011c9f30cf7ab85ab</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Feedforward Path">
  <data key="d0">Feedforward Path</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">A feedforward path in a control system directly connects the reference input to the control output, improving response time.&lt;SEP&gt;The feedforward path is a configuration in control systems where disturbances enter the system before the main control process.&lt;SEP&gt;The feedforward path is part of a control system where signals are sent directly to the output without feedback, often modified by compensators.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-95dada5b51e73c98f754b3ae69765f91&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9571f0c498fec38011c9f30cf7ab85ab&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-64543aa197d39924b6592a9f9b8937d3</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Gain Element">
  <data key="d0">Gain Element</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">A component of the control system that amplifies the input signal, represented by the equation K/s + 2 in the block diagram.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-577f4c0582663d9533ba9e75104e8038</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Output C(s)">
  <data key="d0">Output C(s)</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">C(s) represents the controlled variable output of the control system, displayed in the block diagram.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-577f4c0582663d9533ba9e75104e8038</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Positional Servo System">
  <data key="d0">Positional Servo System</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">A type of control system designed to maintain the position of a mechanical component, depicted as System I, II, and III in the block diagrams.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-577f4c0582663d9533ba9e75104e8038</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="PD Control Action">
  <data key="d0">PD Control Action</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">A control method using proportional and derivative actions to improve system response, applied in System II.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-577f4c0582663d9533ba9e75104e8038</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Position Control System">
  <data key="d0">Position Control System</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">A Position Control System is a type of control system that manages the position of an object or system component.&lt;SEP&gt;The position control system is a type of control system designed to regulate or track a desired trajectory based on a reference input signal.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4a3189f7cb3fb2865b828460dc211a36&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a19f7091cfb7ee780a1c0268e833e55f</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Block 1">
  <data key="d0">Block 1</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Block 1 is a component in a signal processing system that performs mathematical operations on the input signal.&lt;SEP&gt;Block 1 is responsible for processing the reference input signal in the control system.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f22851addd0d0d43a22e89bf8e277faf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4a3189f7cb3fb2865b828460dc211a36</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Block 2">
  <data key="d0">Block 2</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Block 2 computes the error between the actual output and the desired output in the control system.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4a3189f7cb3fb2865b828460dc211a36</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Block 3">
  <data key="d0">Block 3</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Block 3 represents a summation operation, adding signals from Block 1 and Block 2.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4a3189f7cb3fb2865b828460dc211a36</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Block 4">
  <data key="d0">Block 4</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Block 4 applies a gain of 4 to the output signal in the control system.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4a3189f7cb3fb2865b828460dc211a36</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Block 5">
  <data key="d0">Block 5</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Block 5 is likely responsible for scaling or normalizing the input signal in the control system.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4a3189f7cb3fb2865b828460dc211a36</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Block 6">
  <data key="d0">Block 6</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Block 6 represents a transfer function, a first-order low-pass filter with a time constant of 0.1s and gain of 2.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4a3189f7cb3fb2865b828460dc211a36</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Block 7">
  <data key="d0">Block 7</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Block 7 represents the output of the system, which is the filtered input signal from Block 5.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4a3189f7cb3fb2865b828460dc211a36</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Satellite Attitude Control System">
  <data key="d0">Satellite Attitude Control System</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">A control system designed to manage the orientation of a satellite, which can be stabilized using tachometer feedback.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a7067d3378c6320f2be99583ea919091</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Schwarz Matrix">
  <data key="d0">Schwarz Matrix</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">A matrix used in control theory, represented as a specific arrangement of elements used to describe system dynamics.&lt;SEP&gt;The Schwarz Matrix is a specific matrix used in control system analysis, particularly for determining the Routh's array of its characteristic equation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5bc3dd38e993fe4446dce1bb2dde017f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a7067d3378c6320f2be99583ea919091</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Routh's Array">
  <data key="d0">Routh's Array</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Routh's Array is a tabular method used in control system engineering to determine the stability of a system by analyzing the characteristic equation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5bc3dd38e993fe4446dce1bb2dde017f</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Adder">
  <data key="d0">Adder</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">A component used to combine or subtract signals within the control system.&lt;SEP&gt;An Adder in control systems combines multiple input signals, influencing the overall system response and dynamics.&lt;SEP&gt;An Adder is a component in a control system that combines multiple signals into a single output.&lt;SEP&gt;The Adder is a component in the control system that combines the input signal with noise.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5518828c63927a29cbd23a5354674537&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9b87b5f3d20e81642454297b93cc16f3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5bc3dd38e993fe4446dce1bb2dde017f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7cb24308be90f4de9c5775f0a3a25ce9</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Multiplier">
  <data key="d0">Multiplier</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">A Multiplier in control systems adjusts the amplitude of signals, essential for gain control and system stability.&lt;SEP&gt;A Multiplier is a component in a control system used to scale input signals by a specific factor.&lt;SEP&gt;A component of the control system that scales the input signal.&lt;SEP&gt;The Multiplier is a component in the control system that amplifies the input signal by a factor of 5.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5518828c63927a29cbd23a5354674537&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-28ae81c40745842e3e00aecec87aa63f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5bc3dd38e993fe4446dce1bb2dde017f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7bd9d9a9ccff6dcc7aa39644785f5940</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Rouths Array">
  <data key="d0">Rouths Array</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Routh’s Array is a tabular method used in control system engineering to determine the stability of a system by analyzing the characteristic equation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5bc3dd38e993fe4446dce1bb2dde017f</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Gain">
  <data key="d0">Gain</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Gain is a parameter in control systems that serves as an amplification factor applied to the input signal, influencing the amplitude and magnitude of the output signal relative to the input. It is represented by a box with a plus sign in the block diagram of a transfer function. Gain plays a crucial role in determining the system's performance and stability, affecting the behavior of the root locus plot and the overall response of the system.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c90833eb9af9b9addb524390cd11c1e7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bb2cafb27b000379bc871ea7923a83dd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-72d535e84ba337e6877808174fd78fb5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-96901c8e0575c3c8488667c451ac4616&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-971e697aef8fd265a121f8524f1dc978&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ff7353abf978480820979c540d814ee3</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Poles">
  <data key="d0">Poles</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Poles are specific points in the complex plane that play a crucial role in determining the behavior and stability of a system. They are elements in the characteristic equation of the system, located where the roots of this equation are found. Poles influence the system's stability and response characteristics, affecting the root locus plot and system behavior. In the s-domain, poles are values where the transfer function or its derivatives become unbounded or infinite, indicating singular points. The location of poles can be affected by the compensator, and they are essential in determining system stability using concepts like the mapping theorem. Overall, poles are integral to understanding system dynamics, as they determine where the function becomes undefined and how the system responds to various inputs.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f22851addd0d0d43a22e89bf8e277faf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5b3b6091500d4d6709d33e0c55198fc0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-989ef45348532327c1757ed1c157833e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4c51a48b42789ee6452aabf9684ca8e1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-72d535e84ba337e6877808174fd78fb5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-83bded94a3ff142ba41772e1b37f4f63&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-37c3fea424ed4692d82113ae9fd3f941&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6da134176f6025e67c01fe493802f375&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a6b0fb37f85a26f35ea31ded16be6773&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-db115ced573d3098e7cb02b4ddf8625f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3cea608f188f57be123ae911827b07d4</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Zeros">
  <data key="d0">Zeros</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Zeros are specific values in the s-domain, represented as points in the complex plane, where the numerator of a system's transfer function becomes zero. These points, located at s = -a, are crucial in influencing the system's behavior, response, and stability. Zeros play a significant role in the root locus plot and the mapping theorem, affecting the frequency response and stability of a control system. They are singular points where the function G(s) or F(s) equals zero, thereby impacting the system's overall dynamics and performance.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-8230d3f2f6d179c68a787d4d88412e94&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-989ef45348532327c1757ed1c157833e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7cb24308be90f4de9c5775f0a3a25ce9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4c51a48b42789ee6452aabf9684ca8e1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-72d535e84ba337e6877808174fd78fb5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-83bded94a3ff142ba41772e1b37f4f63&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8d688cebd148c9433a92b32b02bb240b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6da134176f6025e67c01fe493802f375</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Root Locus">
  <data key="d0">Root Locus</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Root Locus is a graphical method widely used in control systems and control theory to analyze the roots of a system's characteristic equation or transfer function as system parameters vary, typically focusing on gain (K). This representation helps in determining the possible locations of closed-loop poles and studying how these poles change with variations in system parameters. Root Locus is instrumental in analyzing the stability and response of a system, showing the paths of system poles and examining the angle condition and asymptotes. It serves as a crucial tool for understanding the behavior and stability of control systems as parameters are adjusted.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-ad53164b58b57cd02287907a08cfbb42&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dfdd97bb0a977764c4e6f1642a1944c6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bfaabf07740c618e0c89f07d1cf48dea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53900274f2c35aaf0d99d0a9ee95f307&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7947832f5a2e447102e9db53f42dc041&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-76bc75a542f1bbffc63059af2a43bbeb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-586f97239ba09b5129d50fc14028adbc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0b429aa524429e2d3a0bb88587574c34&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7a366ed13150829b18c3d5756f1ce91c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7fae5f4af39f45021c25745a260390d9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6a89464a87dc57e95afa0a980ba9d6c0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53be6dee22f26bf32b82d94a32a41d2f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2667d2b35ce8e9f7ed3e633e6f40ebbf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-efbee75e0f53cdd0a07a900923cddb09&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8d688cebd148c9433a92b32b02bb240b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8230d3f2f6d179c68a787d4d88412e94&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6da134176f6025e67c01fe493802f375&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1a8d984a98f532ef69747b8e72c4a5bd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3c317b9ff2b2110c0c0b14f7d7fbc89c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8ad1f447db822714c4aaac1a32550e21&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-80115d2c91ecc61916cf6b117778b14b</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Gain Parameter K">
  <data key="d0">Gain Parameter K</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Gain Parameter K is a variable that affects the stability and performance of a control system, influencing the position of poles in the root locus plot.&lt;SEP&gt;Gain parameter K is a variable that influences the system's behavior, affecting the location of poles and zeros.&lt;SEP&gt;The gain parameter K is a variable in the transfer function affecting the system's frequency response and gain.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c0b45e8c05ce6d6a2e33cb4a65bc30d4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6da134176f6025e67c01fe493802f375&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8bb8d79a4bce9c3a0571e1ec2592d1a4</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Angle Condition">
  <data key="d0">Angle Condition</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Angle condition is a criterion used in control systems to determine the validity of root loci points.&lt;SEP&gt;The angle condition is a criterion used in control systems to determine if a point lies on the root locus.&lt;SEP&gt;The angle condition is a criterion used in control systems to determine the existence of root loci on specific sections of the real axis.&lt;SEP&gt;The angle condition is a criterion used in root locus analysis to determine the angles at which poles and zeros affect the system.&lt;SEP&gt;The angle condition is a mathematical criterion used in control systems to ensure proper system behavior, involving equations related to phase angles.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-8f1f8bc5cab7c0486a4991400b504ed8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2172726751d3bb09e7c6c3c9d2e73db9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53be6dee22f26bf32b82d94a32a41d2f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6da134176f6025e67c01fe493802f375&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-80115d2c91ecc61916cf6b117778b14b</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Magnitude Condition">
  <data key="d0">Magnitude Condition</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Magnitude Condition is a mathematical criterion used in control systems, particularly in root locus analysis, to determine the system gain necessary for stability. It involves calculating the gain value, often denoted as K or $K_{c}$, that satisfies the stability requirements of the system. This condition is essential for establishing the magnitude relationship between poles and zeros, ensuring that the gain allows the system to achieve desired pole locations. The Magnitude Condition involves specific equations that must be satisfied during the analysis, making it a crucial component in the design and stability assessment of control systems.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-8f1f8bc5cab7c0486a4991400b504ed8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2172726751d3bb09e7c6c3c9d2e73db9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53be6dee22f26bf32b82d94a32a41d2f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-95dada5b51e73c98f754b3ae69765f91&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-feb8129fd0acf4bdee571b9fbeafe347&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6da134176f6025e67c01fe493802f375</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Open-Loop Poles">
  <data key="d0">Open-Loop Poles</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Open-Loop Poles are specific points on the complex plane that play a crucial role in determining the behavior and stability of a control system. These poles are elements in the s-plane where the denominator of the system's transfer function becomes zero, which directly affects the system's stability and response. In the context of a control system, open-loop poles are the poles of the system before any feedback is applied, impacting the initial system stability and behavior. They are integral to the analysis of the system's root locus, influencing the shape and direction of the root loci, and are essential for understanding the system's dynamics as depicted in the Nyquist diagram. The location of open-loop poles, such as those on the jw axis, adds complexity to the system's response and stability analysis.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-dce32ac491099b3bb3f7e2e4697b0721&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a3d1b21ce83b859c71c888d5c7aae6ba&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-95dada5b51e73c98f754b3ae69765f91&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-35afe3e90a1acbe01560dbd9ac0de547&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1022a5cf276c07ee56ffc50c13799b6d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-40ee0f407a8a7e48b91d23a82652414f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8d688cebd148c9433a92b32b02bb240b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1b54450b954742096d3c9cc14431cc98&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6da134176f6025e67c01fe493802f375&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8230d3f2f6d179c68a787d4d88412e94&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7fae5f4af39f45021c25745a260390d9</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Open-Loop Zeros">
  <data key="d0">Open-Loop Zeros</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Open-loop zeros are the zeros of a system without feedback, affecting the system's frequency response and stability.&lt;SEP&gt;Open-loop zeros are the zeros of the open-loop transfer function, affecting the root loci and system response.&lt;SEP&gt;Open-loop zeros are zeros of the system when no feedback is applied, affecting the initial system response.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-6da134176f6025e67c01fe493802f375&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-35afe3e90a1acbe01560dbd9ac0de547&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7fae5f4af39f45021c25745a260390d9</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
