  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Negative Feedback System">
  <data key="d0">Negative Feedback System</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">A Negative Feedback System is a type of control system configuration designed to enhance stability by mitigating the effects of disturbances. In this system, the output is fed back into the input with a negative sign, which serves to reduce fluctuations and errors. By continuously adjusting the input based on the output, the system effectively stabilizes itself, ensuring consistent performance and minimizing deviations from desired outcomes. This feedback mechanism is integral to maintaining the stability and reliability of control systems.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-53be6dee22f26bf32b82d94a32a41d2f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dfdd97bb0a977764c4e6f1642a1944c6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2e3282077e1fe73808ab0efe43eaa3b8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-526791d98cb78b5ccbbec0c10ad8ce82&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2172726751d3bb09e7c6c3c9d2e73db9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8bb8d79a4bce9c3a0571e1ec2592d1a4</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Gain K">
  <data key="d0">Gain K</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Gain K is a crucial parameter in control systems that significantly influences the system's stability, performance, and response. It acts as an amplification factor, affecting the magnitude of the system's output, often expressed in decibels. Gain K plays a pivotal role in determining the position of poles and zeros on the root locus plot, thereby impacting the root locus path and system stability. It is integral to Bode diagram analysis and can be adjusted to meet specific system requirements, such as satisfying the static velocity error constant. Gain K is also involved in calculating system stability using the Nyquist plot. For instance, it has been calculated as 1.3210 when a positive value of a=3.6056 is considered. Overall, Gain K is a parameter that can be fine-tuned to achieve desired system dynamics and ensure optimal performance.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4bf77eaf82ba369d832435b0e6f076eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7124a0bbfe59c091664e7c98717fbde3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ad53164b58b57cd02287907a08cfbb42&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bfaabf07740c618e0c89f07d1cf48dea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-62181afdae55ce4e95ff620bfa6e1818&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-db115ced573d3098e7cb02b4ddf8625f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2f074d90ecc0d7974d2a0b0c59285149&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7fb6f6f4567355162f36e936387a3dc4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2fac437c10d4b085f8ef3459b8dfdb4f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0b429aa524429e2d3a0bb88587574c34&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a2217ccd983144a03364b841c0519056&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a480968a716a749c7646332b8d7dec12&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4c51a48b42789ee6452aabf9684ca8e1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53be6dee22f26bf32b82d94a32a41d2f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8230d3f2f6d179c68a787d4d88412e94&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-441dbc13e7ef7807ed52d100c9a4f041&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dce32ac491099b3bb3f7e2e4697b0721&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a5b3fa9ac1d3ce61d8dbdd21acb60d10&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3c317b9ff2b2110c0c0b14f7d7fbc89c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fc700758bd11889bde17fb78bf15e8bf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-30758f9c11d60c2ad7dbc4a84ff615d7</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Open-loop Poles">
  <data key="d0">Open-loop Poles</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Open-loop poles are specific points in the complex plane that define the initial state of a control system before feedback is applied.&lt;SEP&gt;Open-loop poles are the values in the s-plane where the system's open-loop transfer function becomes infinite, specifically at s=-1+j, s=-1-j, and s=-3.&lt;SEP&gt;Open-loop poles are values in the control system that determine the behavior and stability of the system, located at specific points in the equation.&lt;SEP&gt;Points in the system's transfer function that determine stability, located at s = 0 and s = -1.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7cb24308be90f4de9c5775f0a3a25ce9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53be6dee22f26bf32b82d94a32a41d2f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53900274f2c35aaf0d99d0a9ee95f307&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-efbee75e0f53cdd0a07a900923cddb09</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Open-loop Complex-conjugate Poles">
  <data key="d0">Open-loop Complex-conjugate Poles</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Open-loop complex-conjugate poles are symmetrical points about the real axis in a control system.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-53be6dee22f26bf32b82d94a32a41d2f</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Complex-conjugate Zeros">
  <data key="d0">Complex-conjugate Zeros</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Complex-conjugate zeros are symmetrical points about the real axis in a control system.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-53be6dee22f26bf32b82d94a32a41d2f</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Real Axis">
  <data key="d0">Real Axis</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The Real Axis is a fundamental component in various mathematical contexts, notably in the analysis of complex numbers and system stability. It is the horizontal axis in the complex plane, commonly referred to as the x-axis, representing the real part of complex numbers. This axis plays a crucial role in different types of plots and graphs, such as root-locus plots and polar plots, where it serves as a reference line for analyzing real values and system stability. In the s-plane, the Real Axis is used to locate root-locus branches, providing insight into the behavior and stability of dynamic systems. Overall, the Real Axis is integral to understanding and visualizing the real component of complex numbers across various mathematical and engineering applications.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-dfdd97bb0a977764c4e6f1642a1944c6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-284ad82d5a54aa2c3d44aa8399b9ac0c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a6b0fb37f85a26f35ea31ded16be6773&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-182dc59d89ae078fb7ffb41be064595c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-db115ced573d3098e7cb02b4ddf8625f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-958b33ac0865635b15e9b137a06a79e4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b1cfae784926f0c2b91ee587d149b8b5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-914a61458271e99a6bf821d8e99ad4cf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53be6dee22f26bf32b82d94a32a41d2f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d30d89b485b27ebd673bf1d2c57c24a7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-486351ecd11a42798affd79b7f5d143b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3cea608f188f57be123ae911827b07d4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-92e23c313eaf5a6d348bce8a1588be0f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1d98502cd8b9198221f2d4fb5c72719c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-691177b7d933c82cade5a9fd3f182f4a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-feb8129fd0acf4bdee571b9fbeafe347&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b52720f9a7352e99f41bd87710c52e0f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a5f65ec7a6f616b6865aa6c974202119&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7e83e0b0f093a4a5ee928c26cb73ad75</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Graphical Computation">
  <data key="d0">Graphical Computation</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Graphical computation is a method used to visually determine the root loci of a control system.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-53be6dee22f26bf32b82d94a32a41d2f</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Damping Ratio ζ">
  <data key="d0">Damping Ratio ζ</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">A parameter that describes the damping characteristics of a system, calculated using frequency values.&lt;SEP&gt;Damping Ratio ζ is a parameter that influences the system's oscillatory behavior.&lt;SEP&gt;Damping ratio ζ is a measure of oscillation reduction in a control system, affecting system stability.&lt;SEP&gt;Damping ratio ζ is a parameter that describes the degree of oscillation damping in a system, affecting the resonant peak and frequency response.&lt;SEP&gt;The Damping Ratio ζ is a measure of how oscillations in a system decay after a disturbance.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a104cc76c4ded119be1e066101146986&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3a5c8adb826b6a09285fe1ad5b9d9b8c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7fb6f6f4567355162f36e936387a3dc4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53be6dee22f26bf32b82d94a32a41d2f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-691177b7d933c82cade5a9fd3f182f4a</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Asymptotes">
  <data key="d0">Asymptotes</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Asymptotes are lines in control system analysis, specifically in root locus plots, that provide insights into the behavior and stability of the system. They represent the paths that the poles follow as the gain increases and the variable s approaches infinity. In root locus diagrams, asymptotes indicate the directions that the root loci approach, calculated based on the number of poles and zeros in the system. These lines are crucial for understanding the limits and boundaries of the root loci, especially in regions far from the origin. Asymptotes are depicted as dashed lines extending from the origin to infinity on the root locus plot, showing the behavior of the system's poles as a parameter approaches infinity and indicating the direction of the loci for large values of s. While they are sometimes mentioned in the context of Bode Plots, asymptotes primarily serve to illustrate the behavior of control systems in root locus analysis, helping engineers and analysts assess system stability and performance.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c90833eb9af9b9addb524390cd11c1e7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dce32ac491099b3bb3f7e2e4697b0721&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4401dd3428e0cf00f995665a23c44646&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6a89464a87dc57e95afa0a980ba9d6c0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-98b7da91ec0504b2cb24aec37c61430a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fc700758bd11889bde17fb78bf15e8bf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3cea608f188f57be123ae911827b07d4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8d688cebd148c9433a92b32b02bb240b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ec1fe4f707d8920dbeac015fecfd158e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53900274f2c35aaf0d99d0a9ee95f307&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-80115d2c91ecc61916cf6b117778b14b</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Negative Real Axis">
  <data key="d0">Negative Real Axis</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The negative real axis is a part of the root locus where specific angle conditions are satisfied, influencing the control system's root loci.&lt;SEP&gt;The negative real axis is part of the real number line used in complex analysis, relevant for determining system behavior.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0b429aa524429e2d3a0bb88587574c34&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-80115d2c91ecc61916cf6b117778b14b</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Figure 6–4">
  <data key="d0">Figure 6–4</data>
  <data key="d1">event</data>
  <data key="d2">Figure 6–4 illustrates the three straight lines that represent the asymptotes in the context of the mathematical equations discussed.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-6a89464a87dc57e95afa0a980ba9d6c0</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Breakaway Point">
  <data key="d0">Breakaway Point</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">A breakaway point is a location on the root locus where the path of the locus moves away from the real axis or a pole.&lt;SEP&gt;A point in the root locus where the root locus branches diverge or 'break away' from the real axis, specifically at s=0.&lt;SEP&gt;Breakaway Point refers to a point in root locus analysis where branches of the locus leave the real axis.&lt;SEP&gt;The Breakaway Point is a location on the real axis where root locus branches diverge or converge.&lt;SEP&gt;The breakaway point is a location in the s-plane where root-locus branches leave the real axis and enter the complex plane, indicating multiple roots of the characteristic equation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-dce32ac491099b3bb3f7e2e4697b0721&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6a89464a87dc57e95afa0a980ba9d6c0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-586f97239ba09b5129d50fc14028adbc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bc4f091219e1ee158ce8e0f022f44c42&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-faf6d229bd33d9a677ddb2c4f3fe8552</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
