  <data key="d0">Equation (6–9)</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Equation (6–9) is used to solve for points where multiple roots occur, relevant to breakaway points.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-6a89464a87dc57e95afa0a980ba9d6c0</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Breakaway Points">
  <data key="d0">Breakaway Points</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Breakaway points are locations on the root locus plot where the locus leaves the real axis and moves into the complex plane, critical for determining system stability.&lt;SEP&gt;Breakaway points are locations on the root locus where branches of the locus diverge from the real axis.&lt;SEP&gt;Breakaway points are specific values on the root locus where the gain K is real and positive.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-ec1fe4f707d8920dbeac015fecfd158e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8ad1f447db822714c4aaac1a32550e21&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8d688cebd148c9433a92b32b02bb240b</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Imaginary Axis">
  <data key="d0">Imaginary Axis</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The Imaginary Axis is a fundamental component of the complex plane, serving as the vertical axis that represents the imaginary part of complex numbers. This axis is crucial in various mathematical and engineering analyses, particularly in control systems and stability analysis. In root locus and s-plane plots, the Imaginary Axis is used to determine system stability and analyze oscillatory behavior of system poles. It is the line where the real part of a complex number is zero, allowing for visualization and examination of imaginary values in mathematical graphs and polar plots. The Imaginary Axis is integral to understanding the behavior and characteristics of systems in the complex plane.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-284ad82d5a54aa2c3d44aa8399b9ac0c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a6b0fb37f85a26f35ea31ded16be6773&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-182dc59d89ae078fb7ffb41be064595c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-db115ced573d3098e7cb02b4ddf8625f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-958b33ac0865635b15e9b137a06a79e4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bc4f091219e1ee158ce8e0f022f44c42&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a2217ccd983144a03364b841c0519056&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b1cfae784926f0c2b91ee587d149b8b5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-914a61458271e99a6bf821d8e99ad4cf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-486351ecd11a42798affd79b7f5d143b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2172726751d3bb09e7c6c3c9d2e73db9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dce32ac491099b3bb3f7e2e4697b0721&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-92e23c313eaf5a6d348bce8a1588be0f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1d98502cd8b9198221f2d4fb5c72719c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-691177b7d933c82cade5a9fd3f182f4a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-feb8129fd0acf4bdee571b9fbeafe347&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8ad1f447db822714c4aaac1a32550e21&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b52720f9a7352e99f41bd87710c52e0f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a5f65ec7a6f616b6865aa6c974202119</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Control Systems Analysis and Design by the Root-Locus Method">
  <data key="d0">Control Systems Analysis and Design by the Root-Locus Method</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">A technical approach and methodology for analyzing and designing control systems using the root-locus method, focusing on compensators and system behavior.&lt;SEP&gt;This category involves the study and application of control systems using the root-locus method to analyze and design system stability.&lt;SEP&gt;This category refers to the study and application of control systems using the root-locus method, a graphical approach for analyzing system stability and dynamics.&lt;SEP&gt;This refers to the methodology of using root-locus plots to analyze and design control systems for stability and performance.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-610f25557b703d8c29a8efc2d292eb77&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2172726751d3bb09e7c6c3c9d2e73db9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-962404cbdd61044ac178296d6d459eed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-526791d98cb78b5ccbbec0c10ad8ce82</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
