  <data key="d0">Figure 6–5</data>
  <data key="d1">event</data>
  <data key="d2">Figure 6–5 is referenced as a part of the process for analyzing root loci in control systems, involving test points and angle conditions.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-2172726751d3bb09e7c6c3c9d2e73db9</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Figure 6–6">
  <data key="d0">Figure 6–6</data>
  <data key="d1">event</data>
  <data key="d2">Figure 6–6 depicts the root-locus plot used for illustrating mathematical concepts related to control systems.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-2172726751d3bb09e7c6c3c9d2e73db9</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Root Loci">
  <data key="d0">Root Loci</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Root Loci are graphical representations used in control system design to illustrate the trajectories of system poles and zeros in the complex plane as system parameters are varied. These plots are essential for understanding how the poles of a transfer function change, indicating system stability and response characteristics as the gain increases or decreases. By showing the paths that the roots of a characteristic equation follow, Root Loci provide valuable insights into the behavior and stability of control systems.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b1cfae784926f0c2b91ee587d149b8b5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1fcd6b26324b9fd5763b9c96f98d151a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1d98502cd8b9198221f2d4fb5c72719c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7fb6f6f4567355162f36e936387a3dc4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-95dada5b51e73c98f754b3ae69765f91&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fc700758bd11889bde17fb78bf15e8bf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-35afe3e90a1acbe01560dbd9ac0de547&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a8cb8c630fac347b0be449cc1bb5f9f1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-810a0f1b2efa8cfcd9d3dfe3cd72ff62&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2172726751d3bb09e7c6c3c9d2e73db9</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Closed-loop Poles">
  <data key="d0">Closed-loop Poles</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Closed-loop Poles are critical components in control systems that play a pivotal role in determining the stability and response characteristics of the system. They are specific values located in the complex plane, often referred to as the s-plane, where the denominator of the transfer function becomes zero. These poles are the roots of the characteristic equation of a feedback control system, and their locations directly affect the system's behavior and stability. In control theory, Closed-loop Poles are considered the eigenvalues of the system, influencing its stability and response. They are also specific points on the root locus that satisfy the magnitude condition, indicating system stability for a given gain. Additionally, Closed-loop Poles are the desired poles for the system's response, calculated to achieve specific performance objectives. Overall, Closed-loop Poles are essential in analyzing and designing control systems to ensure desired stability and response characteristics.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-8c1c7e149cb37d4947fe26456e62a591&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6cffb8feca70304ee8ba81a24e48ddea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fc700758bd11889bde17fb78bf15e8bf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6ece149736a8793b07c50dccb1028cee&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1779b26d10c1b1abb7d91ca4e5524e77&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-22085333984fa377791f298c557d58ea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-761f357930e8cb4b1f367ec8167a5238&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cba4dc46aaf3979ba1619c43d08d1720&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bfaabf07740c618e0c89f07d1cf48dea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2172726751d3bb09e7c6c3c9d2e73db9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ceb4d77b2cbe8e502eaa62894b68c016&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-97ff6cf0253e8f192c45acefe4824e0b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c6daa8560d2825569846748debe43a15</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Complex-conjugate Poles">
  <data key="d0">Complex-conjugate Poles</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Complex-conjugate poles are pairs of poles that have equal real parts and imaginary parts that are equal in magnitude but opposite in sign.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-2172726751d3bb09e7c6c3c9d2e73db9</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Sustained Oscillations">
  <data key="d0">Sustained Oscillations</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Sustained oscillations refer to continuous oscillatory behavior in a system without damping.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-2172726751d3bb09e7c6c3c9d2e73db9</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Unstable System">
  <data key="d0">Unstable System</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">An unstable system has a negative phase margin and inappropriate gain margin, leading to instability.&lt;SEP&gt;An unstable system has negative gain and phase margins, meaning it cannot tolerate increases in gain without becoming unstable.&lt;SEP&gt;An unstable system is one in which small disturbances lead to large deviations from its intended behavior.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-2dd6d60316691290acdbc20600569961&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2172726751d3bb09e7c6c3c9d2e73db9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c90d44afb796b38b91c19121ba0bf8c5</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
