<node id="Conditionally Stable System">
  <data key="d0">Conditionally Stable System</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">A Conditionally Stable System is a type of control system that remains stable only within certain ranges of a parameter, potentially becoming unstable outside those ranges.&lt;SEP&gt;A conditionally stable system is one that remains stable only within a certain range of open-loop gain values, outside of which it becomes unstable.&lt;SEP&gt;A conditionally stable system refers to a system that remains stable under certain conditions or parameter values, as depicted in the Bode diagram.&lt;SEP&gt;Conditionally Stable System is a system that remains stable only under specific conditions.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-094d0f53918a4b57661980c588c5dd88&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7124a0bbfe59c091664e7c98717fbde3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-faf6d229bd33d9a677ddb2c4f3fe8552&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6d35fea10973c2b24ef34a97da4f0e8b</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Nonminimum-Phase System">
  <data key="d0">Nonminimum-Phase System</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">A Nonminimum-Phase System is a type of system in control theory characterized by having poles and/or zeros in the right-half s-plane. This configuration affects its phase-shift characteristics under sinusoidal inputs and results in phase-angle characteristics that are not uniquely determined by the magnitude curve. Such systems can exhibit negative phase and gain margins, yet they may still maintain stability if certain conditions are met. Nonminimum-phase systems are known for their slow response due to faulty behavior at the start of a response and excessive phase lag, often including elements like transport lag. These systems require careful stability analysis, often utilizing the Nyquist diagram approach to ensure proper handling of their stability and response characteristics.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f104d09d0c472d244729f4c0273b66e0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-131d68e1d8fb1dac5267fbb38c0b52ef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c90d44afb796b38b91c19121ba0bf8c5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6a256259f60bf17c11f960427ffa11ff&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a3d1b21ce83b859c71c888d5c7aae6ba&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7124a0bbfe59c091664e7c98717fbde3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-562db1bae3e6a30605a16f1c339c6e3d</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Pole Locations">
  <data key="d0">Pole Locations</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Pole Locations refer to the specific points in the complex plane where the poles of a control system are situated, indicating the system's stability.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7124a0bbfe59c091664e7c98717fbde3</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Zero">
  <data key="d0">Zero</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Zero is a mathematical concept used in various engineering and scientific calculations, particularly in control systems. It represents a point in the complex plane, specifically in the s-plane, where the system's transfer function becomes zero, resulting in the system's output being zero for a given input. This occurrence affects the system's frequency response, phase, and stability. In the context of a system's open-loop transfer function, a zero is a value of the numerator that impacts the system dynamics. For instance, a zero might occur at a specific point such as s=-2. Notably, zeros are distinct from poles, which are points where the transfer function becomes infinite, and are absent in this particular system.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7fb6f6f4567355162f36e936387a3dc4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7124a0bbfe59c091664e7c98717fbde3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-95dada5b51e73c98f754b3ae69765f91&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-76155cfacd298ba99866d06587bc0e7d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7a366ed13150829b18c3d5756f1ce91c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53900274f2c35aaf0d99d0a9ee95f307&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-db115ced573d3098e7cb02b4ddf8625f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0d107c40e8d19f88e2db4b3ea948e97e</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
