<node id="Magnitude">
  <data key="d0">Magnitude</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Magnitude in the context of a Bode diagram refers to the amplitude of the system's output response at different frequencies.&lt;SEP&gt;Magnitude in the context of the transfer function refers to the absolute value of the function's response at a given frequency, depicted as the radius in the polar plot.&lt;SEP&gt;Magnitude refers to the size or length of a vector in complex number representation, depicted in the circular graph.&lt;SEP&gt;The absolute value of a complex number, representing the size or length of the vector in the complex plane.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b1cfae784926f0c2b91ee587d149b8b5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5b3b6091500d4d6709d33e0c55198fc0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-914a61458271e99a6bf821d8e99ad4cf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5ce576a87f38c3c3621dc3b3d8f16913</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Phase Angle">
  <data key="d0">Phase Angle</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Phase Angle is a critical concept in understanding the behavior and dynamics of systems, particularly in the frequency domain. It refers to the difference in phase between the input and output sinusoidal signals, which affects the lead or lag characteristics of a system. This angular difference is essential for determining system stability and phase response. The phase angle is measured as the angle of lag or lead between the input and output signals, calculated using the system's transfer function. It can also be represented as the angle of a vector in complex number representation, often depicted in a rectangular graph. Additionally, the phase angle is the angle component of the polar plot, representing the phase shift of the sinusoidal transfer function as frequency changes. Understanding the phase angle is crucial for analyzing the phase shift introduced by a system at a given frequency, which is vital for assessing system stability and response.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a104cc76c4ded119be1e066101146986&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-131d68e1d8fb1dac5267fbb38c0b52ef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b1cfae784926f0c2b91ee587d149b8b5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-96a98f812e32e184c15978c0d7771176&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa75d98979aa642bf0a23416fce30d35&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-914a61458271e99a6bf821d8e99ad4cf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6b139b34ebbeaf26c5893fedc131b125&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5c5ea418170c18599f96d1d10d3ce8a3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d8efef9175eeea628e4106a451e3ce5d</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Conjugate Symmetry">
  <data key="d0">Conjugate Symmetry</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Conjugate symmetry is a property of complex numbers where certain transformations are symmetrical.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b1cfae784926f0c2b91ee587d149b8b5</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Constant-Phase Loci">
  <data key="d0">Constant-Phase Loci</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Constant-phase loci are curves representing points where the phase angle is constant.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b1cfae784926f0c2b91ee587d149b8b5</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="S-Plane">
  <data key="d0">S-Plane</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">A complex plane used in control theory to analyze the roots of characteristic equations.&lt;SEP&gt;The S-Plane is a complex plane used in control theory to represent system dynamics, with real and imaginary axes.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b1cfae784926f0c2b91ee587d149b8b5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-52feaf2b43bc62724214f66d8266b585</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
