<node id="Angle Deficiency">
  <data key="d0">Angle Deficiency</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">A calculation used in root-locus approach to determine the contribution needed from zeros.&lt;SEP&gt;Angle Deficiency refers to the lack of phase angle needed for system stability in control systems.&lt;SEP&gt;Angle deficiency refers to the shortfall in angle required to achieve a desired closed-loop pole location, addressed by the lead compensator.&lt;SEP&gt;The angle deficiency is a calculated value in the control system that must be compensated by the lead compensator.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7cb24308be90f4de9c5775f0a3a25ce9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-95dada5b51e73c98f754b3ae69765f91&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-36e714a094e63aef48fac634eeddad6c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b1dc98a9423242280b516d7ca4564e43</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Method 1">
  <data key="d0">Method 1</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Method 1 is a design approach that results in a larger static velocity error constant, providing smaller steady-state errors for ramp inputs compared to Method 2.&lt;SEP&gt;Method 1 is a procedure for determining the zero and pole locations of the lead compensator to maximize system performance.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-95dada5b51e73c98f754b3ae69765f91&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9fd33644dda3aedeaeec8c353b048358</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Closed-Loop Pole">
  <data key="d0">Closed-Loop Pole</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">A closed-loop pole is a point in the system's transfer function that affects stability and performance, located on the root-locus plot.&lt;SEP&gt;A point within the system's characteristic equation where the system becomes unstable, labeled in the diagram.&lt;SEP&gt;Closed-loop poles are locations in the complex plane that determine the stability and response characteristics of a closed-loop control system.&lt;SEP&gt;Closed-loop poles are specific points in the s-plane that determine the stability and response of a control system.&lt;SEP&gt;The closed-loop pole is a critical component in control systems, influencing stability and performance based on its location in the system.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-95dada5b51e73c98f754b3ae69765f91&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bb2cafb27b000379bc871ea7923a83dd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-12f9482a2de5a4049338d2f20af641c2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1b54450b954742096d3c9cc14431cc98&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-191e311819ac0dda5c8069a7a8cfa1fd</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Compensated System">
  <data key="d0">Compensated System</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The Compensated System refers to a control system that has been modified to enhance its performance, stability, and response characteristics. This system typically undergoes adjustments such as the addition of compensators, controllers, or feedback control methods like PID controllers to meet desired specifications. Common techniques used in the modification process include the application of lead, lag, or lag-lead compensators, which are designed to improve dynamic response, accuracy, and stability. The system is often analyzed using root-locus plots and is characterized by faster response times, higher steady-state values, and lower steady-state errors compared to an uncompensated system. These improvements are illustrated in unit-step and unit-ramp response graphs, which show superior outputs and reduced latency over time. Overall, the Compensated System outperforms the original, uncompensated system, demonstrating enhanced performance in real-world applications.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7fb2f374d1a1fada7677f9980d7c9fe7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c90833eb9af9b9addb524390cd11c1e7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0948b1e428ea01364b1b1ec1955431f0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-96a98f812e32e184c15978c0d7771176&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-95dada5b51e73c98f754b3ae69765f91&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9e6b7d8c1849925b0d0f1ad9793983e9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c7d9a35f388915f791a96d8cdc32529b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ec1fe4f707d8920dbeac015fecfd158e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f90ff0e91c9dc075f3521e774137ba02&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-59498f3e851cf3c70c4395e945e7e809&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a2ddda49062cacf815e40ae9bc8167c7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7cbeb374a3410f00ce0c005645f04e14&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8c39c97551687b0bf865de0bebf31277&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c3a55af272f2092fd9d053a8d89af4b4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4ef7861303c3473cd3d47c68848af64d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bb2cafb27b000379bc871ea7923a83dd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-486351ecd11a42798affd79b7f5d143b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4e48cc17fab45352c6a28829079bd2b7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-896487ddef7a3f411006102c6c7a9b2b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cfe03dc7efd5be8b8b6f705865273b86&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ac56debe5e5db6d5809231139242ca61&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-37c3fea424ed4692d82113ae9fd3f941&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bca6ad29eb261282c2df664425ef7d0b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ed1ff4c236c16a58ee408da4a88effc4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a35e7b410420b5623c1fd33526a3e9d0</data>
