<node id="Pole-Zero Cancellation">
  <data key="d0">Pole-Zero Cancellation</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Pole-zero cancellation occurs when a system's pole is canceled by a corresponding zero, affecting the system's dynamic behavior and stability.&lt;SEP&gt;Pole-zero cancellation refers to the phenomenon where poles and zeros in a transfer function cancel each other, affecting controllability and observability.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-ec1fe4f707d8920dbeac015fecfd158e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b773a713efcb75c181833b78c35c4f74</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Stability Region">
  <data key="d0">Stability Region</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The stability region is the area in the complex plane where all system poles have negative real parts, indicating stable system behavior.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-ec1fe4f707d8920dbeac015fecfd158e</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Desired Closed-Loop Poles">
  <data key="d0">Desired Closed-Loop Poles</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Desired closed-loop poles are specific locations in the complex plane where system poles are placed for desired performance.&lt;SEP&gt;The desired closed-loop poles are specific points in the complex plane that the system aims to achieve for optimal performance and stability.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-ec1fe4f707d8920dbeac015fecfd158e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5ab66ab2ea102011daec41a066d3877c</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
