<node id="Control Systems Analysis and Design">
  <data key="d0">Control Systems Analysis and Design</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Control Systems Analysis and Design involves the study and design of control systems using methods such as frequency response.&lt;SEP&gt;Control Systems Analysis and Design refers to the study and creation of systems to regulate the behavior of other systems using various methods, including frequency-response.&lt;SEP&gt;Control Systems Analysis and Design refers to the study and development of systems using methods like frequency-response to meet specific performance criteria.&lt;SEP&gt;Control systems analysis and design involves examining system responses using frequency-response methods to ensure stability and desired performance.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-131d68e1d8fb1dac5267fbb38c0b52ef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-810a0f1b2efa8cfcd9d3dfe3cd72ff62&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-053e8edb648cfc91fffeac7f5faec45b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a2ddda49062cacf815e40ae9bc8167c7</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Gain Constant K">
  <data key="d0">Gain Constant K</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">A parameter in control systems that determines the proportionality of the output response to the input signal.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-810a0f1b2efa8cfcd9d3dfe3cd72ff62</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Transfer Function C(s)">
  <data key="d0">Transfer Function C(s)</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">A mathematical representation of the output-to-input ratio of a control system in the Laplace domain.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-810a0f1b2efa8cfcd9d3dfe3cd72ff62</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Nichols">
  <data key="d0">Nichols</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Nichols is a figure associated with control systems, mentioned in relation to charts and plots.&lt;SEP&gt;Nichols played a significant role in the advancement of frequency-response methods in control theory in the 1930s and 1940s.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-053e8edb648cfc91fffeac7f5faec45b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e8ea5a2f30b2dcfbbb586c6854279bee</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Frequency-Response Methods">
  <data key="d0">Frequency-Response Methods</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Frequency-response methods are analytical techniques used in control systems to study the steady-state response to sinusoidal inputs, offering advantages in design and analysis without needing a mathematical model.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-053e8edb648cfc91fffeac7f5faec45b</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Sinusoidal Signal Generators">
  <data key="d0">Sinusoidal Signal Generators</data>
  <data key="d1">equipment</data>
  <data key="d2">Sinusoidal signal generators are devices used to produce sinusoidal signals for frequency-response tests in control systems.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-053e8edb648cfc91fffeac7f5faec45b</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Frequency">
  <data key="d0">Frequency</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Frequency is a parameter in sinusoidal transfer functions, affecting the magnitude and phase angle of the system's response.&lt;SEP&gt;Frequency refers to the rate at which a periodic event occurs, significant in analyzing system responses.&lt;SEP&gt;Frequency refers to the rate at which a sinusoidal input signal oscillates, measured in rad/sec or cycles/sec.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9f0dfc9266a7cbf0daf9292867c654cf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8c39c97551687b0bf865de0bebf31277&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-053e8edb648cfc91fffeac7f5faec45b</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Sinusoidal Input">
  <data key="d0">Sinusoidal Input</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Sinusoidal Input refers to a type of input signal characterized by a smooth, repetitive oscillation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-053e8edb648cfc91fffeac7f5faec45b</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Open-Loop System">
  <data key="d0">Open-Loop System</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">An Open-Loop System is a type of control system that operates without feedback, relying solely on the input to determine the output.&lt;SEP&gt;An open-loop system is a configuration where the system operates without feedback from output signals.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-053e8edb648cfc91fffeac7f5faec45b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f599210548e4a595268cbd4a1d4b5e3c</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Stable, Linear, Time-Invariant System">
  <data key="d0">Stable, Linear, Time-Invariant System</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">A system characterized by stability, linearity, and time-invariance, where certain mathematical properties ensure predictable behavior over time.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5b3b6091500d4d6709d33e0c55198fc0</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Complex Conjugate">
  <data key="d0">Complex Conjugate</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">A concept in mathematics where a complex number is paired with another complex number having the same real part but an opposite imaginary part.&lt;SEP&gt;Complex Conjugate refers to changing the sign of the imaginary part of a complex number, with applications in calculating magnitudes.&lt;SEP&gt;The complex conjugate of a complex function is formed by changing the sign of the imaginary part, used in various mathematical operations.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-284ad82d5a54aa2c3d44aa8399b9ac0c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-814b833e231cf6e96b1f333e7eb5bbc4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5b3b6091500d4d6709d33e0c55198fc0</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Angle">
  <data key="d0">Angle</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The phase angle of a complex number, indicating the direction of the vector in the complex plane.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5b3b6091500d4d6709d33e0c55198fc0</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Sinusoidal Waves">
  <data key="d0">Sinusoidal Waves</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Sinusoidal Waves refer to the waveforms depicted in the image, both having identical wavelengths and differing amplitudes.&lt;SEP&gt;Waves characterized by a smooth, periodic oscillation, often used to describe alternating signals.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5b3b6091500d4d6709d33e0c55198fc0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa75d98979aa642bf0a23416fce30d35</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Complex Frequency">
  <data key="d0">Complex Frequency</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">A frequency expressed in complex form, used in the analysis of systems in the frequency domain.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5b3b6091500d4d6709d33e0c55198fc0</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Multiplicities">
  <data key="d0">Multiplicities</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The number of times a particular pole is repeated in the transfer function, affecting the system's response.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5b3b6091500d4d6709d33e0c55198fc0</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Sinusoidal Function">
  <data key="d0">Sinusoidal Function</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">A mathematical function that describes a smooth, periodic oscillation, often used to model waveforms.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5b3b6091500d4d6709d33e0c55198fc0</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="X Wave">
  <data key="d0">X Wave</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The X Wave is one of the two sinusoidal waves depicted in the image, characterized by a slightly higher amplitude compared to the Y Wave.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-fa75d98979aa642bf0a23416fce30d35</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Y Wave">
  <data key="d0">Y Wave</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The Y Wave is one of the two sinusoidal waves depicted in the image, with a lower amplitude compared to the X Wave but identical wavelength.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-fa75d98979aa642bf0a23416fce30d35</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Wavelength">
  <data key="d0">Wavelength</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Wavelength is the distance between successive peaks of the sinusoidal waves, which is identical for both X and Y waves.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-fa75d98979aa642bf0a23416fce30d35</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Sinusoidal Transfer Function">
  <data key="d0">Sinusoidal Transfer Function</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The Sinusoidal Transfer Function is a mathematical representation used to describe and analyze the behavior of systems in response to sinusoidal inputs. It is defined by its magnitude and phase angle, which characterize the system's output response to these inputs. Denoted as G(jω), this complex function represents the ratio of output to input sinusoidal signals within a linear system, making it a valuable tool in frequency analysis. By focusing on the frequency domain, the sinusoidal transfer function provides insights into how systems behave when subjected to sinusoidal stimuli, offering a comprehensive understanding of their dynamic characteristics.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-fa75d98979aa642bf0a23416fce30d35&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-914a61458271e99a6bf821d8e99ad4cf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9f0dfc9266a7cbf0daf9292867c654cf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fbfe186ba42a9341b463befd113d8ee4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2afbc3d7bb9c389990910a284d3b9dfd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-182dc59d89ae078fb7ffb41be064595c</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Nyquist Plot">
  <data key="d0">Nyquist Plot</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">A Nyquist Plot is a graphical representation widely used in control systems to analyze the frequency response and stability of a system. It is depicted in the complex plane, often utilizing polar coordinates to illustrate both the magnitude and phase angle of the system's transfer function across various frequencies. The plot is instrumental in determining the stability and relative stability of a control system by examining the encirclement of critical points, particularly the -1+j0 point, which is crucial for assessing system behavior. Nyquist Plots are generated using tools like MATLAB and are essential for understanding the performance and compensatory characteristics of control systems. As a type of polar plot, the Nyquist Plot provides a comprehensive view of a system's frequency-response characteristics, making it a vital tool in control system engineering and analysis.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a3d1b21ce83b859c71c888d5c7aae6ba&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9f0dfc9266a7cbf0daf9292867c654cf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a8c47160042a9398aa709e25eded3b0c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cf4e759f85cb69aeac07e60c7ccbf414&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-75625ca25735063f07915ffbf3fb4581&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d8a3a4cd5f5a625120a672d9302619e4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-52feaf2b43bc62724214f66d8266b585&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2f074d90ecc0d7974d2a0b0c59285149&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-004c5161c3b1d015eb48ffa3f8bf337c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-958b33ac0865635b15e9b137a06a79e4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-550487a7cadc4b8eeb83211703d232ec&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-021d406fb1875a04e6ca85d6a4589897&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bc420a4e236b18e1114725fd8e9d12b5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9e943fefbd15dc3bfc97bcb9515cd1eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4e3fcd1b5ae55946bb03cb8fa5a6859d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-73bfc3b55b53a6d06667c4a72e7fb9df&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b780aae2274d6d12811004661f2bb425&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9b8dd02e28c6fb3ac089290386b4c847&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2160cb6aaf30b68e3cbc695bfa7548cd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1fc8172454011b7e111636e450b0431d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b30d3f27196cdc41b81a736db5f1aee7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-243f0eb7fbab51b314764e54593d5d37&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-97ff6cf0253e8f192c45acefe4824e0b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fb053307052f18015012648da15e3c35&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9e830557547fd0c9d37cbd07da38c0e6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cfe03dc7efd5be8b8b6f705865273b86&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6b1619851fc851316cb57de0ea4fe27f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a5b3fa9ac1d3ce61d8dbdd21acb60d10&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a55275992e9d9009ddd4f2b26d4260c1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-646ed44ca6a37b5351e0aef0dd18547a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-40ee0f407a8a7e48b91d23a82652414f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-30758f9c11d60c2ad7dbc4a84ff615d7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f86521265de1eaaef568da798d2e3a53</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Nichols Plot">
  <data key="d0">Nichols Plot</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">A Nichols plot is a log-magnitude-versus-phase plot used in control systems to represent the frequency response of a system.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9f0dfc9266a7cbf0daf9292867c654cf</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Integral and Derivative Factors">
  <data key="d0">Integral and Derivative Factors</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Integral and Derivative Factors are components of a transfer function, represented as $(j\omega)^{\mp1}$, affecting the system's frequency response.&lt;SEP&gt;Integral and derivative factors are components of transfer functions that influence system behavior, represented as (jω)^±1 in frequency plots.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-550487a7cadc4b8eeb83211703d232ec&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4bf77eaf82ba369d832435b0e6f076eb</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="First-order Factors">
  <data key="d0">First-order Factors</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">First-order Factors are elements of a transfer function, expressed as $(1\,+\,j\omega T)^{\mp1}$, influencing the system's behavior at different frequencies.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4bf77eaf82ba369d832435b0e6f076eb</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Quadratic Factors">
  <data key="d0">Quadratic Factors</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Quadratic Factors are components of a transfer function, represented as $\left[1\,+\,2\zeta\bigl(j\omega/\omega_{n}\bigr)\,+\,\bigl(j\omega/\omega_{n}\bigr)^{2}\right]^{\mp1}$, affecting system dynamics.&lt;SEP&gt;Quadratic Factors in control systems are mathematical expressions that describe signal behavior with complex or real poles.&lt;SEP&gt;Quadratic factors are mathematical expressions that form part of the sinusoidal transfer function, affecting its behavior.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-182dc59d89ae078fb7ffb41be064595c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-98b7da91ec0504b2cb24aec37c61430a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4bf77eaf82ba369d832435b0e6f076eb</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Decibel Conversion Line">
  <data key="d0">Decibel Conversion Line</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The Decibel Conversion Line is a graphical tool used to convert numbers into decibels, illustrating the relationship between numerical values and their logarithmic representation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4bf77eaf82ba369d832435b0e6f076eb</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Logarithmic Scale">
  <data key="d0">Logarithmic Scale</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">A Logarithmic Scale is used in graphs to represent a wide range of values in a compact form, often employed in Bode diagrams for frequency analysis.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4bf77eaf82ba369d832435b0e6f076eb</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Magnitude Plot">
  <data key="d0">Magnitude Plot</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The Magnitude Plot is an integral component of the Bode Diagram, which is used to analyze the frequency response of systems. It displays the gain of the system, measured in decibels, as a function of frequency. This plot illustrates the amplitude of a system's output signal relative to frequency, often highlighting amplification or attenuation at different frequencies. The Magnitude Plot is presented on a semi-logarithmic scale, allowing for the observation of how the system's gain changes, typically decreasing with increasing frequency. Additionally, it showcases the magnitude of the transfer function, emphasizing peaks and slopes that characterize the system's response.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-289220cc5e6fcc41f8da8923ec0e0105&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2f074d90ecc0d7974d2a0b0c59285149&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-92e23c313eaf5a6d348bce8a1588be0f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-550487a7cadc4b8eeb83211703d232ec&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-618424e4625f7d33fa3ab142d292d0b7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-181fee5061e5d9adf0f04d196cc15e7f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6b139b34ebbeaf26c5893fedc131b125&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4f6dad58f78a125a7c454b0f2177f13a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ccb516dd7fa038fc65484f2cc9669722&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6b6f14da320af4579baf89f7ad82028e</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Phase Response">
  <data key="d0">Phase Response</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Phase Response is a crucial aspect of analyzing how a system's phase angle changes with frequency. It is typically represented as a graph or plot that displays the phase angle of the system, measured in degrees, against angular frequency (rad/sec). This graph helps in understanding the phase shift of a system's output relative to its input across various frequencies, thus indicating changes in system behavior. Phase Response is an integral component of the Bode diagram, which visually represents the system's phase shift in degrees across a range of frequencies. By examining the Phase Response, one can gain insights into the frequency response and phase change of the system, which are essential for assessing system performance and stability.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-016ebe44f8f410b4e420081ccf7a65f9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-98b7da91ec0504b2cb24aec37c61430a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9b87b5f3d20e81642454297b93cc16f3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6b139b34ebbeaf26c5893fedc131b125&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-484e9407831fd22fae06241d1b7e75e5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7d685d32ace4fbb1daf3dfa98d88fd29&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8c39c97551687b0bf865de0bebf31277</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Log Magnitude">
  <data key="d0">Log Magnitude</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Log magnitude is a logarithmic measure of the amplitude of a system's output, often expressed in decibels.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-6b139b34ebbeaf26c5893fedc131b125</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Cutoff Frequency">
  <data key="d0">Cutoff Frequency</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Cutoff Frequency is the frequency beyond which a system no longer responds effectively.&lt;SEP&gt;Cutoff frequency is the frequency at which a filter begins to significantly attenuate the input signal.&lt;SEP&gt;Cutoff frequency is the point at which the amplitude of a signal begins to decrease significantly, marking the limits of effective bandwidth.&lt;SEP&gt;The Cutoff Frequency is a system characteristic indicating the frequency at which the system begins to significantly attenuate signals.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-6b139b34ebbeaf26c5893fedc131b125&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9b87b5f3d20e81642454297b93cc16f3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-faf6d229bd33d9a677ddb2c4f3fe8552&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-27923b259bf2de83650da5b28f76726e</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="First-Order Factor">
  <data key="d0">First-Order Factor</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">A mathematical expression representing a system's response as a function of frequency, specifically $1/(1\,+\,j\omega T)$.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d8efef9175eeea628e4106a451e3ce5d</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Corner Frequency">
  <data key="d0">Corner Frequency</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Corner Frequency is a critical concept in system analysis and design, particularly in the context of filters and compensators. It is the frequency at which a system's response begins to change significantly, marking a transition in behavior. This frequency is crucial for designing compensators like lag-lead compensators, as it helps to set poles and zeros to manage phase margins and prevent phase lag. In the context of low-pass filters, the corner frequency is where the output power falls to half its passband value, indicating the boundary between passband and stopband. Additionally, it is the point where the asymptotes of a frequency-response curve intersect, signifying a change in the system's behavior. Understanding corner frequencies is essential for analyzing and designing systems to ensure desired performance across different frequency ranges.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5518828c63927a29cbd23a5354674537&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f22851addd0d0d43a22e89bf8e277faf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-29f2803ebeab2bb881d9725116174a56&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ae064a86d4a638278e16558779a2cbf9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2fac437c10d4b085f8ef3459b8dfdb4f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4a53699f6a5efb94ddd426141d45cba8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-faf6d229bd33d9a677ddb2c4f3fe8552&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7d685d32ace4fbb1daf3dfa98d88fd29&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d8efef9175eeea628e4106a451e3ce5d</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Log-Magnitude Curve">
  <data key="d0">Log-Magnitude Curve</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The Log-Magnitude Curve is a graphical representation of the magnitude response of a system, plotted on a logarithmic scale. It is an essential component of frequency response analysis, particularly as part of the Bode plot, illustrating the magnitude of a system's transfer function in decibels. This curve provides valuable insights into system behavior across different frequency ranges, showing the relationship between frequency and magnitude. By depicting the logarithmic magnitude of a system's frequency response, the Log-Magnitude Curve is used to assess the frequency characteristics and overall performance of the system.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-131d68e1d8fb1dac5267fbb38c0b52ef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3a5c8adb826b6a09285fe1ad5b9d9b8c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b2a20c61ffd29ee123aa4008d1f4ba5f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-484e9407831fd22fae06241d1b7e75e5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-955b6d1a9437f260081a7c8bfe86f0af&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d8efef9175eeea628e4106a451e3ce5d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ccfe5606d87942d95cd99b0924a1abcd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c16de2cbe7e947bb8bc1c1fd886e33c</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Asymptote">
  <data key="d0">Asymptote</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">A straight line on a graph that approaches a curve but never intersects it, used in the approximation of frequency-response curves.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d8efef9175eeea628e4106a451e3ce5d</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Exact Curve">
  <data key="d0">Exact Curve</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The exact curve serves as a reference for comparing other curves in the graph, highlighting deviations and similarities.&lt;SEP&gt;The precise representation of a system's response on a frequency-response graph, contrasting with asymptotic approximations.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-562db1bae3e6a30605a16f1c339c6e3d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d8efef9175eeea628e4106a451e3ce5d</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Frequency-Response Curve">
  <data key="d0">Frequency-Response Curve</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">A graphical representation showing how the amplitude and phase of a system's output vary with frequency.&lt;SEP&gt;The Frequency-Response Curve is a plot that represents how the amplitude and phase of a system's output signal vary with frequency, showing the system's response to different input frequencies.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5c5ea418170c18599f96d1d10d3ce8a3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4a53699f6a5efb94ddd426141d45cba8</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Asymptotic Expression">
  <data key="d0">Asymptotic Expression</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The Asymptotic Expression is an approximation technique used in Bode diagrams to simplify the representation of a system's frequency response, particularly at high frequencies.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4a53699f6a5efb94ddd426141d45cba8</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Bode Plot">
  <data key="d0">Bode Plot</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">A Bode plot is a graphical representation used in signal processing to illustrate a system's frequency response. It consists of two plots: one for magnitude and one for phase angle, showing how these parameters change across different frequencies. The Bode plot is instrumental in analyzing system characteristics such as stability, gain margin, and phase margin, providing insights into the gain and phase shift experienced by the system at various frequencies.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-98b7da91ec0504b2cb24aec37c61430a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2591937b2f7c7c5341658076669c1b02&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-550487a7cadc4b8eeb83211703d232ec&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-484e9407831fd22fae06241d1b7e75e5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-62181afdae55ce4e95ff620bfa6e1818&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c7a34174c65e8a6a1a11ac9ee67b0bbe&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6b6f14da320af4579baf89f7ad82028e</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Frequency Response Curve">
  <data key="d0">Frequency Response Curve</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The Frequency Response Curve represents the behavior of a system's output in response to different frequencies, with key features like asymptotes and resonant peaks.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7d95cccfff7173da89105e8a30a753bb</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Corner Frequency (ωₙ)">
  <data key="d0">Corner Frequency (ωₙ)</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The Corner Frequency (ωₙ) is the frequency at which the low-frequency and high-frequency asymptotes intersect, crucial for the quadratic factor analysis.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7d95cccfff7173da89105e8a30a753bb</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Quadratic Factor">
  <data key="d0">Quadratic Factor</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The Quadratic Factor is a mathematical expression used to describe the frequency response of a system, dependent on damping ratio and corner frequency.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7d95cccfff7173da89105e8a30a753bb</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Graph Image">
  <data key="d0">Graph Image</data>
  <data key="d1">event</data>
  <data key="d2">The Graph Image visually represents various mathematical functions, including frequency response curves, with colored lines and curves for comparison.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7d95cccfff7173da89105e8a30a753bb</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Quadratic Transfer Function">
  <data key="d0">Quadratic Transfer Function</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">A mathematical function used to describe the relationship between input and output in a system, particularly in control systems and signal processing.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5c5ea418170c18599f96d1d10d3ce8a3</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Corner Frequency (\(\omega_{n}\))">
  <data key="d0">Corner Frequency (\(\omega_{n}\))</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The frequency at which the magnitude of the system's response begins to decline, marking a transition point in the frequency-response curve.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5c5ea418170c18599f96d1d10d3ce8a3</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Resonant Frequency (\(\omega_{r}\))">
  <data key="d0">Resonant Frequency (\(\omega_{r}\))</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The frequency at which the system's amplitude response reaches its peak, influenced by the damping ratio.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5c5ea418170c18599f96d1d10d3ce8a3</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Resonant Peak Value (\(M_{r}\))">
  <data key="d0">Resonant Peak Value (\(M_{r}\))</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The maximum amplitude of the system's frequency response at the resonant frequency.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5c5ea418170c18599f96d1d10d3ce8a3</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Magnitude \(\left|G(j\omega)\right|\)">
  <data key="d0">Magnitude \(\left|G(j\omega)\right|\)</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The magnitude of the system's transfer function, indicating the amplitude of the output signal relative to the input signal at different frequencies.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5c5ea418170c18599f96d1d10d3ce8a3</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Equation (7–7)">
  <data key="d0">Equation (7–7)</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">An equation that describes the phase angle of a system in terms of frequency and damping ratio.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5c5ea418170c18599f96d1d10d3ce8a3</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Equation (7–10)">
  <data key="d0">Equation (7–10)</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">An equation used to determine the conditions for the resonant frequency in a system.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5c5ea418170c18599f96d1d10d3ce8a3</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Equation (7–12)">
  <data key="d0">Equation (7–12)</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">An equation that provides insight into the behavior of the system's response based on damping ratio and frequency.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5c5ea418170c18599f96d1d10d3ce8a3</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Bode Diagrams">
  <data key="d0">Bode Diagrams</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Bode diagrams are graphical representations used in engineering to analyze the frequency response of systems. They depict the magnitude and phase response as a function of frequency, which is essential for determining minimum-phase transfer functions. Bode diagrams are particularly useful in analyzing the gain margin and phase margin in control systems, as well as comparing the frequency response of different systems, such as systems U1 and U2. These diagrams are applicable to linear systems and are widely used to understand and visualize the magnitude and phase characteristics of a system's frequency response.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a104cc76c4ded119be1e066101146986&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0477d9985a7d5b2045fb80504b1c802a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c90d44afb796b38b91c19121ba0bf8c5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c0b45e8c05ce6d6a2e33cb4a65bc30d4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7d685d32ace4fbb1daf3dfa98d88fd29&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-193a843542d1b083c02404daa115957e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d64c265c70a4b363e906e1040d781b2b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c16de2cbe7e947bb8bc1c1fd886e33c</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Figure 7–10">
  <data key="d0">Figure 7–10</data>
  <data key="d1">event</data>
  <data key="d2">Figure 7–10 is referenced as a visual representation showing the relationship between the resonant peak magnitude and the damping ratio in a second-order system.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a104cc76c4ded119be1e066101146986</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Resonant Peak">
  <data key="d0">Resonant Peak</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Resonant peak is a point in the frequency response where the system exhibits a peak in magnitude, often due to specific pole-zero configurations.&lt;SEP&gt;The Resonant Peak refers to the maximum magnitude value in a system's frequency response, expressed in decibels.&lt;SEP&gt;The resonant peak is the maximum amplitude observed in a frequency response curve, affecting system stability.&lt;SEP&gt;The resonant peak refers to the maximum magnitude of a system's frequency response at its natural frequency, influenced by the damping ratio.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a104cc76c4ded119be1e066101146986&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0e2d5c8521bc1f35d5a5daaf0b7fe7eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cba4dc46aaf3979ba1619c43d08d1720&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-27923b259bf2de83650da5b28f76726e</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Magnitude G(jω)">
  <data key="d0">Magnitude G(jω)</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Magnitude G(jω) is a function representing the amplitude of the system's output at different frequencies, critical in frequency response analysis.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a104cc76c4ded119be1e066101146986</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Transfer Function G(jω)">
  <data key="d0">Transfer Function G(jω)</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">A mathematical representation of the system's output-to-input ratio in the frequency domain, critical for analyzing system behavior.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-ae064a86d4a638278e16558779a2cbf9</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Magnitude Curve">
  <data key="d0">Magnitude Curve</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">A curve representing the magnitude of the system's response at various frequencies, plotted on the Bode Diagram.&lt;SEP&gt;The Magnitude Curve in a Bode Diagram represents the system's gain across different frequencies.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-ae064a86d4a638278e16558779a2cbf9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-05bc9bb179b1e6a64609fa853f1c7706</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Phase-Angle Curve">
  <data key="d0">Phase-Angle Curve</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">A curve representing the phase angle of the system's response at various frequencies, plotted on the Bode Diagram.&lt;SEP&gt;The phase-angle curve is used to verify the transfer function by comparing experimental and theoretical phase angles in frequency response analysis.&lt;SEP&gt;The phase-angle curve represents the phase shift of the system's output relative to its input across frequencies.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f4c46665e037287407ddbff2463962aa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ae064a86d4a638278e16558779a2cbf9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c16de2cbe7e947bb8bc1c1fd886e33c</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
