  <data key="d2">A specific figure referenced in the text that illustrates the Bode Diagram with various curves for analysis.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-ae064a86d4a638278e16558779a2cbf9</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Minimum-Phase System">
  <data key="d0">Minimum-Phase System</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">A Minimum-Phase System is a type of system in control theory characterized by its stability due to having all poles and zeros in the left-half of the s-plane, ensuring that there are no poles or zeros in the right half of the s-plane. This configuration allows its open-loop transfer function to be uniquely determined from the magnitude curve, contributing to a stable system response. Minimum-phase systems are often used as a design criterion because both phase and gain margins must be positive for stability. Additionally, they are characterized by specific magnitude and phase relationships in their transfer functions, where the slope of the log-magnitude curve and the phase angle at high frequencies meet certain criteria, indicating a more stable response.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-131d68e1d8fb1dac5267fbb38c0b52ef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c90d44afb796b38b91c19121ba0bf8c5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6a256259f60bf17c11f960427ffa11ff&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a3d1b21ce83b859c71c888d5c7aae6ba&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-562db1bae3e6a30605a16f1c339c6e3d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-52feaf2b43bc62724214f66d8266b585</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Fourier Transform Pairs">
  <data key="d0">Fourier Transform Pairs</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Mathematical equations showing the relationship between time-domain and frequency-domain representations of signals.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-562db1bae3e6a30605a16f1c339c6e3d</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
