  <data key="d0">Minimum-Phase Transfer Function</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Transfer functions with no poles or zeros in the right-half s plane, allowing unique determination from the magnitude curve.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-562db1bae3e6a30605a16f1c339c6e3d</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Nonminimum-Phase Transfer Function">
  <data key="d0">Nonminimum-Phase Transfer Function</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">A nonminimum-phase transfer function is a type of system response that can exhibit certain phase characteristics not aligned with typical minimum-phase systems.&lt;SEP&gt;Transfer functions with poles and/or zeros in the right-half s plane, resulting in non-unique phase-angle characteristics.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0477d9985a7d5b2045fb80504b1c802a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-562db1bae3e6a30605a16f1c339c6e3d</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="All-Pass Filters">
  <data key="d0">All-Pass Filters</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Filters that maintain the magnitude curve of a transfer function while altering its phase curve.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-562db1bae3e6a30605a16f1c339c6e3d</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="System G1(jω)">
  <data key="d0">System G1(jω)</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">A sinusoidal transfer function given by G1(jω) = (1 + jωT) / (1 + jωT1), representing a minimum-phase system.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-562db1bae3e6a30605a16f1c339c6e3d</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="System G2(jω)">
  <data key="d0">System G2(jω)</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">A sinusoidal transfer function given by G2(jω) = (1 - jωT) / (1 + jωT1), representing a nonminimum-phase system.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-562db1bae3e6a30605a16f1c339c6e3d</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Transport Lag">
  <data key="d0">Transport Lag</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Transport Lag, also known as dead time, is a delay in the response of a system to an input. This phenomenon is often characterized by changes in phase angle and is typically represented in transfer functions as an exponential term, specifically the transcendental function e^{-Ts}. This delay is critical in control systems as it affects the stability, particularly when the gain (K) exceeds 2.65. Transport Lag is a nonminimum-phase behavior that introduces excessive phase lag without attenuation at high frequencies, which can significantly impact system stability. It is prevalent in various systems, including thermal, hydraulic, and pneumatic systems, and arises due to the time taken for a signal to travel through the system. Understanding and approximating Transport Lag is vital for stability analysis and ensuring the effective operation of control systems.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-131d68e1d8fb1dac5267fbb38c0b52ef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b2a20c61ffd29ee123aa4008d1f4ba5f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fb053307052f18015012648da15e3c35&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-75625ca25735063f07915ffbf3fb4581&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-76155cfacd298ba99866d06587bc0e7d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f59f8623e4ff8e64d49175daeeadad05&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-955b6d1a9437f260081a7c8bfe86f0af&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1fc8172454011b7e111636e450b0431d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-50c4cfa192898028842df51ccba67794</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="G1(jω)">
  <data key="d0">G1(jω)</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">G1(jω) is a transfer function with phase angles starting at 0° at low frequencies and increasing to approximately 45° as frequency approaches infinity.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-131d68e1d8fb1dac5267fbb38c0b52ef</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
