<node id="Gain Margin">
  <data key="d0">Gain Margin</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Gain Margin is a critical measure of system stability in control systems, expressed in decibels. It indicates the amount by which the system's gain can be increased before the system becomes unstable. A desired gain margin is typically 10 dB or more, ensuring the system can handle gain variations without losing stability. This measure is essential in control system design, as it specifies the system's ability to tolerate increases in gain and is often used alongside phase margin to assess the degree of damping in a system. Gain Margin represents the difference in gain between the phase crossover point and the critical point, and is noted as +∞ dB in some contexts, indicating robust stability. Overall, Gain Margin is integral to understanding and ensuring the stability of control systems by quantifying how much gain increase can be tolerated before the onset of instability.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-846bca86e18f84abac3699480d87b280&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-87b0e0bfbf5774f7c90a99f131e0bb53&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fd21bbb7bb0f22b9146dce0bacde7f33&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-50c4cfa192898028842df51ccba67794&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4e42383167231f90ca804ba822f6b73d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2fac437c10d4b085f8ef3459b8dfdb4f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-926952abfdc3443e6470e2a990053a6c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9e943fefbd15dc3bfc97bcb9515cd1eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-484e9407831fd22fae06241d1b7e75e5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b467c546fd593046d07eac1e9826b33c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7bfba099219ef0865c8cfbb638e366f1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e0c2c5d2084cf4dc42eac20165445910&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ff7353abf978480820979c540d814ee3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-016ebe44f8f410b4e420081ccf7a65f9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-289220cc5e6fcc41f8da8923ec0e0105&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8baf5807e5435582388b6ba8ea165e4d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2dd6d60316691290acdbc20600569961&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1c56a057e86a61de468030b87f550fa4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-189305ffdfb5a3f7a9695e56559bcef3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8221452e166faf8569286a459dc76b3e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-05bc9bb179b1e6a64609fa853f1c7706&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-36751d4e3f942193eb2e541b3e4dd23b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-97ff6cf0253e8f192c45acefe4824e0b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c90d44afb796b38b91c19121ba0bf8c5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ab1222a4f68b9900993c6bdde861432b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-92e23c313eaf5a6d348bce8a1588be0f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bca6ad29eb261282c2df664425ef7d0b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-731aadaa135cc2ef1c9482aab829f4e1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-30758f9c11d60c2ad7dbc4a84ff615d7</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Phase Margin">
  <data key="d0">Phase Margin</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Phase Margin is a critical measure of system stability in control systems, representing the degree to which a system can tolerate phase variations before becoming unstable. It is expressed in degrees and indicates the difference between the phase angle of the system's open-loop transfer function and -180 degrees at the gain crossover frequency. This measure is frequently specified in practical design problems to assess the system's ability to withstand changes in system parameters and ensure robustness against oscillations.

Phase Margin is desired to be approximately 50 degrees in this context, although specifications in the control system range from 50 to 60 degrees. It indicates the margin of additional phase shift required to bring the system to the verge of instability, representing the degree of phase lead or lag needed to reach critical stability. A negative phase margin, such as -16 degrees, indicates instability in the system.

Overall, Phase Margin serves as a key specification in control system design, providing insights into the system's damping, stability, and robustness against parameter changes.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-846bca86e18f84abac3699480d87b280&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-87b0e0bfbf5774f7c90a99f131e0bb53&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fd21bbb7bb0f22b9146dce0bacde7f33&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-50c4cfa192898028842df51ccba67794&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4e42383167231f90ca804ba822f6b73d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-29f2803ebeab2bb881d9725116174a56&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2f074d90ecc0d7974d2a0b0c59285149&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2fac437c10d4b085f8ef3459b8dfdb4f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-926952abfdc3443e6470e2a990053a6c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9e943fefbd15dc3bfc97bcb9515cd1eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-484e9407831fd22fae06241d1b7e75e5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b467c546fd593046d07eac1e9826b33c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7bfba099219ef0865c8cfbb638e366f1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4e3fcd1b5ae55946bb03cb8fa5a6859d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e0c2c5d2084cf4dc42eac20165445910&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ff7353abf978480820979c540d814ee3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f22851addd0d0d43a22e89bf8e277faf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-016ebe44f8f410b4e420081ccf7a65f9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-289220cc5e6fcc41f8da8923ec0e0105&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8baf5807e5435582388b6ba8ea165e4d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2dd6d60316691290acdbc20600569961&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1c56a057e86a61de468030b87f550fa4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-189305ffdfb5a3f7a9695e56559bcef3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4ef7861303c3473cd3d47c68848af64d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8221452e166faf8569286a459dc76b3e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-05bc9bb179b1e6a64609fa853f1c7706&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-36751d4e3f942193eb2e541b3e4dd23b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-97ff6cf0253e8f192c45acefe4824e0b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c90d44afb796b38b91c19121ba0bf8c5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ab1222a4f68b9900993c6bdde861432b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-92e23c313eaf5a6d348bce8a1588be0f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-471b11b0e8da61744401ac854b4948f8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bca6ad29eb261282c2df664425ef7d0b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-731aadaa135cc2ef1c9482aab829f4e1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-30758f9c11d60c2ad7dbc4a84ff615d7</data>
