  <data key="d3">chunk-5ce576a87f38c3c3621dc3b3d8f16913</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="State Space System">
  <data key="d0">State Space System</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">A State Space System is defined by matrices and vectors representing the state, control, output, and transmission characteristics of a dynamic system.&lt;SEP&gt;State Space System is a mathematical model of a physical system represented by matrices defining its state, control, output, and direct transmission.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b30d3f27196cdc41b81a736db5f1aee7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5ce576a87f38c3c3621dc3b3d8f16913</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Phase Shift">
  <data key="d0">Phase Shift</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Phase Shift indicates the change in phase angle of a system's output signal relative to its input, varying with frequency.&lt;SEP&gt;Phase shift in the graph indicates the transition of lines from positive to negative values at regular intervals, suggesting periodicity.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-ab1222a4f68b9900993c6bdde861432b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5ce576a87f38c3c3621dc3b3d8f16913</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Polar Plot">
  <data key="d0">Polar Plot</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The Polar Plot is a graphical representation commonly used in control systems to visualize the frequency response of a system. It displays the magnitude and phase of a system's transfer function on a polar coordinate system, often illustrating the relationship between gain and frequency. This type of plot is particularly useful for analyzing complex functions, such as sinusoidal transfer functions, and is known for showing semicircular patterns in the frequency domain. Additionally, polar plots, sometimes referred to as Nyquist plots, are employed to determine the closed-loop frequency response and to illustrate the behavior of control systems in terms of magnitude and phase angle. They also help in identifying the resonant peak and resonant frequency (ωr) of a system. Overall, polar plots serve as a vital tool in complex analysis and signal processing, providing insights into the magnitude and phase relationships within a system.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a3d1b21ce83b859c71c888d5c7aae6ba&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-87b0e0bfbf5774f7c90a99f131e0bb53&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-62181afdae55ce4e95ff620bfa6e1818&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4f64aff6c5ff2d6c3093c613b43ba0c4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-182dc59d89ae078fb7ffb41be064595c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1022a5cf276c07ee56ffc50c13799b6d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-004c5161c3b1d015eb48ffa3f8bf337c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-550487a7cadc4b8eeb83211703d232ec&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-15b91f5c5b4a075330ef41ad3ec74fe9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a480968a716a749c7646332b8d7dec12&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fbfe186ba42a9341b463befd113d8ee4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-914a61458271e99a6bf821d8e99ad4cf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2dd6d60316691290acdbc20600569961&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f86521265de1eaaef568da798d2e3a53&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6b1619851fc851316cb57de0ea4fe27f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-691177b7d933c82cade5a9fd3f182f4a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-30758f9c11d60c2ad7dbc4a84ff615d7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6d35fea10973c2b24ef34a97da4f0e8b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6b6f14da320af4579baf89f7ad82028e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5027e7ae655fabb2069b339ee5f89f71</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Phase Plot">
  <data key="d0">Phase Plot</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The Phase Plot is an integral component of the Bode Diagram, which is used to analyze the frequency response of a system. It specifically illustrates the phase shift between the input and output signals as a function of frequency. This shift is measured in degrees and displayed on a semi-logarithmic scale. The Phase Plot effectively shows the phase angle of the system's transfer function, indicating how the phase angle changes across a range of frequencies. By visualizing these changes, the Phase Plot provides valuable insights into the system's phase response, helping to understand the dynamic behavior of the system over different frequencies.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-289220cc5e6fcc41f8da8923ec0e0105&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2f074d90ecc0d7974d2a0b0c59285149&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-92e23c313eaf5a6d348bce8a1588be0f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-550487a7cadc4b8eeb83211703d232ec&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-618424e4625f7d33fa3ab142d292d0b7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-181fee5061e5d9adf0f04d196cc15e7f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ccb516dd7fa038fc65484f2cc9669722&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fbfe186ba42a9341b463befd113d8ee4</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="First-Order Factors">
  <data key="d0">First-Order Factors</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">First-order factors are components of transfer functions, represented as (1 + jωT)^±1, affecting the system's response to frequency changes.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-550487a7cadc4b8eeb83211703d232ec</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Frequency (ω)">
  <data key="d0">Frequency (ω)</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Frequency (ω) is a parameter that describes the rate of oscillation and is crucial in analyzing polar plots and transfer functions.&lt;SEP&gt;Frequency, denoted as ω, is a variable related to angular velocity, affecting the magnitude and phase of the transfer function.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-182dc59d89ae078fb7ffb41be064595c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-914a61458271e99a6bf821d8e99ad4cf</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Radius">
  <data key="d0">Radius</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Radius refers to the distance from the center to any point on the circle in polar plots, illustrating the magnitude of the transfer function.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-914a61458271e99a6bf821d8e99ad4cf</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Semicircle">
  <data key="d0">Semicircle</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">A semicircle is a half-circle used in diagrams to represent complex numbers and their relationships in the polar plot.&lt;SEP&gt;The semicircle is the shape formed by the polar plot of the transfer function, indicating its magnitude and phase characteristics over varying frequencies.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-84a06449dbf5e39bcd3e56867b9d2ab2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-914a61458271e99a6bf821d8e99ad4cf</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Resonant Frequency (ωr)">
  <data key="d0">Resonant Frequency (ωr)</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The resonant frequency (ωr) is the frequency at which the amplitude of a system's response is at its maximum, important in the context of polar plots.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-182dc59d89ae078fb7ffb41be064595c</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Resonant Frequency ωr">
  <data key="d0">Resonant Frequency ωr</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Resonant Frequency ωr refers to the frequency at which the system's response reaches its peak amplitude.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-691177b7d933c82cade5a9fd3f182f4a</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Undamped Natural Frequency ωn">
  <data key="d0">Undamped Natural Frequency ωn</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Undamped Natural Frequency ωn is the frequency at which the system oscillates when there is no damping.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-691177b7d933c82cade5a9fd3f182f4a</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
