<node id="R(s) (Reference Input)">
  <data key="d0">R(s) (Reference Input)</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Represents the reference input or setpoint for the system, providing the desired value the system aims to track.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-136efeec2362cbbe6ae14a66f80a408f</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="G(s) (Controller Transfer Function)">
  <data key="d0">G(s) (Controller Transfer Function)</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Denotes the transfer function of the controller, processing the error signal to generate control action based on the difference between reference input and actual output.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-136efeec2362cbbe6ae14a66f80a408f</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="C(s) (Plant or Process)">
  <data key="d0">C(s) (Plant or Process)</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Represents the plant or process being controlled, responding to the control action from the controller, influenced by its own dynamics and characteristics.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-136efeec2362cbbe6ae14a66f80a408f</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="H. Nyquist">
  <data key="d0">H. Nyquist</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Developed the Nyquist stability criterion, which relates open-loop frequency response to the stability of closed-loop systems.&lt;SEP&gt;H. Nyquist is known for his work on regeneration theory.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-136efeec2362cbbe6ae14a66f80a408f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-60880254c6a30ba36adce6783cec8116</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
