  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">A complex number located at point (0, j2) with a real part of 0 and an imaginary part of 2j.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-136efeec2362cbbe6ae14a66f80a408f</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Complex Number 2">
  <data key="d0">Complex Number 2</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">A complex number located at point (-1, -j1) with a real part of -1 and an imaginary part of -j1.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-136efeec2362cbbe6ae14a66f80a408f</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="s Plane">
  <data key="d0">s Plane</data>
  <data key="d1">geo</data>
  <data key="d2">The s Plane is a coordinate system used for plotting functions and curves in mathematical diagrams, particularly related to complex functions.&lt;SEP&gt;The s plane is a mathematical concept used to represent complex numbers, with real and imaginary axes.&lt;SEP&gt;s Plane represents the complex plane in which control system stability analysis is performed, including real and imaginary components.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9afe985fc7dab84bc86b6f660723b27e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-814b833e231cf6e96b1f333e7eb5bbc4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-75625ca25735063f07915ffbf3fb4581</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="F(s) Plane">
  <data key="d0">F(s) Plane</data>
  <data key="d1">geo</data>
  <data key="d2">The F(s) plane is a transformed space where complex number mappings occur, based on the function F(s).</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9afe985fc7dab84bc86b6f660723b27e</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Mapping Theorem">
  <data key="d0">Mapping Theorem</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The Mapping Theorem is a mathematical concept used to relate the number of zeros and poles within a closed contour in the s-plane and their encirclements in the F(s) plane.&lt;SEP&gt;The mapping theorem explains the relationship between contours in the s plane and their corresponding curves in the F(s) plane, crucial for understanding stability in complex systems.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9afe985fc7dab84bc86b6f660723b27e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-83bded94a3ff142ba41772e1b37f4f63</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Circle Arrows">
  <data key="d0">Circle Arrows</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Circle arrows indicate the direction of values on the axes, with arrows pointing left for negative real values and up for positive imaginary values.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9afe985fc7dab84bc86b6f660723b27e</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Equation (7–15)">
  <data key="d0">Equation (7–15)</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Equation (7–15) is the characteristic equation for the function F(s) used in complex mappings.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9afe985fc7dab84bc86b6f660723b27e</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Nyquist Path">
  <data key="d0">Nyquist Path</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">A path in the complex plane used to plot the Nyquist plot, typically a semicircle with specific orientation and direction.&lt;SEP&gt;A path in the s plane used for analyzing the stability and frequency response of a system.&lt;SEP&gt;The Nyquist Path is a contour used in control system analysis, enclosing the right-half s-plane to assess system stability.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-40ee0f407a8a7e48b91d23a82652414f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-021d406fb1875a04e6ca85d6a4589897&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-83bded94a3ff142ba41772e1b37f4f63</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Closed Contour">
  <data key="d0">Closed Contour</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">A closed contour is a path in the complex plane that starts and ends at the same point, often used in evaluating integrals.&lt;SEP&gt;A closed contour is a path in the s-plane that forms a loop, used in mapping theorems to analyze poles and zeros.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4a3142f3de4d17a618aa6820c7f8e32a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-83bded94a3ff142ba41772e1b37f4f63</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Real Part">
  <data key="d0">Real Part</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The real part of a complex number is the component that can be represented on the x-axis in a rectangular coordinate system.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-83bded94a3ff142ba41772e1b37f4f63</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Imaginary Part">
  <data key="d0">Imaginary Part</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The imaginary part of a complex number is the component that can be represented on the y-axis in a rectangular coordinate system.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-83bded94a3ff142ba41772e1b37f4f63</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="F(s)">
  <data key="d0">F(s)</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">F(s) is a complex function that can be expressed in terms of its magnitude and phase, often analyzed in the context of poles and zeros.&lt;SEP&gt;F(s) is a function representing the ratio of two polynomials in s, used in the mapping theorem for analyzing complex equations.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4a3142f3de4d17a618aa6820c7f8e32a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-83bded94a3ff142ba41772e1b37f4f63</data>
