  <data key="d1">event</data>
  <data key="d2">Figure 7–61 shows the Nyquist plot of G(jω)/K, used to determine the range of gain K for system stability.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a480968a716a749c7646332b8d7dec12</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Inverse Polar Plots">
  <data key="d0">Inverse Polar Plots</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Inverse Polar Plots are graphical representations used in control systems to analyze the behavior of inverse transfer functions, particularly in the context of the Nyquist Stability Criterion.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f59f8623e4ff8e64d49175daeeadad05</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Unity Feedback System">
  <data key="d0">Unity Feedback System</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">A Unity Feedback System is a control system configuration where the feedback loop has a gain of one, simplifying stability analysis.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-52feaf2b43bc62724214f66d8266b585</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Conformal Mapping">
  <data key="d0">Conformal Mapping</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Conformal Mapping is a mathematical technique used to preserve angles and shapes in complex analysis.&lt;SEP&gt;Conformal Mapping is a mathematical technique used to transform complex plane figures, aiding in the analysis of control systems.&lt;SEP&gt;Conformal mapping is a mathematical technique used to transform complex functions while preserving angles.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-faf6d229bd33d9a677ddb2c4f3fe8552&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2160cb6aaf30b68e3cbc695bfa7548cd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-52feaf2b43bc62724214f66d8266b585</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="G-Plane">
  <data key="d0">G-Plane</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The G-Plane is a transformed representation of the S-Plane, used to illustrate relationships between complex numbers in control systems.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-52feaf2b43bc62724214f66d8266b585</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Open-Loop Frequency-Response">
  <data key="d0">Open-Loop Frequency-Response</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Open-Loop Frequency-Response refers to the characteristics of a system's output in response to varying input frequencies, used to estimate closed-loop poles.&lt;SEP&gt;Open-loop frequency-response refers to the behavior of a system when there is no feedback loop, often visualized through graphs to assess stability and performance.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-bc203beda6b14fd1cf52a94919ab90a6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-52feaf2b43bc62724214f66d8266b585</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="G(s) Plane">
  <data key="d0">G(s) Plane</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The G(s) Plane is a mathematical plane where the constant M circles are plotted, showing their geometric properties.&lt;SEP&gt;The G(s) plane is used to map complex functions for analysis of stability and dynamics in control systems.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-2160cb6aaf30b68e3cbc695bfa7548cd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-68abbb58b262f59e481e8f8f8079891e</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Step Transient Response">
  <data key="d0">Step Transient Response</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Step transient response refers to how a system reacts over time to a sudden change in input.&lt;SEP&gt;The reaction of a system to a step input, focusing on how the system transitions to a new steady state.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-2160cb6aaf30b68e3cbc695bfa7548cd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5ccf7d143be8a45626048d565c73daab</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Real and Imaginary Axes">
  <data key="d0">Real and Imaginary Axes</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The real and imaginary axes are components of a complex plane, helping visualize complex numbers.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-2160cb6aaf30b68e3cbc695bfa7548cd</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Phase and Gain Margins">
  <data key="d0">Phase and Gain Margins</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Phase and Gain Margins are measures of system robustness in frequency-response analysis, indicating stability against modeling errors.&lt;SEP&gt;Phase and gain margins are measures of system robustness in control theory, derived from polar plots.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-2160cb6aaf30b68e3cbc695bfa7548cd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-16d9cf4a88154292d8f7994b72a870f0</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="G Plane">
  <data key="d0">G Plane</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The G Plane is a complex plane used in control system analysis, illustrating the relationship between gain and frequency.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-2dd6d60316691290acdbc20600569961</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Stable System">
  <data key="d0">Stable System</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">A stable system is characterized by positive gain and phase margins, indicating that it can handle increases in gain without becoming unstable.&lt;SEP&gt;A stable system is characterized by positive phase margin and appropriate gain margin, ensuring system stability.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-2dd6d60316691290acdbc20600569961&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c90d44afb796b38b91c19121ba0bf8c5</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Open-loop Gain K">
  <data key="d0">Open-loop Gain K</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Open-loop gain K is a parameter that affects system stability, with large values leading to instability.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-2dd6d60316691290acdbc20600569961</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Phase Crossover Frequency">
  <data key="d0">Phase Crossover Frequency</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Phase crossover frequencies are specific points in a frequency response plot where the phase angle crosses the 0° mark.&lt;SEP&gt;The frequency at which the phase angle of the open-loop transfer function equals -180°, critical for determining system stability.&lt;SEP&gt;The phase crossover frequency is the frequency at which the phase angle is critical for stability, identified as 2 rad/sec in the system.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-29f2803ebeab2bb881d9725116174a56&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c90d44afb796b38b91c19121ba0bf8c5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6a256259f60bf17c11f960427ffa11ff</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Gain Crossover Frequency">
  <data key="d0">Gain Crossover Frequency</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The Gain Crossover Frequency is a critical frequency in control systems, defined as the point at which the magnitude of the system's transfer function or open-loop transfer function equals unity, or 0 dB. This frequency is pivotal for analyzing system stability, determining phase margin, and influencing the design of components such as lag-lead compensators. By marking the specific points in a frequency response plot where the gain crosses the 0 dB threshold, the Gain Crossover Frequency plays a crucial role in assessing stability margins and ensuring the system remains stable.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f22851addd0d0d43a22e89bf8e277faf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c90d44afb796b38b91c19121ba0bf8c5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-96a98f812e32e184c15978c0d7771176&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6a256259f60bf17c11f960427ffa11ff&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-29f2803ebeab2bb881d9725116174a56&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8221452e166faf8569286a459dc76b3e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4ef7861303c3473cd3d47c68848af64d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-60ef1c3f8eed32705fec8c85d559930a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-471b11b0e8da61744401ac854b4948f8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-05bc9bb179b1e6a64609fa853f1c7706&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7d685d32ace4fbb1daf3dfa98d88fd29&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4e3fcd1b5ae55946bb03cb8fa5a6859d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-36751d4e3f942193eb2e541b3e4dd23b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-50c4cfa192898028842df51ccba67794</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Graph 4">
  <data key="d0">Graph 4</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Graph 4 displays a pattern similar to Graph 3 but peaks near t = 1 second with a function value just above y = 0.5.&lt;SEP&gt;Graph 4 features a line graph with a curve that increases from left to right, with no clear pattern.&lt;SEP&gt;Graph 4 is a graphical representation featuring x-axis values between -0.2 and 8 and y-axis values spanning from -3 to 2, with three distinct lines exhibiting unique characteristics.&lt;SEP&gt;Graph 4 is a logarithmic graph illustrating the relationship between loop gain and phase margin in control systems.&lt;SEP&gt;Graph 4 represents the function x4 plotted against time, showing a single peak at t=2 and oscillating between -1.5 and 1.5.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c90d44afb796b38b91c19121ba0bf8c5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-485e298f6b5c334208195cfaf790e781&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bae8df079707c93331cb95e16abe02aa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e7ee96b6c7a32a3b2a58081228221eda&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d1c6b6475b03e27e6db3ac883a90115c</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Log-Magnitude Versus Phase Plots">
  <data key="d0">Log-Magnitude Versus Phase Plots</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Log-magnitude versus phase plots are used to analyze the stability of control systems through gain and phase margins.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c90d44afb796b38b91c19121ba0bf8c5</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Phase Crossover Frequency ω1">
  <data key="d0">Phase Crossover Frequency ω1</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The phase crossover frequency ω1 is the frequency at which the phase angle of the open-loop transfer function equals -180°.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c90d44afb796b38b91c19121ba0bf8c5</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Phase Crossover Frequency ω2">
  <data key="d0">Phase Crossover Frequency ω2</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The phase crossover frequency ω2 is the second frequency point in the phase crossover analysis.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c90d44afb796b38b91c19121ba0bf8c5</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Conditionally Stable Systems">
  <data key="d0">Conditionally Stable Systems</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Conditionally stable systems have two or more phase crossover frequencies and may require specific conditions to remain stable.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c90d44afb796b38b91c19121ba0bf8c5</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Nyquist Diagram">
  <data key="d0">Nyquist Diagram</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Nyquist diagrams are used to assess the stability of systems, particularly nonminimum-phase systems.&lt;SEP&gt;The Nyquist Diagram is a polar plot used to analyze the stability of control systems by representing complex numbers in the complex plane.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-bc420a4e236b18e1114725fd8e9d12b5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6a256259f60bf17c11f960427ffa11ff</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Output Signal">
  <data key="d0">Output Signal</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The Output Signal is a crucial component in control systems, representing the system's response to control inputs. It is a scalar result produced by the type 1 servo system, reflecting the system's behavior over time, and is denoted as y, C(s), or c(t) depending on the context. The Output Signal is the final signal produced after processing and integration in the signal processing flowchart, and it is affected by disturbances and noise. In the control system, it is the controlled variable that the PID controller regulates, resulting from the PID controller's processing of the input signal. Additionally, the Output Signal is produced by the plant in response to the control signal and is used by the observer to estimate the state.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-6a256259f60bf17c11f960427ffa11ff&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7cb24308be90f4de9c5775f0a3a25ce9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0ae81e09d61ed423567970de31eac0a6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c0dd560e406cc5769efa44c166402303&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c90a28b31b0ff2333ffc79bc30b633af&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bb690227c0aeb29078796de6d1611d85&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-31df32fb8d3f0302ba80571341b33840&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a12a1fcc8b96ef105600adda81cf5b8f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-87f3dae9453dc00aeaa24b0a25b4f9e5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3ad73b7beb45180631dd72fbab2b6c9b</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Phase-Crossover Frequency">
  <data key="d0">Phase-Crossover Frequency</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The frequency at which the phase angle of the system is -180 degrees, used in stability analysis.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-1c56a057e86a61de468030b87f550fa4</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Gain-Crossover Frequency">
  <data key="d0">Gain-Crossover Frequency</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The frequency at which the magnitude of the system's frequency response is 0 dB, used in stability analysis.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-1c56a057e86a61de468030b87f550fa4</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Frequency Response Plots">
  <data key="d0">Frequency Response Plots</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
