  <data key="d0">Gain Parameter</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The gain parameter is a variable that affects the magnitude and phase of a system's frequency response in a Bode diagram.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-fbfe186ba42a9341b463befd113d8ee4</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Cauchy's Theorem">
  <data key="d0">Cauchy's Theorem</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Cauchy's theorem is a fundamental theorem in complex analysis stating that the integral of an analytic function over a closed contour is zero.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-fbfe186ba42a9341b463befd113d8ee4</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Residue Theorem">
  <data key="d0">Residue Theorem</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The residue theorem is a powerful tool in complex analysis used to evaluate contour integrals by relating them to the sum of residues at poles within the contour.&lt;SEP&gt;The residue theorem is a principle in complex analysis used to evaluate contour integrals by considering the residues of poles within the contour.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-fbfe186ba42a9341b463befd113d8ee4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4a3142f3de4d17a618aa6820c7f8e32a</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Complex Analysis">
  <data key="d0">Complex Analysis</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Complex analysis is a branch of mathematics focusing on functions of complex numbers and their applications.&lt;SEP&gt;Complex analysis is a branch of mathematics that studies functions of complex variables and includes concepts such as poles, zeros, and contour integration.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-6b1619851fc851316cb57de0ea4fe27f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4a3142f3de4d17a618aa6820c7f8e32a</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Contour in the s-plane">
  <data key="d0">Contour in the s-plane</data>
  <data key="d1">geo</data>
  <data key="d2">In complex analysis, the contour in the s-plane refers to a closed path along which integrals are evaluated, often enclosing poles and zeros of a function.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4a3142f3de4d17a618aa6820c7f8e32a</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Zero of k-th Order">
  <data key="d0">Zero of k-th Order</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">A zero of k-th order refers to a point where a function has a zero with multiplicity k, meaning the function and its first k-1 derivatives are zero at that point.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4a3142f3de4d17a618aa6820c7f8e32a</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Simple Pole">
  <data key="d0">Simple Pole</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">A simple pole is a point where a complex function goes to infinity in such a way that it behaves like 1/(s-a) near the point.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4a3142f3de4d17a618aa6820c7f8e32a</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Analytic Function">
  <data key="d0">Analytic Function</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">An analytic function is a complex function that is differentiable at every point in its domain.&lt;SEP&gt;An analytic function is a complex function that is differentiable in a region, satisfying the Cauchy–Riemann conditions.&lt;SEP&gt;An analytic function is a function that is differentiable at every point in its domain.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-284ad82d5a54aa2c3d44aa8399b9ac0c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4a3142f3de4d17a618aa6820c7f8e32a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-989ef45348532327c1757ed1c157833e</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Origin Encirclement">
