<node id="Controllability">
  <data key="d0">Controllability</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">A concept in control systems indicating the ability to control the state of a system through its inputs.&lt;SEP&gt;Controllability is a property of a control system that indicates whether the system's state can be driven to a desired state using available inputs.&lt;SEP&gt;Controllability is a property of a system that determines whether its internal state can be driven to a desired state using appropriate inputs.&lt;SEP&gt;Controllability is the ability to guide a system's state to a desired state through inputs.&lt;SEP&gt;Controllability refers to a system's ability to reach a desired state from any initial state through suitable inputs.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-faf6d229bd33d9a677ddb2c4f3fe8552&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-56a514da1cb0f88116234689304bc8d7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-952ac88fc20d94741d460ff36bcefd2c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bb63a19b57948f30bda30ff5517c1eba&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ad1dc64fc9b2da439067495b6c1bf166</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Observability">
  <data key="d0">Observability</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">A concept in control systems indicating the ability to infer the internal state of a system from its outputs.&lt;SEP&gt;Observability is a property of a system that indicates whether the internal states of the system can be determined by observing its outputs over time.&lt;SEP&gt;Observability is the ability to deduce the internal state of a system based on its outputs.&lt;SEP&gt;Observability is the ability to determine the state of a system from its outputs over time.&lt;SEP&gt;Observability is the ability to infer the entire state of a system from its outputs, crucial for system monitoring and control.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e5be935af21c311ce3f7db40fa58876e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3402b465fcfb648982e49c37a455c189&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-952ac88fc20d94741d460ff36bcefd2c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ba9e6dce047704f9bd794f5f4ed1e2a7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bb63a19b57948f30bda30ff5517c1eba</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Canonical Forms">
  <data key="d0">Canonical Forms</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Canonical Forms are standardized representations in control theory, including controllable, diagonal, Jordan, and observable forms.&lt;SEP&gt;Different structured forms of state-space representation, including controllable, observable, and diagonal forms.&lt;SEP&gt;Various standard forms of state-space equations used for system representation and analysis.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-faf6d229bd33d9a677ddb2c4f3fe8552&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e37fd32e67cfb4b06fcec5633146e195&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-952ac88fc20d94741d460ff36bcefd2c</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Two-degrees-of-freedom Control System">
  <data key="d0">Two-degrees-of-freedom Control System</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">A control system design that allows independent adjustment of two variables, enhancing flexibility and performance in signal processing.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-952ac88fc20d94741d460ff36bcefd2c</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="State-space Analysis">
  <data key="d0">State-space Analysis</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">A method for analyzing control systems using state variables and vector-matrix notation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-952ac88fc20d94741d460ff36bcefd2c</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Control Systems Analysis">
  <data key="d0">Control Systems Analysis</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Control Systems Analysis involves examining the behavior and stability of systems using mathematical models, particularly in state space.&lt;SEP&gt;Control Systems Analysis involves studying and designing systems that regulate themselves to achieve desired outputs, often using state space methods.&lt;SEP&gt;Control Systems Analysis involves the study of dynamic systems and their behavior using mathematical models and techniques.&lt;SEP&gt;Control Systems Analysis refers to the study of systems that manage, command, direct, or regulate the behavior of other devices or systems using control loops.&lt;SEP&gt;The study of control systems to understand and improve their functionality and performance.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9e97cb703344a8d83dd7f1085591043d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6d8c0bd0542b3fd879eac68953207dee&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c5e9c5e918d686131ecabed119aa3d7f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ba4c929673f3e997b800f45ab5b8f8b5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-952ac88fc20d94741d460ff36bcefd2c</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="System Models">
  <data key="d0">System Models</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Mathematical representations of systems used to analyze and predict system behavior.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-952ac88fc20d94741d460ff36bcefd2c</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Vector-Matrix Differential Equation">
  <data key="d0">Vector-Matrix Differential Equation</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">A mathematical equation involving vectors and matrices, used to describe dynamic systems.&lt;SEP&gt;A mathematical representation used in state-space analysis to describe complex systems.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-952ac88fc20d94741d460ff36bcefd2c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9c2225c69899b581fb582e25a195224</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Controllable Canonical Form">
  <data key="d0">Controllable Canonical Form</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Controllable Canonical Form is a specific representation of a state-space system that emphasizes control properties, making it easier to design state feedback controllers. This arrangement highlights the system's controllability, which is particularly useful in control systems design, especially in the pole-placement approach. By structuring the system to emphasize its controllability, the Controllable Canonical Form simplifies the process of control design, making the controllability properties of the system apparent.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-3180ab91b878b6037f04ac75d2ec907c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8185f3f5e2ea2ff5f1955f91117943ca&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7abd7647d540dcfe486743c3fa15b49c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6d8c0bd0542b3fd879eac68953207dee&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6cc628802fecc6640ad03f377ce29869&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-faf6d229bd33d9a677ddb2c4f3fe8552&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e37fd32e67cfb4b06fcec5633146e195&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0abcb038b73f1c1825b1810df74969ca</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Observable Canonical Form">
  <data key="d0">Observable Canonical Form</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The Observable Canonical Form is a specific representation of a state-space system that emphasizes the observability properties of the system. This form is particularly useful in facilitating the analysis and design of state observers by structuring the system to highlight its observability. The transformation of system equations into the Observable Canonical Form makes it easier to assess and ensure that the system is observable.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-3180ab91b878b6037f04ac75d2ec907c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8185f3f5e2ea2ff5f1955f91117943ca&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9e97cb703344a8d83dd7f1085591043d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7abd7647d540dcfe486743c3fa15b49c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6d8c0bd0542b3fd879eac68953207dee&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e37fd32e67cfb4b06fcec5633146e195&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f55b04c74c20ff1394ec57c53c60a408</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Diagonal Canonical Form">
  <data key="d0">Diagonal Canonical Form</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">A state-space representation where the system matrix is diagonal, simplifying system analysis.&lt;SEP&gt;Diagonal Canonical Form is a matrix representation where all non-diagonal elements are zero, simplifying analysis.&lt;SEP&gt;Diagonal Canonical Form is a representation of state-space systems where the denominator polynomial involves only distinct roots.&lt;SEP&gt;Diagonal Canonical Form, also known as Jordan Canonical Form, represents systems in a diagonalized state-space form.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-3180ab91b878b6037f04ac75d2ec907c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e37fd32e67cfb4b06fcec5633146e195&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-faf6d229bd33d9a677ddb2c4f3fe8552&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-50a65ab288a27fc3df2630882da9d54d</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Pole-Placement Approach">
  <data key="d0">Pole-Placement Approach</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The Pole-Placement Approach is a control system design method that allows for the arbitrary placement of closed-loop poles, provided the system is controllable. This method is used to place the poles of a system's transfer function at desired locations to achieve specific performance characteristics and ensure system stability. By determining the location of poles, the Pole-Placement Approach allows for the design of a feedback matrix that enhances system dynamics and performance. This technique is essential in achieving desired system stability and performance in control systems.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-3180ab91b878b6037f04ac75d2ec907c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2591937b2f7c7c5341658076669c1b02&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dfd846455e2fefcbadf8b8c8b3d71195&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9cff8b4398a7527756d649d91c3b5802&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ac56253b9f22aee18032b4cf99f1d27d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9b6f2069d9a69517d279f1786974d9e9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3ad73b7beb45180631dd72fbab2b6c9b</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Jordan Canonical Form">
  <data key="d0">Jordan Canonical Form</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The Jordan Canonical Form is a mathematical and matrix representation used in linear algebra and control theory to simplify complex systems and matrix operations. It is particularly useful when diagonalization is not possible, involving the use of Jordan blocks to handle repeated eigenvalues and linked eigenvalues in Jordan chains. This form is a modified state-space representation that comes into play when the denominator polynomial of a system involves multiple roots. Additionally, the Jordan Canonical Form simplifies the computation of the matrix exponential for a given matrix A, making it a valuable tool for analyzing linear transformations and system dynamics when a matrix cannot be diagonalized.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5e25465698e9728fefd91973fb2caccd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dfe09b7b703c389e8f2522a6967742f4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-04a1c0b94bb767b480f265211df2d3c7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-da77b0da419e3b09a32a7956bf055282&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c5e9c5e918d686131ecabed119aa3d7f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-50a65ab288a27fc3df2630882da9d54d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-36f2caf127ba71d0e038dc68e38f26ca&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a587ec6eec2d51c833758323b1607ad7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-660f747fbdc575c7df68ed347887ea9e</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Transfer-Function System">
  <data key="d0">Transfer-Function System</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">A Transfer-Function System is a representation of a system's input-output relationship using mathematical functions.&lt;SEP&gt;A mathematical representation of a system using a transfer function, specifically represented as Y(s)/U(s) = (s + 6)/(s^2 + 5s + 6).&lt;SEP&gt;A transfer-function system is a mathematical representation of the relationship between the input and output of a system.&lt;SEP&gt;Transfer-Function System describes the relationship between the input and output of a system in the frequency domain.&lt;SEP&gt;Transfer-Function System is a mathematical representation of a system's input-output relationship using transfer functions.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9e97cb703344a8d83dd7f1085591043d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6d8c0bd0542b3fd879eac68953207dee&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-04a1c0b94bb767b480f265211df2d3c7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e37fd32e67cfb4b06fcec5633146e195&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-50a65ab288a27fc3df2630882da9d54d</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Transformation x = Pz">
  <data key="d0">Transformation x = Pz</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">This transformation involves the use of matrix P to change the basis of matrix A to achieve diagonalization.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5e25465698e9728fefd91973fb2caccd</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Matrix P">
  <data key="d0">Matrix P</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Matrix P is a mathematical construct that plays a crucial role in various aspects of control systems. It is primarily used in solving the quadratic optimal control problem and is essential for system stability, requiring it to be positive definite. Matrix P is involved in the Riccati equation, where its positive-definite nature is a key indicator of system stability. It is also a real symmetric or Hermitian matrix used in calculating the performance index of control systems, involving parameters such as p11, p12, and p22.

The matrix is integral to analyzing system controllability and is composed of matrices A, B, and C. Its rank is critical for determining the relationship between vectors x(0) and y(t), and it must be equal to n for certain conditions to be satisfied. Additionally, Matrix P serves as a transformation matrix, with its columns being the eigenvectors of Matrix A, used to convert Matrix A into a diagonal matrix. It is also related to Matrix Q in evaluating the controllability of a system and is used in transformations related to state controllability. Overall, Matrix P is a versatile and indispensable component in the field of control systems, serving multiple functions to ensure optimal performance and stability.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-da505be49d4d9c37eb80545921311479&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d634460bf0500e11bc074875672732ca&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e4413c8298743c78468e47ffbe0e228a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5e25465698e9728fefd91973fb2caccd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7901b85c41f86e4c8c10a0b273e9c765&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6ece149736a8793b07c50dccb1028cee&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-292c92c504fb43e76a57c3f50454fcbe&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e37fd32e67cfb4b06fcec5633146e195&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c5e9c5e918d686131ecabed119aa3d7f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-27adbbe8c3daf01de8b63ffa252de1e6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-44fa931a2a4a572fbb711636672bfbd1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-74159e67faae8cc0d15754e4e7dbee64</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Diagonal Matrix">
  <data key="d0">Diagonal Matrix</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">A Diagonal Matrix is the result of transforming the Coefficient Matrix using the Transformation Matrix P, showcasing uncoupled scalar state equations.&lt;SEP&gt;A diagonal matrix is a matrix in which the entries outside the main diagonal are all zero, simplifying the computation of the matrix exponential.&lt;SEP&gt;A diagonal matrix is the result of transforming matrix A using matrix P, containing eigenvalues on its diagonal.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-37b28f4da7cad971f45737e82572ff87&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9c788b304947ce9ba784353e01e43778&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5e25465698e9728fefd91973fb2caccd</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
