<node id="State Controllability">
  <data key="d0">State Controllability</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">State Controllability is a fundamental property of control systems that defines the ability of a system to be driven from any initial state to any desired final state within a finite time period using appropriate control inputs. This condition ensures that the state of the system can be fully controlled and manipulated through suitable inputs, allowing for the placement of closed-loop poles at arbitrarily chosen locations. Essentially, State Controllability indicates whether all system states can be controlled, enabling the system to reach any desired condition in its state space.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-8185f3f5e2ea2ff5f1955f91117943ca&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e863c1a50016793179faa1e0b91cb9df&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e5be935af21c311ce3f7db40fa58876e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-817b95a5144d0dce10f2c3a02063acbe&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3402b465fcfb648982e49c37a455c189&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-292c92c504fb43e76a57c3f50454fcbe&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2ebe8a3128b2b3aabe653cb149142a6e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-63ee60925a95b0307b6e89fce826e877&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-761f357930e8cb4b1f367ec8167a5238&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e7d38d679f58a32db379c91feb378361&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1a7e6ee6eefc1562c87e6317d173028b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c5f8bc3750c711a5de6e8037c4fda4c0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c5e9c5e918d686131ecabed119aa3d7f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8258faffcaea77860b8003c251cb0674</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Stabilizability">
  <data key="d0">Stabilizability</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Stabilizability is a concept related to detectability, where a system can be stabilized by state feedback even if not all states are observable.&lt;SEP&gt;Stabilizability is the concept of ensuring a system remains stable, even if not all states are controllable.&lt;SEP&gt;Stabilizability is the property of a partially controllable system where unstable modes can be controlled and stable modes are uncontrollable, allowing the system to be stabilized through feedback.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e5be935af21c311ce3f7db40fa58876e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3402b465fcfb648982e49c37a455c189&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bb63a19b57948f30bda30ff5517c1eba</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Equation (9–51)">
  <data key="d0">Equation (9–51)</data>
  <data key="d1">event</data>
  <data key="d2">Equation (9–51) describes the mathematical model of a continuous-time system used to analyze controllability.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e5be935af21c311ce3f7db40fa58876e</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Controllability Matrix">
  <data key="d0">Controllability Matrix</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The Controllability Matrix is a mathematical construct used to assess the controllability of a system. It is derived from the system's matrices and is crucial in determining whether the system's state can be fully controlled by its inputs. The matrix is used to evaluate if a system is completely state controllable, with the rank of the matrix playing a key role in this determination. A rank less than the number of states, denoted by 'n', indicates limited controllability. Additionally, the inverse of the Controllability Matrix is utilized in various calculations related to system control.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9f818528633d493e4d171630e04e1d9b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0e2680709d2b529eb353b1601aca0714&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-292c92c504fb43e76a57c3f50454fcbe&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c5f8bc3750c711a5de6e8037c4fda4c0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b773a713efcb75c181833b78c35c4f74&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9838df333da6616701c1cce66c00e732</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Control Vector">
  <data key="d0">Control Vector</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">A control vector is an input vector used to manipulate the state or output of a system in control theory.&lt;SEP&gt;Control Vector refers to the allowable set of control actions that influence the state of a system in the optimal regulator problem.&lt;SEP&gt;The control vector is used in System S1 and System S2 to influence the system's behavior, reflecting input actions.&lt;SEP&gt;The control vector, denoted as u, is an r-dimensional vector that influences the state of the system.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-16d9cf4a88154292d8f7994b72a870f0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3402b465fcfb648982e49c37a455c189&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c5f8bc3750c711a5de6e8037c4fda4c0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8a707fa18fd529fe3c1718e4570e7abb</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Eigenvectors">
  <data key="d0">Eigenvectors</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Eigenvectors are vectors associated with eigenvalues, representing directions in which a matrix acts by stretching.&lt;SEP&gt;Eigenvectors are vectors that define the directions along which a linear transformation acts by stretching or compressing.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c5f8bc3750c711a5de6e8037c4fda4c0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c5e9c5e918d686131ecabed119aa3d7f</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Rank">
  <data key="d0">Rank</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">In the context of matrices, rank refers to the dimension of the vector space generated by its rows or columns.&lt;SEP&gt;Rank is a measure of the dimension of the vector space spanned by the columns of a matrix.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e37fd32e67cfb4b06fcec5633146e195&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c5f8bc3750c711a5de6e8037c4fda4c0</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Matrix Equation">
  <data key="d0">Matrix Equation</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Matrix Equation refers to a mathematical representation involving matrices, specifically focusing on eigenvalues, eigenvectors, and matrix transformations.&lt;SEP&gt;The Matrix Equation involves a 2x2 matrix operation resulting in a matrix with elements [6, -8; -9, 1].</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c5e9c5e918d686131ecabed119aa3d7f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-670bc9d19f233014556f6058e19da1a2</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Jordan Block">
  <data key="d0">Jordan Block</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">A Jordan block is a specialized matrix form used in linear algebra that helps in analyzing the structure of a linear system, particularly in the context of eigenvalues and eigenvectors.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-1a7e6ee6eefc1562c87e6317d173028b</data>
