<node id="Output Controllability">
  <data key="d0">Output Controllability</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Output controllability is the ability of a system to control its outputs to any desired state by manipulating the inputs.&lt;SEP&gt;Output controllability is the ability of a system to drive its outputs to desired values within a finite time period.&lt;SEP&gt;Output controllability is the ability to control the output of a system using its inputs.&lt;SEP&gt;Output controllability is the concept of controlling the output of a system rather than its state, ensuring the output can be driven to any desired final state.&lt;SEP&gt;Output controllability refers to the ability of a system to drive the output to a desired state.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-8185f3f5e2ea2ff5f1955f91117943ca&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-817b95a5144d0dce10f2c3a02063acbe&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3402b465fcfb648982e49c37a455c189&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e87750a7956fffd621ae63f70162b51a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-27adbbe8c3daf01de8b63ffa252de1e6</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Uncontrollable System">
  <data key="d0">Uncontrollable System</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">An uncontrollable system is one where certain subsystems are physically disconnected from the input, preventing full control over the system.&lt;SEP&gt;Uncontrollable System is a system in which not all states can be controlled or influenced by inputs.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-3402b465fcfb648982e49c37a455c189&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-76155cfacd298ba99866d06587bc0e7d</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Rank of Matrix">
  <data key="d0">Rank of Matrix</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The rank of a matrix is a measure of the dimension of the vector space generated by its rows or columns, important for determining controllability.&lt;SEP&gt;The rank of a matrix is a measure of the dimension of the vector space spanned by its rows or columns.&lt;SEP&gt;The rank of a matrix is the dimension of the vector space generated by its rows or columns.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-27adbbe8c3daf01de8b63ffa252de1e6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3799700b8e604e5c5384e0643a874e09&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3402b465fcfb648982e49c37a455c189</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Output Vector">
  <data key="d0">Output Vector</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">An output vector contains the output variables of a system, used to analyze observability and system performance.&lt;SEP&gt;An output vector is a vector that represents the outputs of a system, which can be used to infer the system's internal states.&lt;SEP&gt;An output vector represents the outputs of a system, which can be controlled or observed.&lt;SEP&gt;The output vector is a representation of the system's response, used in both System S1 and System S2.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b773a713efcb75c181833b78c35c4f74&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3402b465fcfb648982e49c37a455c189&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ba9e6dce047704f9bd794f5f4ed1e2a7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8a707fa18fd529fe3c1718e4570e7abb</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Unconstrained Control Vector">
  <data key="d0">Unconstrained Control Vector</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">An unconstrained control vector allows for manipulation of the output without restrictions, crucial for output controllability.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-3402b465fcfb648982e49c37a455c189</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="State Feedback Control">
  <data key="d0">State Feedback Control</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">State feedback control involves using the state of a system to determine the control input for achieving desired system behavior.&lt;SEP&gt;State feedback control involves using the system's state variables to compute control actions that achieve desired performance.&lt;SEP&gt;State feedback control is a control strategy where the feedback is derived from the state variables of the system to stabilize or control its behavior.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-cf54b3d3fa835e379c76e3ccf4ecfa3f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ba9e6dce047704f9bd794f5f4ed1e2a7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9838df333da6616701c1cce66c00e732</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Complete Observability">
  <data key="d0">Complete Observability</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Complete Observability refers to the ability to infer the entire state of a system from its outputs.&lt;SEP&gt;Complete observability refers to the ability to determine all state variables of a system from its outputs over a finite time period.&lt;SEP&gt;Complete observability refers to the ability to fully determine the initial states of a system from its outputs.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-faf6d229bd33d9a677ddb2c4f3fe8552&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5ae5ca3b7a89038d8b6dbc78ab1fcc29&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ba9e6dce047704f9bd794f5f4ed1e2a7</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Observability Matrix">
  <data key="d0">Observability Matrix</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The Observability Matrix is a mathematical construct used to determine the observability of a system in control theory.&lt;SEP&gt;The observability matrix N is used to determine the observability of the system and has a rank of 2.&lt;SEP&gt;The observability matrix helps assess whether a system is observable, based on the rank of the matrix.&lt;SEP&gt;The observability matrix is used to determine if a system is completely observable, requiring it to have a full rank of n.&lt;SEP&gt;The observability matrix is used to determine if a system's internal states can be observed from its outputs.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-8051d18cbff63321d1754c1c1f43cd85&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-22085333984fa377791f298c557d58ea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-817b95a5144d0dce10f2c3a02063acbe&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f55b04c74c20ff1394ec57c53c60a408&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b773a713efcb75c181833b78c35c4f74</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="System Controllability">
  <data key="d0">System Controllability</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">System controllability refers to the ability of a control system to move from any initial state to any desired final state in finite time.&lt;SEP&gt;System controllability refers to the ability of an external input to move a system from any initial state to any desired final state within a finite time period.&lt;SEP&gt;System controllability refers to the ability to control the state of a system using its inputs.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-27adbbe8c3daf01de8b63ffa252de1e6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-65dda872b092acba83b3de464ee4c24b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-817b95a5144d0dce10f2c3a02063acbe</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Rank Condition">
  <data key="d0">Rank Condition</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Rank condition is a mathematical requirement for matrices to have a certain rank, used to determine controllability and observability.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-817b95a5144d0dce10f2c3a02063acbe</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
