<node id="System S1">
  <data key="d0">System S1</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">System S1 is a theoretical system used to illustrate conditions for state controllability and observability in control theory.&lt;SEP&gt;System S1 is described by a set of equations involving state, control, and output vectors, emphasizing the concepts of controllability and observability.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-bb63a19b57948f30bda30ff5517c1eba&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8a707fa18fd529fe3c1718e4570e7abb</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="System S2">
  <data key="d0">System S2</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">System S2 is another theoretical system used to demonstrate the principles of state controllability and observability, with a dual relationship to System S1.&lt;SEP&gt;System S2 is the dual system of System S1, defined by equations involving conjugate transposes of matrices, focusing on the duality principle.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-bb63a19b57948f30bda30ff5517c1eba&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8a707fa18fd529fe3c1718e4570e7abb</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Conjugate Transpose of A">
  <data key="d0">Conjugate Transpose of A</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The conjugate transpose of Matrix A is utilized in System S2, illustrating the duality in system dynamics.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-8a707fa18fd529fe3c1718e4570e7abb</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Conjugate Transpose of B">
  <data key="d0">Conjugate Transpose of B</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The conjugate transpose of Matrix B is part of System S2, reflecting duality principles.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-8a707fa18fd529fe3c1718e4570e7abb</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Conjugate Transpose of C">
  <data key="d0">Conjugate Transpose of C</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The conjugate transpose of Matrix C is used in System S2, emphasizing the duality between controllability and observability.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-8a707fa18fd529fe3c1718e4570e7abb</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Detectability">
  <data key="d0">Detectability</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Detectability is a property of a system where unobservable modes are stable, ensuring that the system is at least partially observable.&lt;SEP&gt;Detectability refers to the ability to infer the presence of certain states in a system from its outputs.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-faf6d229bd33d9a677ddb2c4f3fe8552&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bb63a19b57948f30bda30ff5517c1eba</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Algorithm Flowchart">
  <data key="d0">Algorithm Flowchart</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The algorithm flowchart is a visual representation of a detailed algorithm, showing various components and processes that are interconnected to form a comprehensive system.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4fa35e34d0d4ee616cac17aa9a32795c</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Input/Output Blocks">
  <data key="d0">Input/Output Blocks</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Input/Output Blocks refer to the components of the algorithm that handle the inputs and outputs, depicted as 'u' and 'y' in the flowchart.&lt;SEP&gt;Input/Output blocks are components of the algorithm flowchart, labeled 'u' and 'y', representing the inputs and outputs of the algorithm.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4fa35e34d0d4ee616cac17aa9a32795c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6d8c0bd0542b3fd879eac68953207dee</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Addition/Subtraction Operations">
  <data key="d0">Addition/Subtraction Operations</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Addition/Subtraction Operations are arithmetic processes applied to input values to produce intermediate results in the algorithm.&lt;SEP&gt;Addition/Subtraction operations are processes performed on the input values within the algorithm, resulting in intermediate results.</data>
