<node id="Closed-loop control system">
  <data key="d0">Closed-loop control system</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The closed-loop control system is described by the equation $u\,=\,-\mathbf{K}\mathbf{x}$, illustrating control dynamics.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-292c92c504fb43e76a57c3f50454fcbe</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Matrix K">
  <data key="d0">Matrix K</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Matrix K is a critical component in control systems, specifically in the realm of signal processing and feedback control. It serves as a feedback gain matrix, which is calculated using the lqr (Linear Quadratic Regulator) function, and is derived from solving the Riccati equation. Matrix K is employed to minimize the performance index in quadratic optimal regulators, thereby optimizing control laws within state space design to modify the system's behavior. Additionally, it plays a vital role in placing the eigenvalues of the system matrix at desired locations, influencing both the magnitude of the system's output and its overall stability. Through its optimal configuration, Matrix K ensures efficient feedback control within the system.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-da505be49d4d9c37eb80545921311479&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d634460bf0500e11bc074875672732ca&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e4413c8298743c78468e47ffbe0e228a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7901b85c41f86e4c8c10a0b273e9c765&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-292c92c504fb43e76a57c3f50454fcbe&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-570cd2cd2a847eac2402e00924491700&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c6daa8560d2825569846748debe43a15&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-58531aa8deb3e186c6997e9b42da144f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ad1dc64fc9b2da439067495b6c1bf166</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Controllability Matrix M">
  <data key="d0">Controllability Matrix M</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The Controllability Matrix M is used to determine if the system is completely state controllable.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-8288442d2392d765b02cbc791d1b5378</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Transformation Matrix T">
  <data key="d0">Transformation Matrix T</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The transformation matrix T is used to convert the state vector into a form suitable for analysis in the controllable canonical form.&lt;SEP&gt;The transformation matrix T is used to convert the system state equation into a controllable canonical form.&lt;SEP&gt;The transformation matrix T is used to transform a system state equation into the controllable canonical form.&lt;SEP&gt;Transformation Matrix T is used to transform the state equation into a controllable canonical form.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-8288442d2392d765b02cbc791d1b5378&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0abcb038b73f1c1825b1810df74969ca&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f56557021ba0f275fa8f2409761b1c6a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ad1dc64fc9b2da439067495b6c1bf166</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Matrix M">
  <data key="d0">Matrix M</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Matrix M is defined as a combination of matrix B and its transformations, used for system control analysis.&lt;SEP&gt;Matrix M is involved in the transformation process, contributing to the equation verification.&lt;SEP&gt;Matrix M is part of the equation used to determine the transformation matrix T.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9be306b73353043a81456867d4cbe302&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f56557021ba0f275fa8f2409761b1c6a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5e0212ccf3d9b6dc7dff4b33d16a1ae9</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Matrix W">
  <data key="d0">Matrix W</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Matrix W is a matrix defined with coefficients of the characteristic polynomial.&lt;SEP&gt;Matrix W is part of the equation transformations and plays a role in the proof.&lt;SEP&gt;Matrix W is part of the equation used to determine the transformation matrix T.&lt;SEP&gt;Matrix W is used in the calculation of the observer gain matrix, representing a weighting matrix.&lt;SEP&gt;Matrix W is used in the transformation process of the equations.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-ef23f0827a35b7fb671dc50cf6d63ea0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f56557021ba0f275fa8f2409761b1c6a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9be306b73353043a81456867d4cbe302&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c5c7e0dc112b305ed237d6ecd3031c05&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5e0212ccf3d9b6dc7dff4b33d16a1ae9</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="State Feedback Gain Matrix K">
  <data key="d0">State Feedback Gain Matrix K</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The State Feedback Gain Matrix K is a mathematical matrix utilized in control systems to determine the state feedback control law. It plays a crucial role in adjusting the system dynamics by determining the control input necessary to place the poles of the system at desired locations. This process, known as pole placement, is essential for achieving specific system dynamics and ensuring the desired closed-loop behavior. The matrix K is instrumental in adjusting the output based on state feedback, thereby facilitating the desired control system performance.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-6cc628802fecc6640ad03f377ce29869&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cd465393991808ab3570984282218782&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f56557021ba0f275fa8f2409761b1c6a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1779b26d10c1b1abb7d91ca4e5524e77&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8c1c7e149cb37d4947fe26456e62a591&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7e83e0b0f093a4a5ee928c26cb73ad75&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-efd0888bc14d737e070a0d39a6f897cb</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Ackermanns Formula">
  <data key="d0">Ackermanns Formula</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Ackermann's Formula is a method used in control systems and control theory to determine the state feedback gain matrix, often denoted as matrix K, for state-space representation. It is particularly utilized for placing poles in a desired location, thereby calculating state feedback gains. This mathematical equation is referenced in Equation (10–97) and plays a crucial role in the design and analysis of control systems.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9f818528633d493e4d171630e04e1d9b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6cc628802fecc6640ad03f377ce29869&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a025c4295ffa25ee11426c0bff4fb282&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f56557021ba0f275fa8f2409761b1c6a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3372436c8d88f158b8e5ef00d0368411&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6aa727c1ad6dddc07c40ad930aae27a5</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Desired Eigenvalues">
  <data key="d0">Desired Eigenvalues</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Desired eigenvalues are specific values chosen for the observer matrix to achieve desired system dynamics.&lt;SEP&gt;Desired eigenvalues are used to write the characteristic polynomial for control systems.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-8051d18cbff63321d1754c1c1f43cd85&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f56557021ba0f275fa8f2409761b1c6a</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="System State Equation">
  <data key="d0">System State Equation</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The system state equation represents the dynamics of a control system.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f56557021ba0f275fa8f2409761b1c6a</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Pole-Placement Design">
  <data key="d0">Pole-Placement Design</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Pole-Placement Design is a control system design method that places the poles of the closed-loop transfer function in specific locations to achieve desired performance.&lt;SEP&gt;Pole-Placement Design is a method used in control systems to choose the locations of desired closed-loop poles based on experience.&lt;SEP&gt;Pole-placement design is a control system design method that involves placing the poles of the system to achieve desired performance.</data>
