  <data key="d0">Input Stage</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The input stage of the signal processing system includes initial signals and values that are processed through various operations.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-2bcf605f83825a20ced09f3cfbb124a8</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Output Stage">
  <data key="d0">Output Stage</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The output stage of the signal processing system represents the final processed signals after undergoing transformations.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-2bcf605f83825a20ced09f3cfbb124a8</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Integrator (I)">
  <data key="d0">Integrator (I)</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The integrator is a component that computes the integral of the input signal over time.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-2bcf605f83825a20ced09f3cfbb124a8</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Differentiator (D)">
  <data key="d0">Differentiator (D)</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The differentiator calculates the derivative of the input signal with respect to time.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-2bcf605f83825a20ced09f3cfbb124a8</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Multiplier (M)">
  <data key="d0">Multiplier (M)</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The multiplier is a block that multiplies the output of the integrator by a constant gain factor.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-2bcf605f83825a20ced09f3cfbb124a8</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="C Block">
  <data key="d0">C Block</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The C block represents additional processing or filtering applied to the signal.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-2bcf605f83825a20ced09f3cfbb124a8</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Observer Error Equation">
  <data key="d0">Observer Error Equation</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The observer error equation is a mathematical representation used to calculate the error in the observer model.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-2bcf605f83825a20ced09f3cfbb124a8</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Observer Gain Matrix Ke">
  <data key="d0">Observer Gain Matrix Ke</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The Observer Gain Matrix Ke is used in the design of a full-order observer to estimate the system's states.&lt;SEP&gt;The observer gain matrix Ke is used to adjust the observer model for desired stability and error reduction.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-2bcf605f83825a20ced09f3cfbb124a8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-efd0888bc14d737e070a0d39a6f897cb</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Pole-Placement Problem">
  <data key="d0">Pole-Placement Problem</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The Pole-Placement Problem involves determining a matrix configuration that results in desired eigenvalues for system stability.&lt;SEP&gt;The Pole-Placement Problem involves determining the feedback gain matrix to achieve desired eigenvalues in system control.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-12121eebc019284d55e0c84cd019271d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2ebe8a3128b2b3aabe653cb149142a6e</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Dual System">
  <data key="d0">Dual System</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The Dual System is a concept in control theory where the observer design problem is transformed into a state controllability problem.&lt;SEP&gt;The Dual System refers to a mathematical construct where the roles of inputs and outputs are reversed for analysis purposes.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-12121eebc019284d55e0c84cd019271d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2ebe8a3128b2b3aabe653cb149142a6e</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="State Observer Gain Matrix">
  <data key="d0">State Observer Gain Matrix</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">State Observer Gain Matrix is used to design state observers, determining the gain required for accurate state estimation.&lt;SEP&gt;State Observer Gain Matrix, denoted as Ke, is a matrix used in observer design to adjust the estimation of unmeasured states.&lt;SEP&gt;The State Observer Gain Matrix, denoted as Ke, is used in control systems to provide feedback and correct the plant model based on unknowns and disturbances.&lt;SEP&gt;The state observer gain matrix, denoted as Ke, is designed to ensure that the observer error remains small despite discrepancies between the observer and the physical plant.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0ae81e09d61ed423567970de31eac0a6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-040d64011f55ec07f471f024c2b1be29&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-76155cfacd298ba99866d06587bc0e7d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e863c1a50016793179faa1e0b91cb9df</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="State Observability">
  <data key="d0">State Observability</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">State observability is the condition under which the internal states of a system can be accurately inferred from its outputs.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e863c1a50016793179faa1e0b91cb9df</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Observer Error Dynamics">
  <data key="d0">Observer Error Dynamics</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Observer error dynamics describe the behavior of the estimation error in a state observer, ideally approaching zero.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e863c1a50016793179faa1e0b91cb9df</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Modeling Errors">
  <data key="d0">Modeling Errors</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Modeling errors are discrepancies between the mathematical model and the physical plant, affecting the accuracy of state observers.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e863c1a50016793179faa1e0b91cb9df</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Transformation Approach">
  <data key="d0">Transformation Approach</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The transformation approach is a method used to derive the state observer gain matrix by manipulating system matrices.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e863c1a50016793179faa1e0b91cb9df</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Direct-Substitution Approach">
  <data key="d0">Direct-Substitution Approach</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The direct-substitution approach is a simpler method for low-order systems to derive the state observer gain matrix by substituting into the characteristic polynomial.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e863c1a50016793179faa1e0b91cb9df</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
</node>
<node id="Feedback Signal">
  <data key="d0">Feedback Signal</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The Feedback Signal is used in control systems to adjust the output based on disturbances and measurement noises, ensuring accurate system performance.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0ae81e09d61ed423567970de31eac0a6</data>
  <data key="d4">Markdown_nofigure.md</data>
