<edge source="Matrix A" target="Matrix B">
  <data key="d5">143.0</data>
  <data key="d6">Matrices A and B play a crucial role in the dynamics and controllability of control systems. They are integral components in defining state controllable systems and are involved in the state-space representation used to model system and input dynamics. Specifically, Matrix A and Matrix B are used together in system dynamics equations to determine system behavior and influence the system's controllability. These matrices interact within various state-space representations, including those for transfer function computation and quadratic optimal regulators.

Matrix A and Matrix B are also involved in matrix operations, such as multiplication, to produce resulting matrices, including Matrix C. Furthermore, they are utilized in calculations to determine the feedback gain matrix K in control systems. In the context of MATLAB programming, Matrices A and B are employed to perform matrix operations and obtain state-space representations, further defining the system dynamics in the state equation. Overall, Matrix A and Matrix B are essential in describing the system's dynamics and control inputs, representing state and input matrices respectively, and contributing to the comprehensive understanding of control system behavior.</data>
