<edge source="Matrix A" target="Jordan Canonical Form">
  <data key="d5">46.0</data>
  <data key="d6">Matrix A can be transformed into Jordan Canonical Form under specific circumstances, particularly when it lacks distinct eigenvectors or cannot be diagonalized due to repeated eigenvalues. This transformation involves using a transformation matrix and is a valuable technique for analysis. The Jordan Canonical Form simplifies the computation of matrix exponentials and illustrates the relationships between multiple eigenvalues through the concept of a Jordan chain. This form is particularly useful for expressing Matrix A when diagonalization is not possible due to the presence of repeated eigenvalues.</data>
